      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <p>ron ALL PASTRY MeDOUCALL'S PATENT SELF RAISING FLOUR Is UNRIVALLED. The Examiner, Saturday, April 12, 1913.</p>
        <p>Old cal t: ar CHICALA A DRY FEED FOR CHICKENS</p>
        <p>sion to accept his name for the single appointment. The Rev.A M MITCHELL thought Mr Billing° was adopting the right line in advocating the appointment of a distinct officer for the administration ot out-relief. The amendment, was lost by 11 votes to 5. Mr FURNESS proposed an additional amendment that the appointment he made subject to Mr Aukland enter- ing into arrangements with the Board to offer further facilities for dealing with registration and relief cases. Mr JOLLF:Y pointed out that such r.n arrangement as Mr Furness suggested was understood to be included in the appointment. The amendment was withdrawn and. Mr Aukland's appointment was then put to the meeting, 13 voting for it and one against, while three refrained from voting. On the motion of Mr Mather, seconded by Mr Allen, it was resolved :—" That subject to the sanction of the Local Governmeat Board, Mr Frederick G;dman be appointed:—(a) as relieving officer of the No 3 relief district at a salary after the rate of £l5O per annum ; (b., an intent life protection officer for No 3 relief dis- trict at a salary after the rate of .£2 10s. per annum; (c) as vaccination officer of the Rixton vaccination district at the following remuneration act 20-1 (a) 5d per case; (b) is 3d per case; (c Is 31 per case. NURSES' EXAM INATION. Dr Bowden's report cn the recent final examination of Probationer nurses, in which all the candidates were successful, w,s submitted. The CHAIRMAN said it was very satisfactory to have such a favourable report on the names, Dr Bowden having commented on the soundness of the training given in the institution.</p>
        <p>AGENTS:</p>
        <p>CARTER &amp; SON, BRIDGE ST. WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>DR ITODGKINSON'S ILLNESS. Dr Johnson wrote stating that owing to the illness of Dr ,Hodgkinson and his inability to attend to his nrofessional work, arrangements had been mado for Dr Edward Fox to carry out the necessary duties at the Workhouse Inflrmary, and for Dr Segar to attend to the patients in the district work. Tie Board approved the arrangement. VOTES OF THANES.</p>
        <p>As this meeting was the final meeting of the Board as at present constituted, Mr Billings proposed a vote of thanks to Mr Bent for the distinction with which he had presided over the deliberations of the Board. Mr FUFtNESS seconded and said Mr Bent had dis- charged the duties with dignity and to the satisfaction of the members generally. Miss IinoADBENT thanked Mr Bent for the graceful and kindly way in which he had presided over the meetings. Mr -MATHER supported and the vote was heartily passed. Mr BENT, in returning thanks; said he was enteeing on his twentieth year as a member of the Board. lie had been a member of the Padgate Cottage Homes Committee ever since he became a Guardian (for part of the time as Chairman), and he had derived more satisfaction from that position than from any other he had been privileged to hold. He regretted that the Board was about to lose the services of several members. Mr A Brooks. whom they all admired, had been chairman of the Board and had also presided over that most important body. the Assess- ment Committee. As a member of the Building Com- mittee, he had also been of great assistance in getting plans through. Mr Bent also made reference to the I defeat of Mr Rushworth who, he said, had fought a Inoble fight and had always supported anything for the better administration of the Poor Law. Having alluded to the retirement of Mr W Gill and Alderman James Evans, the Chairman concluded by moving a vote of thanks to the deputy-chairman (Mr A J Jolley). Mr A BI:00Ks seconded and said that, without Mr Jolley's help, the Board would not have reached that standing among the Unions of the country which it bad reached to-day. Mr MA'THER supported and said Mr .Tolley had been more progressive than any ether member of the Board. In responding. Me JOLLEY proposed a vote of thanks to Mr Bottomley and the staff. He did so in no per- functory manner, as their woriehad been very efficiently and ably done. Miss BROADBENT seconded and said they were extremely lucky in having such a staff. The CHAIRMAN, in supporting. said they were indebted nat only to the clerical staff but to those officsrs who gave their attention to the poor in the institution and particularly in the sick-wards. Mr BOTTOMLEY responded. He said the officers had tried to give the Board their very best, and to know that their efforts were appreciated was splendid pay- ment for them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>PRACTICAL POULTRY</head>
        <p>PRACTICAL POULTRY</p>
        <p>FARMING.</p>
        <p>[BY REDCAP.]</p>
        <p>POULTRY DEMONSTRATION. MR. PAINTER'S SYSTEM OF REARING TABLE CiIICKENS.</p>
        <p>At the invitation of Mr Richard P Ward (Director of Education Cheshire County Council) I, along with other Press representativeq, paid a visit on April Ist to the experimental table chicken farm at Haslington Ball, Crewe. We were met at Crewe Station by Mr A Allsebrook (Small Holdings Commissioner for the district.) On our way to the demonstration, Mr Allsebrook informed us that the Board of Agriculture had for some time been watching Mr Payner's experi- ments, and on the results shown from his accounts last year. they concluded that his system was worth consideration. The Cheshire County Council were approached on the question of land, they veiy kindly offered the required acreage, and with a grant from the Development Fund the work was started, with Mr Paynter in charge. _., THE PRODUCTION OF FOWL FLESH.</p>
        <p>Mr ,Paynter said he looked on the production o table poultry at a profit to the producer as a question of national importance, which he had been studying and experimenting in for some nine years. As a result of his experiments he had come to the con- clusion that given a capital of A:400 to I'soo a man should be able to make an annual profit of F-200 by the raising of some 4,000 chicks a year. It is intensely interesting to hear Mr Paynter speak of his stock as representing a beast weighing so many pounds. And then to hear him calculating the cost in the prcduction of so much flesh. He goes on to show you the relative cost in the production of so many pounds cf beef and then strikes a balance to prove the value of the system in producing chicken .flesh. It seems strange to speak of a poultry farm where no adult fowls are kept, and where no eggs are laid. Such however is the case on Mr Paynter's farm at Baslington Hall. He does not go in for the production of eggs, neither does he fatten or cram the birds be rears. His speciality is that of rearing big well-fed chickens ready for market purposes at the age of 12 to 16 weeks old. He calculates his profits at not les, than one shilling per bird the season through. Therefore, you will see that to make a total profit of 5:200 it is necessary to rear and market 4,000 birds a year. Mr Paynter does not however operate in the production of chickens, the whole year through. He commences hatching in December and concludes in June. The last c,f the chickens are cleared off about the middle of October, so the whole of the land has six weeks rest before operations commence again. It is impossible for me to give my own observations in this week's issue. I want to give the full details of the system in this article. Therefore, I append the particulars and 'meanwhile ask you to read carefully and when you have read next week's artich-, I will endeavour to organise a trip to Haslington Hall for the benefit of any readers. I feel sure that with your hearty co-operation we could make such a visit highly inter- esting and, I feel certain, most profitable too. Don't forget to continue next week's article, I tiave got a hundred and one useful hin s to give you as a result of my visit to Mr Paynter's farm. The following extracts are taken from the leaflet explaining the system issued by the Cheshire County Council :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>SYSTEM,</head>
        <p>THE SYSTEM,</p>
        <p>The complete system, as practised by Mr Paynter, consists in hatching chickens at the rate of about 120 a week from December to June, and in rearing them until the age of twelve to sixteen weeks, when they are sod alive for table purposes. The time neces- sary for the work is from the first week in December, when the first incubator is started, until the niddle of October, when the last twelve to sixteen weeks-old</p>
        <p>, fowl is sold. Summarised, the method is as follows. About 2.5'0 eggs are bought every week from December 1 to June 7. These eggs are incubated, one incubator of at least 240 egg capacity being started each week during that period. Each hatch produces about 120 chickens. These chickens are reared in brooders on grass runs, and at the age of twelve to sixteen weeks they weigh about 31;- lb. each, and command a ready market at prices varying from 3s. 9d each in April to 2s Gd each in September. INCUBATION (3 WEEKS). Each incubator is started on a Sunday, the eggs being received and got ready on Saturday. This avoids extra Sunday work, as the chickens are taken out of the incubator on Monday mornhig. MrPaynter keeps three incubators going, and a fourth in reserve in case of accident, and so as to give each incubator every -fourth week off for cleaning and repairs. On the seventh day the eggs are tested and unfertiles are made use of in other ways. (A tester is usually supplied with each incubator.) The eggs are turned twice daily until the eighteenth day. The eggs batch twenty-one days from the date when first put in. Great care is taken as to the source from which eggs are procured, and if possible the stock birds are inspected, and it is ascertained that they are of a suitable age and properly fed, and,if possible,kept on a free range. The price Mr Payn ton pays per dozen for eggs is as follows :—Decemner, 3s; January to February, 28 Gd; March. 2s; April and May, Is Gd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>REARING (12-1 G WEEKS.)</head>
        <p>REARING (12-1 G WEEKS.)</p>
        <p>F;rst Weels.—The chicks are left in the incubator -until the twenty-second day, when they are transferred to brooders. For the first week these should be kept -close at home (if possible under cover), so that the chickens may be under constant observation and may be trained to run up to the cover of the brooder when they feel cold, and so avoid the risk of their " pecking" or clowding. Food.—For the (list week the food piven consists of the following mixture : —Wheat: (cracked), 50; millet. 15 ; caruiryseed, 15; best meat, 10 ; maize. (cracked), 5; ric. 5 per cent. A handful is given -when the last handful has been cleared up. Water ad. lib. is given twice a day. Grit is scattered on the floor plentifully when the chicks are first put in, and later is constantly kept before the chickens in suitable troughs. An abundant supply of grit at all times is cnnsidered important. SECOND TO FOURTH WEEK, When the chicks are a week old they are moved to a brooder in the rearing field, having a small wire run attached to the end of the brooder. This small run is removed at the end of one day and then arranged to surround the brooder. On the third day this run is taken away, thus giving the chickens access to di., large run, which is enclosed by wire netting 2ft. high, and is t.) yards by 40 yards in area. FOODS AND FEEDING. The food given during this period is as follows : First feed, day-break : Overnight three tins (with wire guards to prevent scratching) are put out, con- taining respectivily : (1) boiled rice, (2) beef scrap, 3) pinhead oatmeal cr groats. Second Feed, 0 a.m.: A soft food is given, consist- ing of biscuit meal and 10 per cent, of meat dried off with equal parts of sifted bailey meal and sharps. At the same time any oatmeal not cleared up in tin 3 is put away. (A flat box with a tin lid is kept in each inn for storing these tins.) Third Feed, 1 p.m : A feed similar to that given at -9 is given, and tin 2 containing any scrap meat not cleared up is put away in the box. Fourth Feed : Half an hour before sunset or 0 p.m (whichever is the earlier) as much dry chick feed is given in pans as the chicks will eat. Water ad. lib. Grit : A plentiful supply in suit- able troughs. The brooders have their floors well</p>
        <p>VALUABLE ADVICE TO LADIES. We advise all our readers who desire to remove their wrinkles and improve their complexions, to add to their shopping list tu-day, Creme To-Kalon, the new absorb- ant toilet cream, ot which everyone is now taiking.</p>
        <p>Farthings subscribed in aid of the building fund of St. :lichael's, Cricklewood, reached the total of 122,019.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Promotion for Relieving Officers.</p>
        <p>BOARD DECLINES TO JOIN " PHTHISICAL</p>
        <p>CARE ' COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Two promotions of relieving officers, consequent upon the appointment of Mr Bowes as registrar of births and deaths, were made at the meeting of the Warring- ton Board of Guardians on Wednesday. Mr E L Bent presided and there were &amp;so present :—Mrs Simpson, Miss Broadbent,Revs T R Matthews and A M Mitchell, Messrs A Jolley,' (deputy -chairman), .1 Billinge, A Brooks, G Mather, W C Brooks, W Furness, R Lee J A Hughes, G Allen, T Ritchie, W Flanagan, P' Longton, A Bottomley (clerk), It I Partridge (azsistant clerk), W J Brown (master of the workhouse) and C E Lawrance superintendent of Cottage Homes.) lIEFOSAL TO JOIN " PBTIIISICAL CARE" COMMITTEE. The House Committee considered a letter trout the Secretary of the Warrington Civic Guild of Helponvit- ing the Board to appoint a representative upon a " Phthisic,l Care Committee" proposed to be appointed by the ouild and to work in conjunction with it, the objects of such committee to be as follows :—To care for phthisical patients in the following ways and in any others that way be deemed suitable, viz :—(1) by providing suitable clothing and nourishment, if needed; (2) by seeking suitable employment and advising thereon ; (3) by securing industrial training ; (4) by co-operating with the insurance Committee, Health Committee, Education Committee, Guardians, Labour Exchange, employers of labour and philanthropic I institutions. The committee, aster careful considers-</p>
        <p>tion, came to the conclusion that no good purpose would be served by the Board appointing representatives</p>
        <p>upon such a committee, there being, in their opinioy, ample machinery already arranged for dealing with the matter.</p>
        <p>The minutes were approved. RELIEVING OFFICERS PROMOTED. Mr A J JOLLEY moved : That subject to the sanction of the Registrar General and the Local Goverment Board respectively, Mr Frank llerbert Aukland be appointed (a) as registrar of births and deaths for the Newton-in-Nlakerfleld sub district • (b) as relieving officer of No 2 relief district at a salary after the rate of £lOO per annum ; (c) as infant life protection officer for the No 2 district ate salary after the rate of £2 10a per annum. He said the resolution was in accordance with the poiicy outlined at the last meeting, which commended itself to the majority of the Board.</p>
        <p>Mr A BROOKS seconded. Mr FERNEss said at, the last meeting he pointed out that they were tentatively, if not actually, committing themselves to the appointment. He also drew attention to the very important feature that they were making a dual appointment, end he thought that before taking such EL step the Board ought to be in possession of full details as to the income derived from the positions. They could well afford to wait another fortnight, and in the meantime the Finance Committee could go into the matter and furnish information as to the value of the appointment as registrar. Individual members were often in the dark on such matters, and it would not only be in the best interests of the ratepayers, but within the rights of the Board, that the particulars should be given. The CLERa said the combined positions were worth about £2BO gross. lhe Rev A M MITCHELL snid it would hardly he worth Mr Auklaea's while to go to Ea.rlestown unless the two appointment were united. He should certainly support Mr Aukland's appointment under any circum- stances, but the point Mr Furness had raised was worthy of the most careful consideration. They ought to know exactly what the emoluments were. The tact that Haydock had made an application to be regarded as a separate registration area lent force to what Mr Furness had said. The Board ought to give as many facilities as possible to the different areas for the registration of births and deaths. His own people at Burtonwood sometimes had great difficulty in getting certificates, as they had to go to Fiddlers' Ferry for them. An area like Haydock, with a population of 10,000, was entitled to consideration. Mr BILLINGE said he was not so much concerned about the income derived from the appointments as about the practicability of successfully working them as joint appointments. He would be pleased to support Mr Aukland's promotion, if it were in the interests of the ratepayers, but having regard to the large extent of the area and to the conditions of the new relief order, which had not yet been put into practice in that district, he thought the best course would be to obtain the very best man they could for the single position of relieving officer and pay him the very best salary. He doubted whether the Local Government Board would sanction the joint appointment. Mr FonNese considered the Board was morally corn- mitted to the joint appointment, but, having 'aces- tamed the amount of the remuneration they should, before making the appointment, consider whether they could not offer more facilities for relief and registra- tion purposes. He suggested the appointment of a. small sub-committee to consider the advisability of making improved arrangements without entailing much extra labour on the official concerned. The Rev A M MITCHELL said they could not expect a relieving officer to fulfil those duties separately for 1:100 a year. Mr FLANAGAN said be did not think one man could do the work of both appointments if be had to pay outs relief from door to door, as it was intended to do. Only last week they had 145 relief cases before the Newton Committee and they weie spread over a large area. Mr A BROOKS said the work had been done until recently by a man who was far from well—in fact, tie was now practically pensioned off. it was a very good pension— he had got put into a nice soft job. Having been on the Kelief Committee for fifteen years, he knew that Mr Aukland could do probably three times as much 'as Mr BOWES had been able to do for some years peat. Ile was an alert, active, thoroughly painstaking official who knew the work—in fact he had done the lion's share of it for some time. He (the speaker) knew sufficient of Mr Aukland to be able to say that he would not undertake the work if he did not think he could do it thoroughly and efficiently. They sympathised with those districts which had not adequate facilities for registration,but unfortunately they could not help them very much. The most they could do was to promise that they would make provision, satisfactory to themselves, for the registration work to be carried on at Haydock and all over the district. As to the remuneration attaching to the appointments. Mr Aukland would, have to pay the rents of registration stations. He had certainly been with the Board long enough to deserve something in the nature of a plum, if the Board had one to give. The Rev 'l' R MATTHFAVS said he had heard -Mr Aukland say he could and would do the work of, both departments very well, if he had the opportunity. He doubted. very much, from the tone of his conversation, whether Mr Aukland would accept the divided office. The Rev A M MITCHELL: IL would not be worth his while. Mr MATHER : It is not worth the while of any officer we have now to accept either of the offices, if they are divided. Mr NV C Bnootes pointed out that all the relief cases had been " written up" by Mr Bowes, and everything was in apple-pie order. Ha believed Mr Aukland would be quite capable of filling the two positibns. The CHAIRMAN said a great principle was involved, and he was inclined to fall in with Mr Furness's suggestion that nothing would be lost by deferring the matter for consideration by the Finance Committee or the Relief Committee for the district. Mr A Bnomes said that, as the old Board went out of office that day, a delay of a month would ensue before the matter could be dealt with. The CLERK said the appointment of registrar was in the hinds of the Guardians for fourteen days only, unless he was instructed to ask the Registrar General for an extension of the period. With regard to the payments to the recipients of out-door relief at their own homes, Mr Aukland wished it to be known that he could not visit the whole of the cases weekly, but that a complete visitation would be made every month. Mr eurteiEss moved an amendment that the appoint- ment be deferred until the Finance Committee had bad an opportunity of conferring with Mr Aukland with a view to providing further facilities for registration and relief cases. He was not opposing Mr Aukland's appointment, but he did not want them to make it without going into details, and then find that the unsatisfactory conditions continued to exist. The Rev A M MITCHELL seconded the amendment, while heartily hoping that Mr Aukland would be appointed. If the work was to be done properly, they wanted two officers and each should be well paid-. Mr JOLLEY said the real issue raised by those who advocated delay was that the relief area was too large for one man to work. If that were so, what was wanted was not a dual but a treble appointment. They were suggesting that it was necessary to have two relieving officers and a registrar. (" No, no.") The resolution was part of the policy brought forward at the last meeting and approved by an overwhelming majority of the Board,in the interests of the ratepayere and of efficient administration. If they delayed it, they were simply throwing away a chance of securing efficient and economical administration. They had else an opportunity of recognizing long and faithful services and giving promotion to a very deserving officer. If they deferred the appointment for a month, their chance of giving the most remunerative side of the work—the registrarship—would have passed away and they would he left with the duty of appoint- ing two relieving officers—in other words adding very substantially to the expenditure of the Union. They could not hope to get the right type of relieving officer for £lOO a year. All the older Guardians knew what a splendid officer Mr Aukland had proved himself to be. He (Mr Jolley) strongly opposed his appoint- ment to his present position but he said frankly now that he was very glad he was defeated on that occasion, as they could not possibly have had a better officer. The amendment to defer the appointment was defeated by 11. votes to 5. Mr BILLINGE then brought forward a further amend- ment that a relieving officer for the No 2 district be appointed at a salary of £l6O. The CLERK said they had not Mr Aukland's perruis-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>MANURING OF SWEDES AND TURNIPS.</head>
        <p>MANURING OF SWEDES AND TURNIPS.</p>
        <p>Basic Slag at Drilling.</p>
        <p>The most important fertilising ingredient for swedes and turnips is phosphoric acid. A phosphatic manure drilled with the seed or broadcasted earlier hastens the seedlings into the rough leaf,thereby giving them a better chance of escaping the ravages of the turnip" fly." Also a liberal phosphatic dressing helps them to pass safely the critical stages of their further growth, and to "finish off " well. Therefore, as farmyard manure is low in phosphoric acid, it is advisable not to apply it alone, even if in large quantities, but to use moderate dress- ings and to supplement same with a phosphatic fertiliser. This will give a quicker growth, a larger return, aud sounder crop than large dressings of dung alone. The old theory that, as swedes and turnips cannot easily assimilate phosphoric acid, the latter must be applied in water soluble form, has been completely upset by the results of innumerable experiments and extensive farm practice during recent years; and hiLhly citric soluble basic slag is coming more and more into favour, applied at, or shortly before time of drilling. If farmyard manure (10-12 tons per acre) is applied for turnips and swedes. a dressing is about Gowt., slag is usually sufficient to secure more than an average crop of good quality on land not subject to finger-and-toe, but where this disease is known to prevail, or is suspected, then 2 or 3 cwt. more per acre can be used with advantage On poor land, or with only a light dunging, better results still may be expected from a complete dressing consisting of S cwt. slag. cwt. muriate or sulphate of potash, and 1 cwt, nitrate of soda (or an equivalent amount of nitrogen in other nitrogenous fertiliser). Where no farmyard manure is available for where the farmyard manure availa: le is tainted with the refuse from a preceding crop affected with finger-and toe, and ought therefore to be used for crops other than swedes and turnips, quite satisfactory crops may be grown with artificials only ; but in this case it is as a rule necessary to apply not only phosphoric acid, but potassic and nitrogenous manures also. A dressing par acre consisting of 7 to Scat. slag, 'cwt.. muriate or sulphate of potash, and lovt. sulphate of ammonia, or 1 !cwt. nitrate of soda or nitrolire, will yield a remunera- tive crop on average soils. I.</p>
      </div>
