      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>ADULTS.</head>
        <p>ADULTS.</p>
        <p>TERRITORIALS AND SPORT.</p>
        <p>Major-General Bethune, Director-General of the Territorial Force, speaking in Gravesend on Saturday, at a Territorial prize distribution, said he loved the voluntary system through and through. He thought they could do a little hit more in the way of influencing other people to take on the obligations of service. Toe question of numbers had to be wrestled with, and that was being faced at the War Office. The difficulty was that they had so much advice, all in different directions. It therefore, took a great deal of trouble to get the right thing, but they were determined to get it. What form it would take he could not say now. The volun- tary system should be got to work better by tapping a strata of Society they had not yat touched. Too much superfluous energy was got rid of in sport. It was looked upon as a pursuit. He had got hold of football players and cricketers, but golfers were his desperate enemies. (Laughter.) He did not think they wanted to see conscription yet. They were, he thought, going to pull through and bring public opinion round.</p>
