      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <p>Borough Police Court. SATURDAV.—Before Mr R Fairclough (in the chair), Alderman Tinnion, Messrs A J Jolley and T Pemberton WORKS THEM—Albert Cadman, puddler, 14. Liver- pool road, was charged with stealing during the past three months, 41bs of scrap brass value Is 6d, the pro- _ .</p>
        <p>perty of Messrs Monks Hall and Co. and Frederick Mullinder, Fennel-itreet was charged with receiving the same. —The Chief Constable said that Cadman was stopped by officers in Liverpool-road and at first denied having stolen the brass. On the way to the police station, he admitted having taken small quantities from time to time. He said he had sold the tunas for ninepence. If any brass was found in the works, it was the duty of the employees to take it to the lodge. The prisoner Mullinder said he had " planted " the brass in Stewart's yard. Officers visited the place and behind some rubbing stone they found a small sack, containing old lead and rubber. Prisoner was told the brass was not there and said it must have got lost whilst being transferred on the cart. The property had not been found. Cadman had no previous record but Mullender made his fourth appearance. The police had done as much as they could for him and had given him chances to make an honest living.—Cachnan was fined 10s. including costs and Mullinder was sent to prison for three mouths. CHARGING FOR STAMPS.—Percival Powell, a well- dressed young man, of Samuel-street, was locked up on warrant for being .t 1 18s in arrears of maintenance- towards his wife.—The Chief Constable said the prisoner's parents had paid the money since the warrant was issued. Tne order was for is ed per week, and when the wife asked for the warrant she had not received the money. When she went home however, there was a 7s 4:1 postal order—the defendant having deducted twopence for postage and the order. On the outside of the envelope he merged " Alitnony."—The Clerk : He has no right to deduct the twopence.—The Chief Constable : As far as lam concerned I feel more for the father who has paid a good portion of tee money. I have no feeling for the prisoner.—The Clark : He was told when he was here before about w citing " Alimony " on the envelope. It was a pity they could not arrange for the money to be paid through a third p trty.—Prisoner said he earned 25s per week, and paid 16s uoard and lodgings, Is on an administration order, end is 6d to his wife.—The Chairman : That does not account for your writing " Alimony " on the envelope.—The Clerk : You could pay the money to Mr Firth.--Prisoner : The last time we were before the court Mr Firth said he had done all he could to get the parties together. He has not interviewed me once. My wile has sent me unsatisfactory post cards (these were handed in).—The Chief : His father is an official under a public body in town. Postcards have been sent which are altogether unsatisfactory.—The Chairman : Who started it ?—The Chief : Neither of them should do it.—Prisoner: When I was at the County Court her mother and herself jeered at me along the street. I have also had several anonymous letters which must have come from her.—Po eell promised to pay the money regularly. A STRANGER IN A STRANGE LAND.—Henry Pugh, 29, Hamilton-street was charged with being elunk and disorderly in Winwick-street.—The Clerk are you guilty or not guilty ?—Prisoner (in a voice similar to that affected by George Formby) : Very sorry, sir, but I ' can't hear what's going on. I admit I had a drink of beer. I did not know what I was doing more than fly. —The Chief: The officer will tell you. (Laughter) Inspector Foster said that when he apprehended prisoner tie said he was a ratepayer and that they could not do anything with him.—Prisoner, who wept in the dock, continued in his plaintive tone" I have never been in a place like this before. lam very sorry it has happened. .1 wish you would forgive me this once and I will never do it again."—The Chief : As a matter of fact, he has been jaw place like this.—The-Clerk : Not a place like this—the old Police Station.—the Chief : It was a court of summary jurisdiction. (Laughter.)— The Clerk : But it was not a place like this. (Laughter.)—Prisoner was fined 5s including costs. MoNDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair) Alder- men bmethurst,Bolton and Tinnion.Messrs W Roberts S Wright, G Pilling, T Locker and W Furness. MILITARY ABSENTEE —Richard J Buike, a sergeant in the 'I bird King's Liverpool regiment was fineu 40s. for being an absentee from the annual training in 1912. TUESDAY.—Before Mr C Jackson .Holmes (in the chair), Aldermen Bolton, Pierpoint and Tinnion, and Mr T Locker. FAULTS ON BOTH SIDES.—John Richard Lyons, labourer, Lythgoes-lank , was summoned for having neglected to provide his wife and two children with reasonable maintenance.—Mr Breewne said the parties were married eight years ago, and three children were living. The defendant would not work long enough to enable him to earn sufficient money to maintain his wife and family. lie had out., gone to work at the intervention of the Roman Catholic priests ; they had, in fact, practically compelled him to work. He lay in bed two or three days at a time, although there was apparently nothing the matter with hini. He had assaulted his wife over and over again, and the wife and children would have starved but for assistance of relatives and neighbours.—Delendant denied that he had ever stopped in bed except when he was ill ; he had certainly never been thsre in conse- quence of laziness. His wife drank excessively and had pawned "everything conceivable in the house, even to the children's bedclothes, nightdresses, and boots." —lnspector Wainwright (N.S.P.O C.) was called .by the defendant, and admitted that there were faults on both sides.- The Bench granted a separation order, ordering the defendant to contribute Ss a week towards his wife s support, and to pay the costs. The wife will have the custody of the children.—ln answer to the defendant, the Chairman said Lyons could makestn application later on to have the order varied. WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr J W Hayward (in the chair) and Mr C Jackson Holmes. DESTROYED OR EXPORTED ?—Thomas Brown, Lairs- street, was fined 23 6d and coals for having worked a horse in an unfit state on the 29th March.—lnspector Dee, R.S.P C.A., stated that the animal was lame in both lore legs, suffering from " one."— Defendant : he horse has been destroyed.—lnspector Dee : I think it has been sent aoread.—Defendent : I have a receipt to the effect that the horse has been destroyed.—The Chief Constable : It will be sent to Belgium I think.—lnspector Dee : The defendant has a very god character.</p>
        <p>TII URSDAY.—Before Mr W Roberts (in the chair) Messrs J Upson, John Fairclougl-, A J Jolley and Dr Naden. A SUSPICIOUS Cif ARACTER.—Ernest Bailey was charged on remand with loi,ering in Wilson Patten- street on April 2nd with intent to commit a felony.— Detective Sergeant Reddington having proved the case —Bailey was sent to prison for 21 days. " LORD COLERIDGE 'I OLD ME."—lsaac Patterson, cutler, no fixed address, was charged with acting as a pedlar in New-road without having a certificate.—The officer stated that when r.sked for a certificate prisoner said " Lord Coleridge tells me I don't require one."— The Clerk (in an undertone): Has he got him here as a witness ?—A fine of 53 and costs was imposed or in default seven days imprisonment. CROSSING GATES USEFUL —Oliver William Porritt Tor Side lielmshore, near Manchester, was summoned for not having a rear light on his motor car properly lighted on March 21st at 4.40 a.m., also with neglecting to produce his license to the constable.—Mr W Woodcock, Haslingden, appeared for the defendant.— P.O Dixon deposed to seeing the car driven by defen- dant proceeding along Bridge-street. The rear lieht was not visible, lie wnistled, but the defendant went on over the bridge, but was held up by the Wilderspool Crossings. Witness caught him up and defendant said he did not know the rear light was out. Witness looked at the electric lamp and found that there was a very small light, only a mere shatlow. Defendant said the battery must have run down, as he had been travelling for most of the night. He also rubbed some dust off the lamp and then there was only the faintest light. Asked for his license the defendant produced one which expired last year. He said he did not know it had expired and that he did not trouble to look. He also added "If it had not been for the gates holding me up, you would never have seen me."—Mr Woodcock said the defendant had travelled all night from Windermere and his battery had run down. Dust obscured the small light there was. He admitted a technical offence, but asked the bench to deal lightly with the case. In Lancashire they received DO advice when the licence had run out.— Mri Upson (a magistrate) : It expired a long time ago ? —Yes.—The Chief Constable : May I ask for the licence to he produced this morning ?—Mr Woodcock : A new one has been taken out, but! have not got either with me this morning. It was simply forgetfulness on the part of defendant.—The Chief Constable pointed out that there was one endorsement on the licence.—Mr Upson: You would never have bad a case if the crossings gates had teen cpen ?—That is so, sir.—</p>
        <p>the - ou krrs - :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
