      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>Defendant was oreerad to pay costs on the first case and in the second waz fined 20s and costs. The licence would be endolsed.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair) Aldermen Bolton, Smethurst, Pierpoint and Tinnion, Messrs A - Jolley, G Pilling, W Furness and S Wright. A DISORDERLY PRISONER.—James Carr, wiredrawer, " 40, Tanners-lane, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Enutsford-road.—P C. Norridge said he - was escorting a horse and lurry along the road when be" saw a man go up to a carter,talk to him and deliberataly stop the horses. He, witness, went across to the mei,' - and the prisoner came up and commenced using obscene language. He became disorderly and said he woula knock his, the oflicer's, brains out. P C Rowles came to witness' assistance and prisoner kicked his hat, breaking it. Prisoner : It is the first time 1 have been druns. The Chief Con- stable : 1 think his behaviour is attributable to drink. Mr Furness (a magistrate) : What is his occupation —A wiredrawer. Mr Furness : Not a carter. A fine of 5s and costs,including damage to policeman's helmet was imposed. CHASED WiTh A KNlFE.—Robert Chesworth, Owen-street, was summoned by Annie Allock, 277-a Winwick-road, for using threats.—lt was alleged that defendant had run after the woman down Winwtos-road , with an open pen knife and had threatened to do her bodily harm.—Defendant said the bother was caused by ' the other side.—He was ordered to pay the costs of the case amounting to 12s Gd. „ POLICE. STATION NOT HOME —James Wood, Mae., Evelyn-street, appeared in answer to a summons isattecl- by his wife Rebecca Wood, , 50, Plumpton-street.—The Clerk : What have you summoned your husband for , again? You have had a separation oroer already.—The-- Chief : Perhaps she wants a couple in order to make a. pair of them and have them framed.—Complainant said she did not know. Her husband had said he would not' give her anything at all and the previous order was for lOs per week and was made in August last. 'They had since been together.—The Clerk: The old order' •.s still in force.—Defendant and his wife were arguing across the Court when the Chief' constable exclaimed : You are in the police station not.' at home.—Defendant : The law has been broken.-- Complainant : He owes me £l.—Defendant : I amtoo, old for a jab being close on GO years of age. 1 arn, thinking of going into the Workhouse and making 0 mi: sons keep me.--The Chief Constable : You will have,. to break stones there and that will be harder still.-- The 3s 6d for the summons was refunded to 0% applicant and Mr Firth toot, the case in band. -•1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>ECZEMA'S TORTURES WERE ALMOST UNBEARABLE.</head>
