      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>TO-DAYS' SEMI-FINAL.</head>
        <p>TO-DAYS' SEMI-FINAL.</p>
        <p>1/ARRINGTON'S CHANCES AT OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>arrington's chances are still not being considered 'seriously outside Warrington. The prevailing opinion 1,4,8e11is to be that while they may possibly oust -7 A'sbury they have no chance of succeeding in the nal. One must admit that the odds are certainly tgaltist the Wirenullers, and I have seen as much as to 1 laid this week against Warrington capturing Ins tropby. But, like most kinds of sport, football is 1;111. of surprises—wherein lies a good deal of the fascination. As a matter of actual fact, the odds have Pa against them on each of the finals in which they 'ld succeed. The only time they were favourites was Ilgainst Halifax at Salford in 1964, and then they lost! Intich for current form and prognostication.</p>
        <p>.1,11:V71!at are Warrington's chanors of beating Dews- ,: lu the semi-final to-day? In my opinion they good as they were at Salford a fortnight ago. ;asp mean feat to beat the Reds on their own be—su, and it should be a no greater task to beat 13:2_1?n1Y on neutral territory. Dewsbury are the .tie 'vs/ holders of the cup, and are an admittedly ver side. But Fortune hatufavoured them so far in them choice of ground in the first three rounds. iuu Fortune has more to do with the result than vc4132., For many years Fortune ,has never smiled on —llegton th at uldham, but it is quite on the cards at tin she mar look with a favourable eye on the War- gt°ll 'axis this afternoon. Are Dewsbury to have -" the luck to the vely end? It is not likely.</p>
        <p>k 4%. There is one thing in Warrington's favour. They e had a fortnight's rest, and every man is I.l2°lted to be in the pink of condition. Dewsbury have two stiff engagements in their fight for the fourth Lac e- They wei e well beaten, 18-3, at Hull on tiatur- but on Tuesday they ran up 30 points against 11._egston Rovers on the Dewsbury ground. Warrington Eit.:l every man available and in a fast game their Power and condition should stand them in</p>
        <p>--- ,wiTtL4 he., team was chosen on Tuesday and is identical 1( that which won at Salford. There was a strong ,f ng about putting Naylor in the forwards in place earnley, and thus giving a bit more weight to the but the younger man got the vote. 1 fully etlninate that Warrington will be beaten for Passession against the big Yorkshire pack, but their in the loose has often counterbalanced this I 'can. It will largely rest with the Warrington tornm-rankers whether the Dewsbury backs are allowed W„get: going. On ,the attack they are superior to tr,7rrington, but they have yet to learn what the n'irePullers' defence is like. The teams have not met in the League for six or ,ren yearß, and during the interval the Dewsbury ta-len have won a reputation almost the equal of their Jear neighbours, Batley. The team to-day contains ekett, the ex-International R.U. full back,a custodian IC is Bore but slow, two good wings in Sharpies and an °cles, a Powerful pack,and two smart halves in Price Milner. The former is the Millom youth whom hi;arrington thought too slight at one time, and Milner I?ix, a reputation for offside tactics to which Oldham e strong testimony. I hope be will be made to a 7 the game in to-day's encounter: -- fl 5 ~following is the draw and officials for the semi- Trinity v Huddersfield at Halifax: i-peree, Mr F Renton (Hunslet) ; touch judges,Messrs wga re.enwood (Halifax) and E F Kilvington (Leeds) bick .rrington v Dewsbury at Oldham: Referee, Mr G (w•lasoa (Halifax) ; touch judges, Messrs R Wailing l'nes) and G F Hutchins (Oldham).</p>
        <p>Vir • -epkarrington have been quietly training at home all Prfd ' atd the practices on Tuesday, Thursday and tiohaY, reveal them at their best physically. Last `•second t theY were the guests of Mr Turpin at the hous. e of the Hippodrome.</p>
        <p>-- Th fro " Specials will run right through to Oldham Arioll the C,entralStation, and one horn Bank Quay or 1.33". ha C.L.C. trains depart at 1.10, 1.25 and train at 1.30. There should 1 e at 0.6 t a couple of thouand enthusiasts to cheer the nfirepullers on.</p>
        <p>-- 14. • Purposes of comparison I once more give the aght-s and weights of the competing teams: WARRINGTON. Height. Weight ft. in. st. lbs. Jolley lirookS Renwick _ ".I.7Noter Rradshaw 5 8i 11 3 Nicholas - In 10 Llaintith Thomas, G 'Cheaters Pearniev Thomas lt j 5 10 12 7 cox Skelhorne 5 9 12 4</p>
        <p>Those who look upon the cup as a certainty for Huddersfield would get something of a shock on Wednesday if they happened to be among the 20,000 people assembled at Central Park. Wigan avenged themselves to the tune of 31 points to 10, and Hudders- field were made to look like second-raters. Of course, with Wagstaffe and Davies absent, the combination suffered, but this was not sufficient excuse for the drubbing they got from Wigan. The Colliers were brilliant in the extreme and Huddersfield were far from being the team of champions one expected to see. They hfid an off-day—as they had at Hull and York— and if they get another on the 26th inst., the Cup may find an unexpected home.</p>
        <p>The League table to Monday is as follows — : For. ARst. Per- _</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. p. G. T. P. c'ntee Hud'field 3l 28 3 0...103172 722...3G 38 186... 90.38 Wigan 32 27 5 0...105150 660...41 47 223... 8437 Hull K.R 31 23 7 1... 80103 469...42 53 243... 7539 Hunslet 33 99 9 2... 71107 453...48 50 246... 69 62 Dewsbury 33 22 10 1... 66124 504...43 48 230._ 6810 Broughton 27 16 7 4... 46 59 269...18 31 132... 66.66 Wakefield 132 19 11 2... 49 81 341-52 80 344... 6250 S4lford 29 18 11 0... 35 53 229...24 43 177... 6206. Oldham 30 16 12 2... 56 72 328...49 53 257... 55'66 Batley 3l 17 13 1... 56 87 373...36 47 213... 56,15 Leeds '33 18 14 1... 61 91 395...43 65 281... 5606 R'hdale H. 31 15 14 2... 39 71 291...56 49 219... 51.61 Widnes 31 14 15 2... 43 76 314...54 67 309... 48.38 Hull 3l 13 15 3... 32 47 205...45 75 315... 46.77 St Helens al 14 16 1... 52 85 359...C1 69 309... 46'77 Swinton 29 13 15 1... 36 48 216...30 59 237... 46'55 Bradford 33 14 17 2... 43 53 245...49 77 329... 45.45 Warrington 29 12 15 2... 34 40 188...34 54 230... 41.82 Barrow 28 9 17 2... 28 57 227...49 72 314... 2571 Runcorn 29 10 19 0... 33 44 198...48 83 345... 34.48 Halifax 32 10 20 2...r34.67 269...56 85 367... 34'37 York .29 9 19 0... 40 55 245...58114 458... 32.14 Leigh ..... 29 821 0... 18 39 153...44 75 313... 2758 Keighley 26 419 3... 23 29 133...58 84 368... 21.15 Bramley _3l 4 26 1... 29 38 172...82174 686... 1451 Coventry 26 025 1... 20 35 157...119190792... P 92</p>
        <p>Warrington " A" team will resume operations to-day in a Combination match at Pemberton. The selected team is: Brown ; Bardsley, Waywell, Hudson, and Davies ; A Brown and Jordan; Gibbs, Harmer, Blundell, Arnold, Clark, and Wright.</p>
        <p>The fare for the habituJs of Wilderspool will be a match in the second round of .the Lancashire Shield Competition between Widnes "A " and Rochdale The match will commence at 3.30. and like the Cup-ties, "all-pay "is the rule.</p>
