      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>IMPS GARDENING.</head>
        <p>THE IMPS GARDENING.</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK: THE IMPS ON A FARM.</p>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>The following are the results of matches played during the past season which ended last Saturday in an easy victory of 6-1 against Swinton Park :</p>
        <p>Goals W D For Against</p>
        <p>Ist Eleven ... 26 ... 14 ... 10 ... 2 ... 89 .... 64</p>
        <p>2nd Eleven::: 22 13;::</p>
        <p>1... 72... 78</p>
        <p>The First Eleven did badly until the New Year. only four games being won, but the introduction of Jhi, A E Bradshaw, and W H Minards made a wonderful difference, as ten out of the last 12 matches were won, including, notable victories away from home against South Manchester, Winnington Park and Mid Cheshire, all by a 3 goals margin. J E Pearson and W H Minards have been the most successful goal-getteis ; Pearson has bad a great neason and his scored 41 goals. On six occasions he has scored 3 goals in one match, and has played a dashing and clever game all along. Minards in the responsible position of centre forward has kept his wings going nicely and found the net himsel 38 times. Of the defence W T Fletcher and A Knight have been consistent and their play has reached a high standard. C Billington has come on well at left back and so has A E Bradshaw in the gruelling position of centre half. J II Wilkinson after thirteen seasons, is playing a strong game at right half and his experience is a valuable asset. J Bruhl voluntaiilv took the goal. keeping, and since he came on the First has hardly made a mistake and the Latchford goal has never been more safely guarded than it has been since Christmas. Some of Bruhl's saves in the crack game this season have been really wonderful. The Second Eleven have a fair record considering that midway in the season they had to give up three of their most successful players to the First Eleven and that in the latter half of the season they were heavily handicapped by illness of players and ii,j tides sustained However, it is anticipated that there will be no need for any great changes next season and with the material at the club's disposal a much stronger combination will be put out. A very strong list of fixtures has been arranged for next season, including the First Elevens of Sale' Bowdon, 'I imperley, Alderlsy Edge, Kersal, South Manchester, Mid Cheshire, Chester, Manchester University, Stockport, Winnington Park, ac., and the club should forge ahead next September.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>GOLF.</head>
        <p>GOLF.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CLUB.</p>
        <p>The tie for the captain's cup was played on Saturday between Mr kt H Airey and Mr G W Brooke' and resulted in the latter winning with a gross score of 103, handicap 20, net 83. FIRST QUALIPYING ROUND.—CAPTAIN'S PRIZE (LADIES).</p>
        <p>Miss Lofthouse Miss Sloane Miss Bainbridge Miss K Picton lO3 30 73 Mrs Bedell Miss D Picton Mrs E A White lll 31 80 Miss Dolan</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S ANGLING ASSOCIATION. (N.A. CnTIZE)• The close season for locals has been a dead letter up to the present. There was a fair muster at the Gowy on Sunday lest but results v;rere practically nil. W.Bailey had a run with live bait, but beyond this the pike were entirely off feed. We hear that one or two members who were taking a look around Acton Bridge length saw a roach rise. If this is correct and fish are again coming down this canal there will be fine times for the workingmen anglers of Warrington. Several names, we see, have been booked for the rambling match and members could assist secretaries by having their names put down as soon as possiole. We can now definitely say there will be a gold medal given by one of our kind patrons to compete for besides the other specials. This match should prove one of the biggest events of its kind from Warrington and the novice has every opportunity of capturing a first prize. Live bait is barred in this contest. A.S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>WORLD'S GREATEST BUILDINGS.</head>
        <p>THE WORLD'S GREATEST BUILDINGS.</p>
        <p>(COPYRIGIIT.I</p>
        <p>In every quarter of the globe wonderful structures have been the common and most prominent objects by which nations have em- bodied their ideas of distinction and homage. It would seem, too, that the utmost variety of motive has inspired their creation. Affection, reverence, patriotism, and worship have all played a part in the world's famous buildings. Thebes has its counterpart in St. Sophia Medina in Seville. As apparent, likewise, is the fact that the earliest historic peoples, no less than the moderns, have raised piles of the most enduring kind, illustrative, as Ruskin observes, of man's profoundest regard. for things around him and the great unseen.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>SOME ANCIENT TEMPLES.</head>
        <p>SOME ANCIENT TEMPLES.</p>
        <p>Dating from the very early annals of time, the world-power of Egypt immortalised, itself by those massive temples at Karnac, Luxor, and Thebes, fit complements of the Pyramids, erected 3,000 years, or more, -before the dawn of the Christian era. Almost of contemporary date are Those superb buildings, chiefly palaces, of Oriental splendour, of Assyria, which Layard, some sixty years ago, disclosed to the gaze of modern times. Persia, by her picturesque remains of noble palaces at Persepolis and Susa, existing anterior to the- birth of Christ; Greece, by the Acropolis and Parthenon; Asia Minor, by the temple of Diana at Ephesus. celebrated for its, beauty, enormous size, and minute details of architecture; and, of a later 'period, Rome, by its mighty Colosseum, severally demonstrate the greatness of human idea and achievement in striking form. Two of the most extraordinary. remains in ancient lands are probably those of Palmyra and Baalbec. The magnificence of the former 'on the northern edge of the Arabian Desert in Northern Syria. comprising the far-famed temple of the Sun (Baal), may be judged from the fact that there still exist ruins of the colonnade, nearly a mile long, and consisting originally of some 1,500 imposing Corinthian columns. Baalbec is repres,ented by the gigantic columns, more or less standing, of her Great Temple, on a yet more splendid scale. Unique in appearance, the 54 still extant are 88 feet high, 22 feet in. circumference, and, measuring individually, 60 feet by 13 feet; while the various halls leading to the principal ceat of the sun-worship are on the same grand proportions.. Some impression of the vastness of the Temple of the Sun at Baalbec may be formed from a notable painting by David Roberts which adorns the walls of the Walker Art Gallery, Liverpool. CHVISTIAN BYZINTIMI,</p>
        <p>With the beginning of the Christian ei fresh styles tif architecture, principally that of the Byzantine type, appear in the early and middle ages. One of the finest buildings of this design is the .Mosque of St. Sophia, the most renowned of the 300 Mosques in Conetantiirple, founded by Constantine and rebuilt by JuS- tinian in 547 A.D. No erection. in Eastern Europe surpasses this Magnificent monument d Byzantine glory. Another of Europe's most retharkable churches has its home in MOECOW., kTiOWII aSt. Saviour, erected to commemorate the departure of Napoleon's army. from MO6- eow. Covering an area of 73,000 square feet, the colossal structure Of St. Saviour rivals all the Celebrated cathedrals of urope, particularly in its interior deeerations. Walls, corridors, altar, cupola, and temple proper are adorned on the mi lavish scale. This princely sanctuary, Said to be capable of accommodating 10,000 worshippers, Was built at a cost of two and a nuarter millions of pounds sterling.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>SPAIN'S MOORISH 3PASTERPIECE.</head>
        <p>SPAIN'S MOORISH 3PASTERPIECE.</p>
        <p>All the world has heard of the 'Alhambra of Granada in • Spain, one of the most ancient relics of the,. Moors in their last Spanish - stronghold. The area enclosed by this fascinating pile of build- ings and its gardens, is some 35 acres in extent. Directly you have passed its threshold you pause in wonder—struck by atnazement—you are rapt out of yourself; you seem in fairyland! Suddenly yQII are transported from the prose of to-day to the romance of the 'Arabian. Nights'!" Marbles, arabesque work, and colourings of brilliant hues, on floor, wall, and ceiling, dazzle the eye- Endless in number and character are its halls nnd courts, embracing ,the "Hall of the Ambassadors," "The Court of the Lions." "The Common Bath." "Tower if the Two Sisters." "Audience Hall." DerhaPs</p>
        <p>W.lKNlCrialr. Cycle and Motor Depot,</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>SPECIAL SHOW OF RALEIGH CYCLES Cash or Easy Payment Terms.</p>
        <p>Douglas and Fludge !rotor Cycles. Enquiries solicited for any make CYCLE, MOTOR CYCLE, CYCLE CAR or CAR.</p>
        <p>Repairs, Accessories.</p>
        <p>tne looEt gorgeous of the Alhambra's, grAndeurs, and the "Hall of the Shields," etc. No beholder- of these i surprised that the defeated Moorish. King wept, when, having reached a hill from vhich he could take the last look he turned and f- aNV the walls and beautiful cupola of his Joved. AlhamEra—glowing in the glory of tin) southern sun,</p>
      </div>
