      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>FIRST DIVISION. RESULTS TO SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Goals.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. For Ag'st Pta Lostock Gralam Barnum Villa Oughtrington 19... 10... 6... 3... 43... 28... 23 Latchford Lymm 19... 9... 5... 5... 33... 33... 23 Alobberley 20... 8... 9... 3... 30— 34... 19 Oughtrington Amat 18... 6... 8... 4... 37... 44... 16 Bowdon Vale Broadheath Altrincham Lads 18... 2... 10... 6... 23... 42... 10 St Vincent's 19... 4... 13... 2... 21... 41... 10 MOBBERLEY V LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>At Mobberley, in fine, but windy, weather. With both teams practicilly out of all honours there was very little Interest in the game. Several fine individuql efforts were made by the visiting centre (Shakeshaft), and from one of these came the only goal of the game. Final; Latchford, one goal Mobberley, nil.</p>
        <p>_ . LOSTOCK GRA-LAM v LYMM. At Lostock. This gime took place after Lostock had heen to Barnton. Weedall, who was playing a fine game at centre forward for Lostock, opened the score with a splendid goal. This proved to be the only scote of an interesting first half In the second portion Lostock Gralam gradually took the upper hand, and it was only occasionally that Lymm broke away, and when they did their efforts were easily repulsed by the home defence. Lostock scored their second goal through Postles, who easily converted a penalty given against Wilson for hands; whilst before the close Costello added a further point, heading through from a corner. Final : Lostock Gralam, 3 ; Lymm, O. OUGTITRINGTON PARK v. BROADBEATII. At Heatley. 'ihe Heathens were the first to score, Clegg after some nice combination, opening their account with the wind in their "favour. The visitors had the best of the exchanges, and further increased their lead through Anderson. At half-time Broadheath led by two goals to nil. Upon the restart the Park took up the pressure and earned a victory with the score : Oughtrington Park, 4 ; Broadheath, 3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SECOND DIVISION. RESULTS TO SATURDAY.</head>
        <p>SECOND DIVISION. RESULTS TO SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Goals. P. W. L. D. for agst Pt Weaver Refining Go 11... 8... 0... 3... 29... 8... 19 Stockton Heath Latchford Rees Padgate Amateurs Cadishead Wesleyans 9... 4... 3... 2... 17... 15... 10 Latchford Reserve 10... 2... 4... 3... 10... 13... 7 Warrington CL B LATCHFORD V LATCHFORD RECREATION.</p>
        <p>This local tie was spoiled by the high wind. Both sides found it difficult to control the light ball. The Rees had the better of the opening half, and led at the interval : 1-0. In the second half the home team had slightly the pull, but could not pierce the Rees' defences a close game ending in a narrow victory for the Rec, with the score: Latchford Rees, 1; Latchford Reserve, 0. ALTRINCHAM JUNIOR COMPETITION. REPLAYED SEMI-FINAL. STOCK-7'ON HEATH V WEAVER REFINERS.</p>
        <p>The third meeting of these ri-.als in the competition, took place at Lymm. V‘eavcir played uphill and at the interval there was no score. When the game had been in progress 78 minutes, Hough, receiving a ball from a throw-in, centred finely. for Newton to head a lovely goal, completely out of Vernon's reach, Weaver Reliners although they had had the worst of the game, opening the score. Aroused by this reverse Stockton Heath fought desperately, but met a sound defence. With only two minutes to go Antrobus put across a capital centre, and Holmes running in from the left equalized the score with a fast low drive. Extra time was necessary and two fifteens were agreed upon. In the last few moments a long dropping shot was put in from the Stockton Heath lett, and Isherwood catching Collier in possession of the ball bundled both keeper and ball into the net. The game ended sensationally in a victory for Stockton Heath with the score : Stockton Heath, two goals; Weaver Refiners, one. HALL WALKER MEDAL COMPETITION. SECoXD ROUND. GREAT SANKEY v GOSSAGE'S, These teams met on the Widnes County Ground last Saturday. A splendid following of spectators journeyed with the Sankey team. Result: Great Sankey, three; Gossage's, two. TO-DAY'S TEAM. SEMI-FINAL TIE.</p>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY V BALFOUR-ROAD OLD BOYS.-013 Widnes County ground. To-day. Great Sankey team—J Bond, goal ; E Glover, B J Kelly, full backs ; J E Glover, R Stephens, El Payne, half backs ; E Darlington,T Whitfield, TCook (captain), A Grainger, W Booth, forwards. lick-off 3.30</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>CATHEDRALS OF EUROPE.</head>
        <p>THE CATHEDRALS OF EUROPE.</p>
        <p>Among the world's monumental elevations the cathedrals of Europe hold high place ink point of • extent , and majesty of appearance. Special, attention may be drawn to the glorious shrines of Abbeville and Rheims, Amiens, and Rouen, the rock-hewn piers of Chartres, • the Church of St. °nen, one of the most 4plendid specimens of ecclesiastical architecture in. Normandy; the massive towers of Notre Dame, Paris; the soaring spire and huge dimensions of Antwerp Cathedral; together with the Gothic charms of Strasburg and the mighty fabric of Cologne. Yet vaster in proportion is_ St. Peter's, in Rome, and more exquisitely beautiful those of the Duomo, Florence; Milan Cathedral,. and St Mark's, in Venice, on the seaboard of the Adriatic; poems in marble, in- comparable in the world-story of art. Russian architecture is notably to the fore by the cathedral of St. Isaac, and the magnificent mass of the Winter Palace in St. Petersburg, and also the marvellous Kremlin of Moscow, the. most sacred spot. in All the Rilssian Empire. Lacking much of the lovely ornamentation and artistic characteristics of Continental churches, those of Britain pre-eminently repre- sented by Durham and Peterborough, Lincoln and Winchester. .Sllisbury, York. and Canter- bury; culminating in St. Paul's and West- minster Abbey, possess features of world-wide fame.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>NATIONAL BUILDINGS.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL BUILDINGS.</p>
        <p>Of modern specimens of architecture reflect— ing national and civic life must be mentioned the Capitol at Washington,. U.S.A., and the alais Justice in Brussels. The United States Capitol at Washington, built after the great national temple of .ancient Rome, is a re- markable State erection in which the 'National CorgresS meets and the Supreme Court holds ite sittings. Conspicuously placed on an eminence, commanding a noble view, and costing over three millions sterling, the Washington Capitol is unique on the American Continent. Not un- like the Capitol in general features is the Palais de Justice' of Brussels, which cost twor millions sterling. one of the largest structures of itg clas3 in Europe. Seen from the plains, of Water some twelve miles distant, in a clear atmosphere, its white marble dome on the hon. zon 11-1-5 an almost unearthly beauty. - THE GLORIES OF THE ORIENT.</p>
        <p>Far East no city in the Orient compares with that of Agra in the possession of numerous monuments of the house of Timur, several in a sCale of unparalleled splendour. Those of the Pearl Mosque and Great Mosque are memorable. in song and story for charms in almost incon- ceivable form. Still more distinguished' is the priceless Taj Mahal. without the city, consist- ing- of an extraordinary and wonderful mansleum.-erceted. by the Emperor, Shah Jehan, for himself and his wife, who died in the 17th century. Upon the tomb 20,000 men were em- ployed incessantly for a span- of 22 years. The complexity and grace of the general design, the elaborate finish of the workmanship, the domes and gracefu/ 'minarets, the rich mosaic setting, and the entire structure of glistening marble, make the Taj Mahal easily one of the wonders of the world.</p>
        <p>If. further, we think of elegance.. gracmi uniquene n arcurtecturati coubtructlon, rather than size and bulk, among great build- ings, we should be disposed to give the palm to the Giralda of Seville and the Campanile of Giotto in Florence, the latter superbly rising above Arno's towers of watch and war, a dream of loveliness, incomparable and divine,</p>
        <p>Fi OR</p>
      </div>
