        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, APRIL 12, 1913.</p>
        <p>The question of housing RURAL accomodation in the rural dis- HOUSING. tricts is one which has lately occupied the attention of the powers that be-to dgreater extent than ever before. It is a question which has become clamant for action. We have been hearing and reading of all too-numerous cases, not only of rural cottages being unfit for human habitation, but of a positive lack of accom- modation of any kind. Rural depopulation may be traced more directly to causes of this kind than to many other of the rather fanciful reasons so often advanced when the subject is being discussed. Here, at least, is one most obvious and most elementary point upon which to begin attacking the problem,and it is interesting to observe that a .voluminous re- port has been presented by the Departmental Committee appointed by the Board of Agri- culture to inquire as to the nature and char- acter of the buildings which should be pro-. vided for small holdings in England and Wales. Voluminous reports will not, of course, bring a revival of the life of the countryside, but the report now before us performs the valuable service of pointing the way by which local authorities, landowners, and others concerned may most advantage- ously set about doing something tangible and effective in the provision of houses and other buildings in connection with the Small Hold- ings Acts. Among the many important poirts in the report we observe the recommend on that, in order to reduce cost, houses and buildings should be erected, as far as pos- sible in pairs or groups within a limited area. Regarding the vexed question of how to erect labourers' cottages which, shall repay the owner, the report remarks that the difficulty of securing an adequate return for rural cottages, in the Committee's opinion, should not be regarded as a permanent condition. It has, they say, arisen largely from the cus- tom of charging nominal rents, which have come to be regarded as the proper rents for cottages. There can be no doubt, add the Committee, that the purposes of economy would be served to some extent if local authorities could look ahead and buy land for housing, not in very small parcels, situ- ated in the centre of the village, where the value is comparatively high, but in larger areas on the outskirts, where it should be obtainable at or near its agricultural value. •</p>
        <p>Strikes are undoubtedly un- THI, CARTERS' pleasant excrecencies on our CLAIM. industrial. system, but there</p>
        <p>are occasions when they be- come both unavoidable and necessary. The uprising of the Warrington carters is- a case in point. When men are only earning 225. per week they are living so near the border- line of poverty that if they be men at all they must, in justice to themselves and their families, strike and strike hard to obtain the wherewithal for that modicum of respecta- bility and comfort which is every honest workers' due. The Warrington men have discovered none too soon, that union is strength, and we wish them well in their struggle with masters who do not appear to realise that the conditions of labour and em- ployment have changed materially in the last 20 years. They are but emulating the ostrich, and burying their heads in the sand, when they stubbornly persist in refusing to " recognise " the men's unions. Wages will inevitably re-adjust themselves—and we give the masters credit for willingness to accord some advance—but they might as well realise now, and for all time, that they will have to accept the principle of recognition. The sooner they gracefully concede this just de- mand, and the sooner will they end the trouble and remove the dislocation of indus- try which is already being felt pretty severely in many directions.</p>
        <p>Ever since the Tariff A SCIENTIFIC Reform campaign was TARIFF, launched, the Free Traders of this country • have been invited to bow the knee to the principle of a " scientific tariff " as it is ex- emplified in Protectionist countries. Now the Washington correspondent of the Times comes forward to explain how a "scientific tariff" is to be evolved and maintained. The only " science " which is concerned in the process is the science of wire-pulling to ob- tain protection for the pet commodities of</p>
