        <p>The imports in March, as shown in the Board of Trade returns, were valued at £61,347,145, or 4'180,531 more than in the corresponding month of last year and £2',808,763 more than in 1911. The exports totalled £41,689,947, or 4'975,976 more than in 1912 and £826,035 more than in 1911. Raw ,cotton imports fell off to the extent of £3,904.278, and the exports of cotton fabrics were £1,052,599 less. The figures for the quarter show an increase of £8,465,365 In imports and £8,687,277 in exports. Last year the coalminers were on strike the whole of Alarch, but on the other hand there was no Easter holiday in the month, as there has been this year.</p>
        <p>* * . -Warrington's town planning scheme advanced another stage on Thursday when the Street Improvement Committee conferred With the owners of part of the area on the extreme eastern boundary of the borough. A Proposals to construct a new road, 80 feet Wide, from Knutsford-road to a point on the river about fifty yards above Weaver Lock —where a new bridge will probably be erected in due course—was agreed to by all Present, subject to further consideration of the cost of the scheme. Other suggested new roads were also approved but exception was taken to two, and further consideration icvls Promised by the committee. Several and-owners readily acquiesced in the pro- Posal to provide open spaces in the propor- tion of one acre in ten. The result of the e2nference was regarded as highly satisfac- tory and promises well for the smooth Working of the Act in Warrington.</p>
        <p>Here are one or two striking figures iard a re, to the Insurance Act as given by Mr. tlasterman, Secretary of the Treasury, at Tzle dinner of the Faculty of Insurance in nd on : We have over ,:.':300,000 a week being paid into the National In-urance Fund apart from the Government contribution. To-dav we have banked to the credit of the fund and earning interest for it ..4.:13,250,000. We estimate that 270,000 persons a week will L e legitimately receiving sickness benefit at a cost of over :!:,100,000 a week to the fund, and that over half a million people will be receiving medical attendance without themselves paying a farthing fur it except for their insuiance con- tributions many of whom never received adequate medical attendance of any sort before. in a normal week we estimate that IS,OOO per- sons will be receiving maternity benefit, at a cost to approved societies of nearly £30,000 a Week.</p>
        <p>* * • There appears to be considerable misunder- standing as to the principle governing the of prominent townspeople for presenta 'lnn to their Majesties the King and Queen 911 the occasion of their visit to Warrington in July. Lord Derby, however, laid down certain definite lines which must be followed In compiling the list, and we find that, acting 01l his intimation, the following ladies and gentlemen will be entitled to the honour : ,-,, 1-!le Mayor and Mayoress, the Town Clerk, '6l! Gilbert Greenall, Bart., C.V.0., and Lady reenall, Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., Mr. and m p. 1.8. J. C. Parr, Mr. and Mrs. Robert councillor Mr. and Yrs. John Fairclough, 'euncillor and Mrs. H. Roberts, Alderman and Mrs. W. Bolton, Alderman and Mrs. J. `3rnethurst and Alderman Forshaw. In lition the Mayor has decided to submit the names of Mr. and Mrs. A. H. Crosfield.</p>
