        <p>Exhibition of Etchings. An exhibition of modern etchings will open at the Museum to-day (Saturday) and will continue until the 17th inst. Some of the best known English artists ire represented. Pictured come.</p>
        <p>During the whole of next week, the management will he showing " Quo Vadis ' ? This picture is a perfectly magnificent achievement. The subject of Sienkiewisz's familiar novel lends itself especially to the art of the "living" photographic. Scarcely a scene or incident is omitted, and the film is produced with a wealth of detail and a superb degree of mount- inc. The picture takes two hours to show and there will 13,3 three performances daily at 3.0 6,30 and 8 45. The is seats may be booked in advance without extra charge. ARRoL-30IINSTON, BAYARD, PALLADIUNI and TALE° CARS.—bole Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51, 53, rind 55 Bridge at., Warrington. See the 8 HT. Bayard, £197. Lead Poisoning. Mr F A Jones (deputy coroner) condacted an inquest at Warrington on Tuesday on the body of Marty Hogan (54), painter, kdging at 327, Manchester- road, who died in the Union Hospatal on Monday. The evidence was to the effect that 10 years ago the deceased suffered from painter's cholic. Six years ago his wife died, and since that time he had been addicted to drink. He had recently said to his son " Keep your heart up John, I won't be long. It is misery." He bad been in the hospital since Christmas, suffering from chronic lead poisoning.—The jury returned a verdict to the effect that death was due to lead Tolson- jog, hut there was nothing to show how deceased had contracted it. DAISY VACUUM CLEANERS,42/-,63/-,81/.—Try one on hire.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge St. Warrington Royal Theatre of Varieties. "The " Royal " repertory Co, have achieved another success this week in their presentation of the thrilling French drama" The Sbadow of the Guillotine" in a highly creditable manner. On Monday Miss Phyllis Morton who was playivg the part of Adele, was seized with a bad attack of influenza, and has had to rest for the remainder of the week. Her part was taken up at a minutes notice by Miss Vera Olga who has been well received. The ( ther characters have all been well sustained. Miss Morton hopes to be able to play as Ethel Harding in "Send him victorious" whfcb will be staged next week. It deals with the adven- tures of a 3 oung officer in the kingdom of Ravonia. Caledonian Association.</p>
        <p>The members and friends of the above association brought their session to a close by a very successful whist drive, social and dance last Thursday evening. Whist was indulged in from 8 to 10.30. Mr J Robertson presented the prizes to the winners : Ladies : 1, Miss Ashdown ; 2, Miss Daintith. Gentle- men 1, Mr Boyle ; 2, Mr Black. Refreshments were served at the interval. The second part took the form of a sccial. Miss Val Lockerbie's selections on the violin were much appreciated; also the pianoforte solo by Mr Ellison. Mr J McArthur was in splendid voice in the rendering of "Annie Laurie" and also in " Afton writer." " It's jist like being at home " was sung by Mr J Robertson. The accompanists were Messrs B Smith and P II Brown. The duties of M.O. wf2re ably discharged by Mr Murray. Dancing was continued until two, and a very enjoyable programme was brought to a close by the singing of "Auld Lang Sync."</p>
        <p>- - Sportsman's Sudden Death. After witnessing the Grand National on Friday last, Mr George Hodgman (59), Stevenedge, Herts, returned to the Lion Hotel, Warrington, where he was staying for the races. About o'clock on Saturday morning Mr Hodgman felt unwell and left the hotel with a view to returning home. As he was entering a cab he had a seizure, and immediately expired. Drs Smith and Fox had been sent for. Whilst in town deceased had been attended by Dr Binns. Mr Hodg- man, who was one of the best-known supporters of the Turf, had for the 69th time witnessed the Grand National. He won the Grand National in 1557, when Charles Boyce steered his colours to victory with Emigrant. He also won the Chester Cup. The deceased gentleman was a great friend of Admiral Roue, the famous handicapper. His typical appear. ance on the racecourse was with a twig of sweet briar in his mouth, as he had long given up smoking a pipe. In recent ye-trs he called most of his horses by names beginning with the letter " V," and asked whether he selected horses with " " because he was a great admirer of Sam Weller, replied, " Satn Weller be bothered. It is so that I can find them readily in the Calendar."</p>
        <p>"John Maginn Branch United Irish League A meeting of the above branch was held in the Club Rooms, Winwick-street!, on Monday. Mr D Currie, (president) presiding. Correspondence was read from Mr Crilly, general secretary, Mr Llughrey, organiser, and Mr F Maginn. The chairman urged odall present the necessity of working hard till the end of the financial year in getting members to join the branch,or at least to take out membership cards, so that when the fight was over they could say they had done some little thing in the final struggle for Irish liberty. On the motion of Mr Kelly seconded by Mr Ryan it was agreed to send 5..15 to the executive for membership cards. The secretary gave a synopsis of the past year, which included the taking of additional rooms, purchase of new piano, re-arranging and re-covering billiard tables, introduction of new lighting plant, &amp;c. Continuing he said all bills were paid up to date and they still had a substantial balance to their credit. (Applause). Messrs Handley, Cain, Mahoney, and others having paid glowing tributes to the secretaries and other officials, the repoit was unanimously adopted. The usual votes of thanks brought a thcroughly successful meeting to a close.</p>
        <p>PREMIER CYCLES, Excelsior Cycles, Lea Francis Cycles, £4 10/-to £l6 IG/-.—Sole Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge Pt., Warrington.—Advt. Warrington Philomathic Society. At a meeting of the above society held on Wednes- day evening last, a considerable audience of members assembled to listen to a team debate on the subject, " Is emigration from the British Isles beneficial to the Nation ? ' The affirmative was taken by the Vice- president's team, six in number, who argued that emigration was at present the safety-valve ( f the British Isles. inasmuch as it provided an outlet for those unfortunate or ambitious people who, at home, lacked the opportunity they desired, and that it relieved these islands in a perfectly natural way of a load of discontent. Attention was. .also drawn to the large amount of British capital invested abroad, and to the consequent desira- bility of Britishers peopling our colonies and also emigrating to reign countries. The question of international goodwill was touched upon, and emigration suggested as a means of fostering it. For the negative, Mr Harold Dutton's eloquent team, of equal number, contended, amongst other things, that emigration drained the nation of much of its best blood; that it tended to turn people's thoughts abroad insteao of towards redressing existing evils at home; and that it was blighting our agricultural interests by attracting men from the land, and also hampering British commerce by creating a shortage of skilled labour. It was also contended that emigra- tion tended to direct abroad British capital, which, if invested at home, might bring about a " terrible " increase of employment in these isles. The President, Mr James Ball, occupied the chair. The voting was equally divided..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>"The Round-up."</p>
        <p>As the title suggests, this thrilling melodrama is a romance of cow-boy life on the Western plains, and as sucb,is a production much above the ordinary. There is plenty of sho‘oting, villainy of the darkest hue and an acceptable smattering of low comedy—essentials which appeal to lovers of a thorough going sparkling drama. A love story is woven round the sheriff of Dead Man's Gulch and the daughter of a wealthy mine owner. A split is threatened when the sheriff dis- oovers that the mine-owner is the one who killed his father in order to obtain possession of a rich mine, but there is a happy conclusion to the drama when it is found that the mine-owner is not the father of the beautiful Lilian but merely her guardian. Needless to say, this thorligh-paced villain receives his just reward. Mr J P Mcßlide is soon a favourite with the audience in the part of Arizona Dan, the sheriff. In a clever yet natural manner he gives a realistic portrayal of the typical cow-puncher, investing the part with plenty of dash and vigour. Mr Charles Levey soon earns the execrations of the " gods " as Colonel Redmond, the bullying villainous mine-owner. Powerfully built and with a cunning styla, Mr Leyvey could not be better placed, Miss Rita Tomkina capti- vates the audience with her breezy style in the role of Lilian Redmond. Miss Edith Giddons and Frank Harrison as Mrs and Mr Hay are responsible for the element of comedy introduced and are loudly applauded for their efforts. All the other characters are capably sustained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 12, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 12, 1913.</p>
        <p>CARTERS' STRIKE.</p>
        <p>WAGES AND RECOGNITION,</p>
        <p>MASTERS' CONCESSION.</p>
        <p>The latest phase of the strike of carters in Warrington, which has been in progress since Monday, is an indication on the part of the masters of a more friendly attitude on the question of " recognition," which i one of the chief points for which the men are strenu- ously contending. At a meeting of the Team- owners' Association, held at the Patten Arms Hotel, Yesterday morning, it was resolved:— " That the Society represented by Mr. Plinston, which has. t7eble the membership of any other Carters' Society, should be</p>
        <p>the Society acknowledged b:v. the Team-</p>
        <p>owners. • The Teamowners have alv,,ays negoti- ated with Mr. Plinston, who is a local man, and they naturally wish to continue and if possible to try and settle the present trouble through him." This decision, however. will not form the basis of a settlement as the Union which the masters are now prepared to recognise—the Tramway and Vehicle Workers' Union—is not the organization at whose instance the strike has been declared. The majority of the carters concerned in the dispute are members of the United Carters and Motormen's Asso- ciation, and they are determined to persist in their demand for the recognition of that body. Mr. H. Ball, district delegate of the United Carters' Association, who is the men's leader in the strike, was interviewed by an Examiner representative last evening and was emphatic in his declaration that no settlement would come about, without full recognition of the United Carters' Association.</p>
        <p>He pointed out that, of the 300 carters on strike, only 86 are members of the Tramway and Vehicle Workers' Union, through which the masters are now prepared to negotiate. It is admitted that the United Carters' Asso- ciation is a new-comer into this town, but it has quickly swept the greater proportion of eligible carters into its fold. Moreover, it was the United Carters' Association which sent out the application for increased wages and better conditions which led to the strike. The local branch of the Tramway and Vehicle Workers' Union has, Mr. Ball stated, agreed to stand by the other Union in its claim for recognition. Mr. Ball regarded the masters' latest move as a sign that they were wavering on the " recognition " issue. This week-end the men on strike will receive strike-pay from their Union. The principal firms affected by the strike, which was declared at a meeting on Sunday evening, are Messrs. Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Mr. E. Isherwood, Messrs. ,J. Buckley and Co., Executors of J. Newton, Messrs. W. and J. Richardson, Messrs. W. W. Westwell and Co., Messrs. R. A. Naylor Ltd., Mr. H. Gandy, Mr. Howard, Messrs. 1. Fair dough and Sons, the Howley Mortar Co., and Messrs. Pendlebury and Co.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>MAYOR AS MEDIATOR. UNSUCCESSFUL MEETINGS AT THE TOWN HALL.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR AS MEDIATOR. UNSUCCESSFUL MEETINGS AT THE TOWN HALL.</p>
        <p>There were hopes of an early settlement when it was announced that, thanks to the timely intervention oi the Mayor, meetings of the employers and the carters- would be held at the Town Hall, on Wednesday, with a view to discussing the possibility of a settle- ment. They were, however, doomed to dis- appointment. The masters adhered to their determination not to concede " recognition," and accordingly the representatives of the respective parties met in separate rooms, the Mayor endeavouring to find common ground between them. The most that the masters would concede, in response to the demand for higher wages, was 235. per week for, single-horse drivers and 20s. for drivers of two horses. They waived the condition that the recipients of this sum must have been in the employ of a given firm for twelve . months. The men's representatives held out for .255. 6d. for one-horse drivers and 28s. for two-horse drivers. The Mayor suggested that the parties should agree to " split the difference," but this was not found possible. A deadlock therefore ensued and, after two hours negotiating, the meetings broke Up. In conversation with an. Examiner reporter the Mayor expressed his willingness to re- sume the office of mediator in the dispute, if the parties desired it.</p>
        <p>MR. HAROLD SMITH'S INTERVENTION.</p>
        <p>Last night it was announced that Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., had written asking whether he could be of assistance to the parties in bringing about a settlement. The men's leaders, however, state that they have no intention of accepting his offer, as they are determined to discuss terms directly with the masters. Both unions have decided to combine in the demand for complete recognition. Local trade unionists are coming to the aid of the strikers with financial support. INCONVENIENCE TO MANUFACTURERS</p>
        <p>The strike is, of course, causing great in- convenience to manufacturers, and unless an early settlement is arrived at, the inability to receive adequate supplies of fuel and raw material, will probably cause a serious dis- location of labour. As it is, a large section of the works of Messrs. Rvlands Ltd. has been closed down, but, up to last evening, the other leading firms had not been driven to this extremity. In the early stages of the strike exciting street scenes were witnessed, some of which have resulted in police court proceedings, which are reported elsewhere. On Thursday evening a body of the strikers marched to the Corporation Depot with the object of inducing Corporation carters to adopt the " sympathetic ' strike, but they failed in their mission, notwithstanding that the carters who persisted in working were subjected to considerable hooting and banter. On Thursday morning the carters employed by Messrs. Pearson and Knowles went back to work, but a few hours later they had re- joined the strikers. Corporation carters, it should be stated, are receiving higher wages than the strikers demand.</p>
        <p>Owing to the dearth of drivers, the heads and managers of several firms have been forced to the expedient of driving their own vehicles, and yesterday a justice of the peace was seen discharging this humble hut very necessary duty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>THREATS TO A NIANAGER.</head>
        <p>THREATS TO A NIANAGER.</p>
        <p>Michael McHaley, labourer, 64. Dallam-lane, was charged at Ito Borough Police Court on Thursday, before Mr ',V Roberts and other magistrates, with using threat:, towards Mr Felix William Praeger, manager of Messrs Crompton and Shavrcross, Ltd., coal dealers, Dallam-lane.—Mr Browne who prosecuted said, the defendant was a nuisance to the manager and the employees in the yard. The previous afternoon the prisoner went into the yard during the unfortunate strike and used threats to Mr Praeger. P.C. Corbett was called in and in reply to the warrant prisoner said " I never said anything of the kind." He also said if Mr Praeger has sent you I shall come in the yard when I like." Mr Browne continued that bis clients wished o carry ou their business in a proper manna r tompatible with the interests of the men. They would not have prisoner near the yard at all. He would certainly do harm to the monager unless he was bound over.—Mr Praeger stated that the prisoner was in a semi-drunken condition and he asked him to go away. He refused and threatened to punch witness in the nose. HA said "I will get evFn with you and get my own back." When witness called for a policeman prisoner said "No bobby will shift me." " I am afraid " continued witness, "that he will do me serious injury."—Prisoner said he was sorry he went in the yard, and was bound over for six months in two sureties of £5 ; to pay the cots of the case, or in default 14 days' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>SEQUEL TO STREET SCENES.</head>
        <p>SEQUEL TO STREET SCENES.</p>
        <p>DISTURBANCES WHICH MIGHT HAVE LED</p>
        <p>TO RIOTS.</p>
        <p>Strikers in Court.</p>
        <p>A sfquel to the lively street scenes was heard at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday, when nine ',len concerned in the attempts to upset carts appear«l before the magistrates on charges of committing a breach of the peace. The well of the Court was about ball filled, but large crcwds assembled outside the Police buildings to iiivait the results.</p>
        <p>The magistrates on the Bench were Mr C Jackson Holmes (iii the chair), Aldermen \V Bolton and W Pierpoint and Mr T Locker. Alderman Tinnion sat on the bench but did not adjudicate. Mr A T Hallawuy (deputy town clerk), prosecuted on bhalf of the police. Shortly alter the first case had com- menced Mr Needham, of Manchester, telegraphed to the Cleik that he had been instructed to defend the prisoners but, having missed hie train, be was on his way to Warrington in a motor car. The cow t decided not to wait and when Mr Needham anived all the prisoners had been dealt with.</p>
        <p>Alfred Moxey (23) carter, 318. linutsfor,'-road was the first prisoner called and pleaued guilty to a cbarge of curnmitting a breach of the peace iu Hofaeraarket- street. _</p>
        <p>Mr Hallaway said the bench would be aware that at the present time under the Trade Disputes Act " peaceful picketing "—he had almost said " peaceful intimidation "—was allowed. A great number or people had come to believe that since the passing of th,t Act they were allowed to do almost anything they wished. Picketing had been carried to a greater extent than the law ever intended, and scenes of violence had been witnessed all over the country. In Liverpool and Manchester recently they had had disgraceful scenes in connection with "peaceful picketing." Fortunately, in Warrington they had never witnessed these occurrences in the past, and they had been able to say that they were extremely proud of the manner in wince Warrington men conducted their disputes ; but on Monday, unfortunately, the feelings of the men on strike ran much higher than usual, and on no fewer than five occasions attempts were made to intimidate men who were wishful to work. If a man wanted to work he had a right to be protected, and in that connection he should like to read the instructions which had been issued to tLe police. He intended to go lengthily into the matter, because this was the first time that such scenes as those about to be described had taken plase in \A arrington, and they hoped it would also be the last time. It was advisable that this statement should be made publicly. The instructions to the police iroin the Home Office stated araong other things, that " A feature of the recent strikes Las been that large crowds of strikers, accom- panied in BOEDO cases by persons who were not strikers, have visited works where there was no dispute and have induced workmen, who had no grievance against their employers, to leave their work and make conuuon cause with the strikers. Where this is done peacetully, and the workmen leave their employment volun tt,rily, and out of sympathy with the strikers, no otjection can be taken to the proceedings But it is alleged in some instances workmen were induced to leave their employment by intimidation, and by fear of violence ; and, where that is the case a serious question arises as to the action which ought to be taken by the police. It has been suggested that such prcceedings are protected and justified by Section 2 of the Trades Disputes Act, 1936, and that the right of peaceful picketing given by that section prevents police interference ; but it is hardly necessary to say that the euactment in question gives DO protection to intimidation or to threats of violence, and that the duty of the police in dealing with tt ese otfences has not been in any way affected. The duty of the police is to maintain order ; to pre- vent riot and damage to property ; ti pi otect from violence and molestation, workmen who vo.untary con- tinue to work." All that was made lawful was picket- ing ':for the purpose of peacefully obtaining ot com- municating information, or of peaceltely persuading any person to wor.l or abstain from working." " Watch ing or besetting " was not criminal If it coeld be done for. the purpose of peacefully obtaining or communi- cating information, or of peacefully pertuatling any person to. work or abstain from working ; but it became criminal.if it went beyond these peaceitil purposes, and was carried on for purposes of compulsion. Tn the present case the prisoner had pleaded guilty to a breach of the peace, as on Monday he cire,v the " kecker-pin "out of a curt at the juncture of Horse- market-street and Scotland-road, and tipped the coa- tents of the cart into the roadway. He Ilan been warned not to commit the act tint tuoL no 110tiCe.</p>
        <p>The Chief C lasitable said that at 11.33 on Nlorritty be was in the vicinity of the Central Station, wileu be saw a large crwAcl of people cJilecting at the end of Scotland-road. He r%ent to the spot end found that a Corporation oust cart had been held up. Those in charge intimated that they bad been stopp.-d and threatened. He told them to proceed on their way nut before he could place his bicycle in a place of bufery, the prisoner, with others, stopped the horse and the prisoner draw the pin out of the" kecker bar." upsett- ing the cart in the street. He seized hold of the prisoner end a sort of half:hearted attempt was made to release him. The pritouer shouted to the crowd, " C ,me on boys, get me away." Witness held him and took him along Horsemarket-street, end handed him over to the custody of a policeman, and the prisoner was charged with committing a breach of the pe ice. The crowd were angry as a matter of fact a semi-riot was pro- ceeding, aud all st r. s of threats were being made. Alderman Pierpoint : Was the prisoner sober ? The Chief Constable : Yes, sir. Prisoner : As to passing remarks to the crowd to release me I did no such thing at all, because I gave myself up to the police. Alderman Pierpoint : Did you take the kecker-bar out ?</p>
        <p>Prisoner : No, sir. The Ci.ik : Have you any reason to give for your conduct ?</p>
        <p>Prisoner : No, I have said all now.</p>
        <p>The prisoner was then hound over to be of good behaviour for six months, himself in £5 and another surety in the same amouni,and ordered to pay the costs or go to prison for 14 days. Thomas Solan, carter (37), 22, Ellesmere-street, was charged with committing a similar offence in Wilders- pool-causeway. Mr A Browne appeared for the prisoner. P.O tiibson said he saw the prisoner calling to ft crowd of strikers to uaset a cart load of cinder; which had been taken over the bridge. He along with P.O Burroughs followed the man who was accompanied iiy six or seven men. The prisoner drew the tar out of the cart, which was tipped up. James Smith, carter, Moore, gave corroborative evidence.</p>
        <p>By Mr Browne (for the prisoner) : He saw the prisoner commit the offence mentioned. Alderman Pierpoint : Why didn't you prevent him? 'rho Cnief Constable: There was a big crowd there. Mr Browne said he had pointed out the absolute insanity of the prisoner's action. Solan was acting in concert with others on Monday, and all the men were very excited, The prisoner asked for leniency on the ground that he bad 11 children to keep. This silly work," added Mr Browne, was prisoner's " gre Aest height of folly." tie had been in the service of a well- known doctor tor many years, but. was now a carter, and he would undertake not to repeat the offence. A similar sentence io the last was inflicted.</p>
        <p>John Barlow (23), a carter, living at 63, Greennll- street, was chirged with disorderly conduct in Church- street, John Lunt (22), 116, Ellesmere-tree, was charged with obstructing P C Anderson while acting in the execution of his duty in Church-street, and Luke Skelland (32), of 25, Helsby-street, was charged %vial disorierly conduct.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Woodland said that he was escorting a cart containing coke, which was on its way from Messrs Pearson. end Knowles's yard in Tanners-lane to Messrs Itylsads Broo works. The cart which was driven by Mr Wright (P and K manager) Was stopped in Church- street, when the pin of the cart was taken out and the contents thrown into the street. Skelland put his hand up to the driver and asked him to stop as he "wished to ask him a question," but the driver refused to stop. Skelland took hold of the horse's head, and Barlow pulled at the "kecker-bar" and tipped up the cart. All the three prisoners were together. Luut tried to pre- vent the arrest of ths prisoners. There was a large hostile crowd assembled. Vhen charged with com- mitting a breach of the peace, Skelland and Barlow made no reply, and Lunt simply replied, " Yes." _ _ _</p>
        <p>. Barlow 'am sorry it happsned. We were told the police were on our side. Lunt said he thought the man bold of Barlow was on ordinary passer-by. The prisoners in this case also were bound over to Ireep the peace, rind the Chairman said the Bench were taking a lenient view of the cases. James Green (56), carter, 16, James-street, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Tanuers-lane, and was tined lOs and 3s costs.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said prisoner, who made his 13th appearance, bad a great deal to be thankful for. Instead of being in the dock he might have been in the Infirmary, if it had not been for police intervention. He was quarrelling with a carter and practically asked to be assaulted.</p>
        <p>James Street (23), labourer, 22, Philip-street ; Albert Atkinson (24), carter, 11, Challinor-street ; William Broomfield (24), carter, 9, Vere-street ; were charged with a breach of the peace in Winwick-street. Acting-Sergeant Edwards stated that at 3.55 p.m. on Monday he was on duty in Winwick-street with other officers in charge of a coal cart, when they had to pass a crowd of from 700 to 800 people. The prisoner Street shouted, " Smash his--face in." Atkinson called out.." Mob him, and get him away." Evidence against the other men was given by Acting Serueant Armstrong and Sergeant Hewitt and the prisoners were hound over to keep the peace with similar sureties.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>I PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>I PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>llt would be difficult to conceive a more laughable II sketch than the one entitled " Three Knocks" which has been presented so successfully at the Hippodrome this week. Bill Ashcroft (a sharp is desirous of marrying the beautiful Edith Fleetwood but in order to do at he has to prove conclusively that Edith's husband has been drowoed at sea. He solicits the help of Jimmy Josser, a simple minded sailor who, in his endeavour to relate the details of the concocted catastrophe, sends the house into uncon- trollable fits of laughter. This latter part is taken by Mr George Lill whose excruciatingly funny actions, especially when he acts as the " spirit, " ensure for him the heartiest applause of all patrons. He is the axis around which all the fun rev Aves and is ably supported by Mr Charles Goff who gives a correct delineation f a typical sharp. Miss Evie Conway captivates as the wife and Mr If Seabam is well placed a; tue merchant Miss May Erne and Erne Chester are ac, uple of versatile entertainers. Miss Erne (Ambi-pe-dexter) does not belie her title for she can use both hands and leet with equal dexterity, and dances remarkably well to her own accompaniment on an an unique instrument fastened round her shoulders. Chester at once makes himself popular with his phenomenal soprano voice. The most pleasing part of the turn, however, was the rendering in a very novel and elf -ctive manner of the rag-time song " That Mysterious Bag," for which they were encored. Curia Van Bern thoroughly mystifies the audience with his clever sleight of hand and other tucks. A host of decepions are carried out before the eyes of the dumbfounded onlookers, and , whilst one is wondering how the ticks are executed, Van Bern introduces a delightful turn of comedy, thus transforming puckered brovis into broad smiles. John and Lilian Grand make a welcome re-appearance, and are well received in a vocal playlet " At home and abroad." Their voices blend harmoniously in many popular numbers, and the setting is beautiful. 'the Four Cherie., give a lnAtliy dancing act, in which is introduced "Toe Staircase of Terpsichore " ; the</p>
        <p>artistes tripping and dancing up and down the stairs simultaueonsly to catchy tunes. Zellini is a comedy jnggler with a number of new tricks and pleasing eccentricities. Kittie Douglas is a captivating comedienne and dancer, and earns hearty applause for the manner in which she strings numerous play titles together in a connected story. The De llayos are a couple cf dainty comediennes and rag-time dancers. A clever child study is shown on the cinema in " Buster and the Pirates. " NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>The programme fur next week includes James A and Herbert 13 Jewel m " Change " or "The haunted castle,' a comedy drama in four lightning transforma- tions ; Jenkins Bros, comedy acrobats; Johnny Fuller, assisted by Miss Ada King, in an original act "Mrs Edwards and her cat"; Karr and Kooney, comedians ; Mabel Costello, the girl with style and voice; Spry and Monti, happy mixture c.f amusement; and James Dixon, Lancashire baritone.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>BOOKS AND READING.</head>
        <p>BOOKS AND READING.</p>
        <p>Lecture by Rev. Dr. Mellor.</p>
        <p>"Books and reading " sas the subjeot of a lecture delivered in Cairo-street Chapel parlour, before a large attendance, on Tuesday evening, by the Rev Dr Mellor. Having referred to the " nauseating poison" which was often offered to the reader in the guise of literature, the lecturer said one of the greatest and most deplorable curses of the present day was the Press—that free Press which had so often been announced as one of the glories of our civilization. What was really "going on" in the world very seldom got into the newspaper ; the Press was no plitee for ideas, unless those ideas were dead and done with. The Press was generally the haunt of unrealities, ti.e home of lost causes, and no newspaper deserved to be read till at least a year after its issue; it would, by that time, perhaps have some historical interest. If a man must read the daily paper, then let, him spend on it as little time as possible. The most valuable kind of newspaper, or journal was that which contained the least " news " and the fewest number of short paragraphs To judge by the number of pictures w inch appeared now in practically all our newspapers, even the reading of the paper must have become a somewhat forlorn business. Could we he sink- ing back- to the earlier stage if picture writing ?Perhaps for our newspapers, we ,;hould yet Come to substitute a system of p-rpetual cinematograph shows, displaying, in motion, all that was supposed to be going forward in this weary world. Instead of reading about a murder, or the trial of Mrs Pankhurst, or some scene in the House of Commons we should behold all t h se things in animatid representation, and the veil of many words wont removed. Personally, he saw enormous possibilities in the idea, and he felt that Perpetual Ptiblic Picture Palaces would be a fine substitute for a Pernicious Public Press. At any rate, pictures could not mis- repreaent. The writing if novels, continued the doctor, had become a trade, wholesale and retail, like the selling of bacon and drugs. Reading was one of the commonest of accomplishments to-day ; it was assuredly one of the most evilly treated, evilly used and evilly served. The test of a genuine book was the extent to which it wakened the deepest powers of the soul to aetivity and appealed to the innermost nature of the thinking spirit. The purpose of a true book was not to lest, uct but to educate, and these were not the same To educate was, truly speaking, to draw out the native powers of mind and soul ; as they ;ut it coll quietly, to make a man think for himself, to increase and strengthen his reliance upon his own inner faculties and feelings. A man might read a veritable multitude of books and at the end of all his reading be as far away from genuine education as he was at the beginning. Another man might read one or two hooks and, from his reading, emerge well on the road towards true education. Some good advice was given as to which books all men ought to read, and the Doctor contended that one should not seek to read too much, but, if possible, possess the books which were read, so that they might be read again and again. They must not be enslaved to a book so that they got nothing from it except what was in it. A good book was a stimulus, nut a narcotic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE BAPTIST ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE BAPTIST ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Western District Conhrence.</p>
        <p>The Spring Meetings of the above Association were held in Golhorransrreet Baptist Church on Thursday sfternoon. The Western District includes Liverpool, Southport, Birkenhead, Chester, Warrington, St Helens and Wigan. The ministers and relegates met at one o'clock when a substantial luncheon was provided by the members of Golborne-street Church. The Rev R D Lloyd, of Bootle, and Mr E R Owens, of Liverpool, expressed the thanks of the visitors for this kindness, and the Rev H Ogden and Mr B Few acknowledged the expression of thanks and welcomed the delegates to WArrington. The arrangements for luncheon were admirably carried out by a committee of ladies con- sisting of Mesdaa es B Few, E Wynne Jones, H Buckby, Pepper, Still, and Misses Wright and Ogden. At 2 o'clock there was a meeting of the general committee, followed by a general business meeting. At 4 o'clock a Public Conference was held, presided over by the Rev D Ross, of Liver- pool.—The Rev John Robertson. of Burnley, gave an address on " Tha young as worshippers." The address aces followed by a very helpful and interesting discus- sion. It was considered a special privilege to listen to the address of Mr Robertson, because this is one of the greatest problems the Church has to face. The large percentage of children pasiiug through our Sunday schools who fail to (Inditheir way :n.a the Church is a very serious matter. Mr Robertaon has succeeded very largely in solving this problem in his own Church. The Conference was followed by a public tea, when a large number of friends from the other churches, in addition to the delegates, were present. Preaching to a congregation in the evening, the Rev L H Marshall, B A. Bill ,ndon), said the oitlicultiss which confronted the Church to-day were totally different from those which faced the early Church, and yet they were just as great and just as baffling. We were living in an ago of theological change,' when our sacred decuments and rites were undergoing a re-interpretation. Sunday school teachers and °trier church workers had to labour in atmosphere that was both critical and sceptical. The task of the Church was never so great. Religious indifference had laid its cold dead hand on thousands upon thousands of our fellow creatures. In order to meet that in.-ifference, the host of non-worshippers and pleasure lovers which thronged about us, we must turn to the three It's of religious education. The first one was. Reverence, the second Reason, "being ready always to give an answer to everyone that asketh you a reason concerning the hope that is HI you." Toe third was Righteousness, having a good conscience." The essence of Christianity was Christ Himself. The Church's doctrines had changed chameleon like. They had changed like the passing scenery on a railway journey, but still Christianity remained the same. The Church's life was her devotion to her Lord. What was wanted in the Christian Church to-day was more rationalism, the holy rationalism of spirit. They wanted greater efforts to make men feul the utter reasonableness of religious life. In the labour and commercial world to-day there was no clear cut line of demarcation</p>
        <p>between the Christian and the non-Christian. Some of the non-Christian employers and employees took off th palm as the best in their sphere of work. It was that fact that tied the hands of the ministers behind their backs. Everyone of them should sanctify Jesus more in their hearts being ready at all times to give to every man a reason of the hope that was in them, having a good conscience. That was the path of duty, the path of power and the path that led to glory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>" EXAMINER " EDITORSHIP. Presentation to- fer: E. V. Walker.</head>
        <p>" EXAMINER " EDITORSHIP. Presentation to- fer: E. V. Walker.</p>
        <p>A pleasing little ceremony took place at the Examiner Office, on Thursday morning, when the whole of the employees of the firm assembled to make a presentation to Mr. E. V. Walker, who has resigned his position as managing director and editor, after 21 years' association with the newspaper and printing departments. The proceedings took place in the news room, where Mr. E. Iddon (chief reporter), on behalf of the staff, handed to Mr. Walker a handsome smoker's cabinet, in polished oak, suitably inscribed. In doing so he referred to the happy relations which had always existed between Mr. Walker and the employees in every department. His per- sonal associations with Mr. Walker had been more those of a colleague than of an em— ployee. He had often envied Mr. Walker his good humour in times of difficulty and his possession of such a facile pen. He con- cluded by wishing Mr. Walker every pros- perity in the future. Similar sentiments were warmly expressed by other members of the staff, and after Mr. Walker had replied in suitable terms, the pro- ceedings terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>BOWLING NOTES.</head>
        <p>BOWLING NOTES.</p>
        <p>On Saturday next a start will be made in the Works Bowling Association competition and it is to be hoped that the weather of the next week wilt be more con- ducive to outdoor sports than has been the case in the past week. The Council of the Works Association have discovered that the individual hvidicapping of tams involves more work than they thought of On Monday night they spent three or four hours in hard work and only handicapped ten teams. Members will be able to form some idea from that statement as to how energetically the officials work in toe interests of the local bowlers. The opinion of the secretary. Mr NV Patrick is that in the end the individual handi- capping system will be the finest they could frame, for if tbey got the individual handicap they were bound to get the collective handicap something like right. Fifty-one teams have entered the competition as against 63 for last year. Then is, however, no sign that interest in the Association is waning as the decreased number is accounted for by the fart that where last year some works ran several teams they have entered a reduced number this year probably owing to oversight. The draw for the Finlayson Cup Competition first round is as follows :</p>
        <p>Cro,fields Shipping v Greenings White Cross Wire Orawers v SC, Helens Cable No 2 Crosfields No 2 v Uuion _Canneries Orford Beam Shed v Cheshire Lines Grappenball Tannery v Roper), No I Crobfields No 3 v Fletchers No 2 Fletchers Moulders No 1 v Ilarrison and Edwards Houghtons v Rylands No 1 P and K Fitters and Turners v St Helens Cable No I Penketh Tannery v Firth Co Ltd White Cross Engineers v Rylauds No 2 Carpenters and Joiners v Fletchers No 1 Thewlis Grilliths and Edelstens v Longford No 2 Fletchers Moulders No 9 v Mersey White Lead Monks Hall &amp; Co Rivet v Richmonds Grappenhall Whitecross Netting v P &amp; K Wheel Shop Nnylors v P Stubbs Ltd Rohinsons Glass Works v Rylands Wet Shop Ropery No 2 v Howley Tannery</p>
        <p>Byes: Gmuity, Asylum, Armitage and Rigby, Longford No I, Castle Rubber, Crosfield Joiner, Warrington Tanning Co, Richmonds Academy-street, Roberteons, Fennell-street Tannery, Corporation Gas Works, Warrington Printers, Lockers.</p>
        <p>The following are the matches for April 19th corn- mencing at two o'clock and finishing at six o'clock. 2-0 Whitecross Wire Drawers, v St Helens Cable,No 2 3-0 Crosfields Shipping v Greeniugs 4-0 Houghtons, v Rylands, No 1 Grappenhail Tannery, v Ropery No 1 6-0 Penketh Tannery, v Firth Co Ltd Referee Mr J Willett.</p>
        <p>Increased interest in the Peter Peacock Challenge Cup Competition conducted by the Warrington Liberal Association, is evidenced by the fact that 57 teams have already entered as against the total of 36 team 4 last year. A meeting was held last night, and we shall publish the draw next week. It is expected that the total number of the teams will be over GO. Competitors have ample him. to practice, as the competition does not start until May 4th. Mr Clay has got the green in fine condition, ad everything will be &amp;me to ensure the comfort of players and spectators. The first round cl,ampionship in connection with the Warlington and District Bowling Association will commenceon April 261. There was a Council meet- ing of the Association on Tuesiday evening in the Pelican Hotel when the rules and other matters were discussed. '[tie draw for the third and Lurth round of the British Crown areen Issaciation bowling handicap was made at the Albion Hotel, Manchester on Welnesday. Play will commence at twelve o'clock to-day (Saturday) over the green of the Chapel House Hotel, Heaton Chapel. Amongst others who received byes was W Bridge, the Warringtoa Central player who, handicapped at five, is playing strong. " THUNIB 81A.5."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. THE FINAL.</p>
        <p>There was a crowded attendance and• Much excite,- mPnt at Messrs Walkers' Club Room, Bewsey-street. la,:t evening, when the final for the League Champion- ship was played off between teams representing the Cmservative Club and the Catholic Young Men's society. The club 3 had tied in the league games, and having defeated one another" at home "a keen con- test on the neutral tables was assured. The Conservatives won the match with ease, though the standard of play was only moderate. Scores : C.Y.M.S. SENIOR CONSERVATIVES. Handicap SO Scratch J Flynn J Durkin 100 T Lawrence P Cunningham ...... 57 IL Hughes M Cunningham F Walker 37 H Parkes lOO B J Kelly B3 H Bennett L Miller 0 Toole (Capt) Total 624 Total 787 Majority for Conservatives M.</p>
        <p>BABY. AND THE FOOD PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>Not one doubts the immense advantage of breast milk over the very best of artificial foods, and in many instances the the use of the bottle is prejudicial to baby's present and future health, and even to his very life. The following is an instance in eeint. It is a letter from the well-known author, Ladbroke W. T. Black, who writes from Chiltern House, Wendover, Bucks, as follows :- "On November 26th last my third son was born. My wife succeeded ;n Lursing him up to Saturday morning. December 7th. As the chid was very weakly we were satisfied it would not live if placed on the bottle. As a last desperate chance we decided to give lactagol a trial. My wife bad two doses that ' night and a third the following morning. Within 12 hours of the first dor e the full flow of milk returned, and has continued abundantly ever since. The baby has put on a whole pound in the last fortnight. I have written from a genuine feeling of gratitude." It is not possible to over-estimate the importance of the fact clearly established by this letter, that the difficulty as to bow natural feeding may be rendered possible to every mother who desires to do her duty by her baby has now been overcome byTscience. The mother, in this case, by taking lactagcl and thereby increasing and enricliing her breast milk, undoubtedly saved her baby's life, and there are many thousands of mothers who have done the same.</p>
        <p>It is agreed on every hand that baby's health and strength can only be ensured by natural feeding, and even then it is eSsential that. the mother's milk should contain its due proportion of fats and albumens. Mothers may have every confidence in taking lactagol, as it is not a manufactured composition but a pure extract of cotton seed, and is kept in stock by almost every chemist. It enables every normally constituted mother to nurse her child herself, thereby giving him the best. possible chance of health and happiness. Another immense advantage is that it prevents all nursing pains, being an excellent tonic for those weakened by illness or overstrain. Every nursing mother should at once obtain a few ounces of lactagol,:so that she may give her baby nature's diet. The usual dose is a teaspoonful of lactagol three times a day, but it may be increased or decreased al occasion may require.</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>WARRINCTON MAN</head>
        <p>WARRINCTON MAN</p>
        <p>Who Had Sciatica.</p>
        <p>"I caught a severe chili about twelve months ago, whch I think must have affected my kidneys," says Mr. C. W. King, of " Cheshire View," 203, Manchester-road, Warrington. " I began to suffer with sciatica, an was laid up for three months with the complaint. A sharp cutting pain used to start from my left hip and shoot down to my knee, and I was in terrible pain night and day. "I could not get any sleep at night. I was confined to the house tor three months.</p>
        <p>"I tried many remedies before 1 used Doan's backache kidney pills, but until I used this medicine I was unable to get any relief. I felt a lot better by the time I had used the first box, and T continued to make such progress th:lt by the time I had taken five boxes I was quite cured and able to get to work again. "I have had no return of the. crinplaint since, and I am only too pleased to give this bccount of my experience for the benefit of other Buffeters. " I have often told others how much good Doan's pills did me. (Signed) "Charles W. King."</p>
        <p>The kidneys are the blood filters—the blood is always flowing through them to be purified. As the blood circulates through the system it collects worn-out and cast-off tissue, and brings it to the kidneys. The kidneys filter it out and throw it off with the waste water, through the urinary system.</p>
        <p>Every twenty-four hours the kidneys dispose of 500 grains of poisonous waste and 3 pints of surplus water front the blood.</p>
        <p>But if they are diseased they cannot do their work thoroughly. Then the blood is only partly filtered, and the poisonous waste that is left gets carried to all parts of the system— Makes you feel heavy, drowsy, and irritable, makes your back ache, your head ache; makes you feel as if you have heart trouble—</p>
        <p>You get rheumatism when the weather is bad; the water is cloudy or</p>
        <p>gravelly; there may be pain in empty- ing the bladder, or you may have to seek relief every few minutes.</p>
        <p>Doan's Backache Kidney Pills over- come kidney and bladder weakness, help the kidneys to keep the blood pure, and throw off the irritants which make the bladder act too often or clog the urinary channels and make it Painful to seek relief. They clear the water when it is cloudy and gravelly, and prevent uric acid from settling in the body. Every box of the genuine Doan 'S Backache Kidney Pills bears a copy of an Analytical Report that the Pills are absolutely pure, and contain no injurious ingredients whatever.</p>
        <p>In one si.7eil raelcaze oil-2/9 bo.r, or 8 boxes 139. NEVE'? 601.1) LOOSE. Of (01 chemists and stores. or from. Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-.t., arford-st., Loll, lon, IV. Don't ask for backache or Kidney Pills,—ask for DOAN'S Back- ache Kidney Pills, and MAKE SURE the name DOAN'S is on the box you buy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>TANNER'S TERROR.</head>
        <p>THE TANNER'S TERROR.</p>
        <p>Death from Anthrax at Warrington.</p>
        <p>The unusual verdict of" Death from anthrax " was returned at an inquest candected by .alr Jones, deputy coroner, at the Warrington C,roner's Court on Wednesday, on the body uf Peter Mclntyre (49), 2. Hart's-place, a tannery employee. Mr Wki eal, H M inspector of Fectories, watched the proceedings and Mr usury Greenall appertied on behalf of the firm. Ci-therine Lloyd, 2, Hart's-place, said she had known the deceased for the past 18 years. he having lodged with her mother. During the whole of that time the deceased had worked in tanneries. About six weeks ago he returned from tramping the country and had no money. Three weeks later he found work at the Vernon-street Tannery. On April Ist the deceased came home and complained that one of his eyes was feeliug sore. He said there was a pimple ou it. In the afternoon he said his eye was much worse. On the Wedueday morn- ing he had to return home from work, his eye being completely closed, and his head very much swoilen. The manager of the works (Mr Yorsteri advised him to go to the Infirmary, and he did so. He had his head dressed and paid another visit in the afternoon. tiis bead and neck began to swell and he suffered great pain. The next day he was detained as an in-patient. On Thursday the deceased said " I taink I am dune far ; I must have got some poison in my eye." His relatives lived in bcotland but she did not know their 'whereabouts. Mr Seal : Did you notice that this pimple was any different from an ordinary pimple? Witness : He had a blister like a water blister over his eye. Mr Seal : Did you notice any discoloration ?—The eye lid was dark. He had a large yellow blister like a burn blister on his eye on Thursday morning. Was he in the habit of going out with men who worked at the tannery P—Not that lam aware of. He was of a quiet disposition. He used to have a glass of drink. The Coroner: Scotchmen generally are quiet. Witness : You never heard much of bine</p>
        <p>Mr Joseph Forster, manager of the Vernon-street Tannery, stated that the deceased had been employed by the firm for about 13 days. Decessed knew practi- cally the whole of the tanning trade. Witness advised him to go to the Infirmary about his eye, which deceased said was very sore. Mclntyre had been working amongst some salted African hides, but he (witness) had be idea there was any disease in them as Chinese hides were the ones mostly affected. The Ccroner : Have you any unarm provided at your . tannery to avoid the anthrax spreiding te the meu ? Witness : There are washing materials. Have the workmen the option of usiug them. Is it compulsory to wash after dealing with the aides ? It is optional. Some will wash themselves and others won't. What do you do to those who won't ?—We cannot drag them to do it ; we simply let them please them- selves. Are you subject to the provisions of the Factory Act witn regard to anthrax ?—Yes. In answer to further questioris, witness stated that if a man had contracted anthrax he might cammunicste it to anyone with whom he shook hands. A glass of beer might be medium through which it could ne communi- cated. Formerly they had used hides from China and at that time the firm mune under the Home Office regula- tions with regard to the provision of wasniug materials for the men. Two years ago they ceased to import those hides which they considered dangerous and the hides they now used did not come within the restrictions of the Home Office regulations. Still they tried to carry out the regulations, although they were not forced to do so. At the present time they were only treating English and African hides—norahassa's and Madagascar's. , The Inspector • What hides did the deceased come in contact with ?—lie had cone 31 hides during the time he was employed by us. The Inspector : Which hides—English ? Witness: They were Madagascars. The Inspector He was solely employed on Madagascars ? Witness : Yes. They arrived in a dry salted state. They were placed into water. The Inspector : Clean water ?—Yes. Witness continued that they Veen put them in lime water twice within throe days. They were hooked up and down and were not touched by the workmen's hands. Deceased was employed on the beam taking the hide off the iron hook. The Inspector: The hides are then in a wet condition and there could be no dust? Witness No, there could be no dust for the man to inhale. The inspector: Would deceased come in contact with the dry hides before they were placed in water. Witness: He might have been out on the bank helping men to put them in the water. Continuing witness said that when he examined the hides at Liver- pool they seemed to be alright. They did not appear different from what he had had before. They were not blood stained. The sample deceased was engaged on was really better than they bad had before. The Inspector: Supposing an animal died of anthrax in Madagascar, would there be anything to indicate that in the skin ? Witness: I could .not say that. The only thing I have seen in Chinese hides has been small rings which might have been anthrax. He had never seen such rings in African hides. African hides were not in the same state as Chinese bides wnen they came. In conclusion witness stated that it was impossible, in his opinion, to locate anthrax in hides by the naked eye. Dr H Sheldon, junior house surgeon at the Infirmary said that when deceased &amp;et came to the Infirmary he was suffering from a slight swelling on the upper and lower eye-lid. He thought it might have been a little septic wound, but there was no evidence of a wound at</p>
        <p>all. He was detained on the Thursday and died on Sunday. They took him in as a query case of anthrax. He called in the pathologist who made a culture and found the disease %'as anthrax. In his opinion death was due to anthrax.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: Is it an infectious or contagious disease. Witness : Anthrax is both. Ihe Coroner: We will not trouble about the difference.</p>
        <p>Witness: Anthrax is very highly infectious. Supposing anyone had shaken bands with him would they nave caught the disease ? Witness: It would just depend. You would have to have an abrasion of some kind.</p>
        <p>The Inspector: Lie could get it by inhaling or by contact?</p>
        <p>NVitnees answered in the affirmative and added that the case was a very extraordinary one. _ . - _ -</p>
        <p>- Asked if he could fix on a date when deceased could have contracted the disease, witness said the diseabe acted very rapidly. He did not think the incubation period was quite known, but it was certainly within a tew days. ice deceased must have contracted the dis- ease at the works where he was last employed from the 17th March. it was not contrected when he was previously employed. The Inspector: Was there a distinct pustule? Witness : No. The inspector: There was no central dark spot? Witness : No. He added that they could not excise any particular part of the body. Teir Greenall : Did you form any opinion of his general health? Witness : He wss a very good, strong, healthy man.</p>
        <p>„ , The Coroner complininnted witne on tile lucid manner in which he had given his evidence. He had been of great service to the court. r Seal (the Inspector), said the firm were originally under special rules which were limited to Chinese and West African hides. The rules were intended to minimise the risk of the workmen. For a long time the firm had not imported those hide- and it was not anticipated that the hides they now dealt with were dangerous. Careful inquiries would be made and the matter would be investigated by the hightest authorities, and if it were found that this class of hide imported from that particular locality was affeoted, representation would ne made to the Home Office in order that it could be scheduled under the special conditions which covered the importation of Chinese hides.</p>
        <p>The jury returned a verdict of death from anthrax, contracted whilst at work at the Vernon-Anet Taunery</p>
        <p>Mr Greeaall said he understood the man had no relatives to Le communicated with. The film would pay the funeral expenses.</p>
      </div>
