      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>SPORT IN AUSTRALIA'S FAR NORTH.</head>
        <p>SPORT IN AUSTRALIA'S FAR NORTH.</p>
        <p>In the aEtreme northern part of Australia (writes Mr C Price Couigrave to " The Sydney Mail ") the sports- man, be he an adept with the gun or fishing rod, may have a right royal time. The cAintry inland from Wyndham. the northernmost town ir Western Australia, may well be said to be an idf-al sportsman's paradise. Over the plains, which extend in every direction, are scattered lagoons, creeks, and other waterways, where, all through the year, and particularly so at certain seasons, the winged game, to say nothing of kangaroos, is only to be numbered by tens of thousands. The residents of Wyndham, many of them after a sweltering week in the little town, which is always in imminent peril of being pushed by the great ranges into the swirling depths of Cambridge Gulf, betake themselves for Sunday to the 20-Nlile Plains. Many species of ducks, besides semi-palmated geese, turkeys, pigeons and scores of others are to he secured. A party with two guns in less than an hour secured over 100 birds. This is no uncommon occurrence, as there are so few visits of sportsmen to the district that all natural life is abundant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Indigestion and Health.</head>
        <p>Indigestion and Health.</p>
        <p>Food should nourish your body and make good the daily waste which never stops, but it can't do that unless your stomach digests it. No wonder dyspeptic men and women are always weak and ailing—they're starved and often poisoned too. Starved, mind you, not for lack of food, but because they don't digest the food they eat. Poisoned, not by eating bad food, but because their stomachs are weak and their bowels inactive, and so the food they eat ferments and gives off poison- ous gases which are carried by the blood stream to every part of the body. It is because Mother Seigel 's Syrup possesses in a remarkable degree . the power to tone, . strengthen and regulate the action of the digestive organs—the stomach, and bowels—that it is still, after forty years' testing, the best known and most successful remedy-for indigestion, constipation, bilious- ness, and the many distressing ailments whicla are traceable to a weak or disordered condition of these important organs. If you suffer from indigestion, and wish to give Mother Seigel's Syrup a trial, be sure . you get the genuine article.</p>
        <p>Etna, Vesuvius, and Stromboli are reported to be in simultaneous activity. Usually when one of these volcanoes is in eruption the others are quiescent. Near Middleton, co. Cork, on Monday, James Conolly, aged 15, was crushed to death under a steam roller through slipping while attempting to ride on the machine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 12, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 12, 1913.</p>
        <p>HEALTH WEEK AT WARRING [ON.</p>
        <p>HOW IT HAS BEEN OBSERVED LOCALLY.</p>
        <p>The great health crusade, in which a large number of progressive municipalities are associated, in the form of a National Health Week observance, has been turned to good account in Warrington. Thanks to the co-operation of the Health Committee of the Corporation and the Citizens' Guild of Help, a variety of simple but practical means have been adopted of awakening a more wide- spread interest in the supremely important question of the public health, and endeavour- ing to uplift and sweeten the lives of those whose lot is cast in unpleasant places. _</p>
        <p>On Sunday most of the preahers ,at the various places of worship sought to bring home to the minds of their congregations, not only the old lesson—" a sound mind in a sound body "—but some of the larger social issues which it inevitably suggests. The addresses at the Bible classes and Adult schools also dealt with the same tonic, the speakers including the Medical Officer of Health (Dr. J. Coote Hibbert) and Mr. Walter Flood (chief inspector of nuisances). At Wycliffe Congregational Church the Free Church Council held a meeting at which Mr. P. M. Oliver delivered a thoughtful and informing address on such questions as infantile mortality and the slum death-rate. During the week lessons on hygiene, first aid and kindred subjects, have been given in the schools. On Wednesday the public were invited to visit the Mothers' and Babies' Welcome and the Bold-street Cripples' Guild which are quietly doing a useful work in widely different spheres. On Thursday the Mayor and Corporation, accompanied by ministers of religion and other interested townspeople, made an official inspection of some of the slum areas of the town and the Mayor afterwards declared that there were ample evidences of an improvement in the social habits of the dwellers in the various courts, as compared with the conditions obtaining last year. In the evening the medical officer of health (Dr Coote Hibb?J-1,) gave a lecture at the Town Hall on "Some essential points in the health work of the borough." The Mayor, who pre- sided, made an interesting allusion to the proposal to provide cheaper bathing facilities for workingmen.</p>
        <p>Last evening the school dental officer (Mr. Hedley Taylor) lectured on 'The care of teeth " at the Bolton Council School.</p>
        <p>A special feature of Warrington's Health Week observance has been a cookery campaign. Through the medium- of mothers' meetings and other organizations leaflets have been distributed in workingcl ass homes, on subjects like "Food and drink." Recipe cards, for simple, cheap and wholesome meals, have also been circulated.</p>
        <p>emerge clearly, viz, that the food problem is of vast importance. It wilt often be possible to trace back the ill-health of adults to unsuitable nourishment. Medical men can already demonstate the ill-effects of igporant feeding or wasteful expenditure. They might will re-echo the cry " Wherefore do ye spend money for that which is not bread, and your earnings for that which satisfieth not ?" That literally is what many are doing at the present aims. What kind of a race can be reared on chips ? The rice-eating Chinaman will soon be better tff than the chip-fed Englishman Engel's law is generally accepted "that the poorer a family is, the greater is the proportion of its income which must be devoted to food." According to a British Government rerrt issued in 1904 no less than Cl per cent of wages was expended on food . here the average income was 21s 4d, whereas only GI per cent was spent on food where the average income was 3Ge Gd</p>
        <p>The food question then is of the most urgent' iquportance where wages are smallest. It is easy ehough for middle class people t condemn wasteful- ness, but a sympathetic approach to the subject will disclose all sorts of difficulties which the poor have to meet—such as shortness of fuel, cramped housing, lack of utensils, overwrought housewives and want of storage accommodation. Those who have investigated the weekly budgets of wage-earners confess that they have learnt in the process far more than they can teach. People who have been responsible for supplying special nouriz,hment to poor families in time of need will have noted the quick tesponse which the body makes to better food. A few 'week's supply of pure milk in addition to the ordinary diet produces a marked improvement in physique. In this connection the Warrington Committee working for the celebration of Health Week, is doing something by supplying Mothers' Meetings with cal ds containing a few simple cookery receipts. All the dishes mentioned are cheap, nourishing palatable and easy to make. The various women's organisations in the town could hardly do a more useful work than to direct attention to the question of food values and the imperative need for proper cooking And is it not well to recognise that while the bread and butter problem is the chief one in life, it is little use to look for high thinking?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>MOTHERS' AND BABIES' WELCOME.</head>
        <p>MOTHERS' AND BABIES' WELCOME.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday afternoon an effort was trade to focus public attention on the work of the Mothers' and Babies' Welcoare, Academy-street, which gives instruction on the care and management of infants. Many towns people took advantage of the opportunity of visiting the Welcome, and mothers, with their babies, were present in exceptionally strong force. A practical addre?er was given by Miss Edwards, of the Health Department, and afterwards cookery recipe cards were handed round. A large number of entries were received in competitions which had been planned for the cooking of Scotch broth and rice pudding, in accordance with the recipes provided. The prizes, which will be awarded a fortnight hence, will take the form of use- ful cooking utensils. We hear that no fewer than 1,500 of the recipe cards have been distribgted through mothers' meetings and other organisations. The various dishes are cheap, nourishing and easily made, and the object of the circulation of the recipes,is to induce a proper appreciation of food values. The mothers were provided with a special tea by the - ladies who undertake the management of the Welcome.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>CRIPPLES' GUILD.</head>
        <p>THE CRIPPLES' GUILD.</p>
        <p>Those few members of the general public who vi,ited the Cripples' Guild in liold-street School, on Wednes- day evening came away feeling deeply impressed with the devoted and self-sacrificing work which is being carried on there, in the interests of the physically afflicted. It is a work which does not come into the limelight—in fact it receives but scant attention from the generality of the townspeople but it has, neverthe- less, an important bearing on the scheme of social regeneration. On Wednesday evening a group of boys were pleasantly engaged in basket- making, under the direction of a blind tutor whose interest in his self-imposed task is beyond all praise They seemed to be enjoying their occupation, judging by the zest with which they entered into it, and their cheery conversation. In another room two helpless little ones were being taught to read by ladies who noted with pride the progress of their pupils. There is also a sewing class for the benefit of the girls. The influence of the guild does not begin and end with the classes, for the workers carry on a systematic visitation of the homes from which the cripples come, the result being that many improvements in their surrounding have been effected. The problem of the crippled child is one of the most important that faces the 'social reformer, and one could imagine no worthier under- taking than this which seeks to place those who are so heavily handicapped, in a fair way to gaining a livelihood by their own handiwork.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>THOUGHTS ON THE FOOD PROBLEM.</head>
        <p>THOUGHTS ON THE FOOD PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>National Health Week has come to stay, and it will be well to grasp its significance and practical value. No one can long be engaged in social work without seeing that unsound health is at the bottom of much of our social degradation Behind the problems of the tramp, the inebriate, the unemployable, and the criminal is the question of physical unfitness. The subject of the feeble frame is not less important than that of the feeble mind. Malnutrition in infancy is dearly paid for in after years. And unfortunately the burden of the later years often falls upon the com- munity. sometimes, of course, the means of living are inadequate to meets the needs. In other instances ignorance and carelessness rather than lack of means are responsible for physical inefficiency. Whenever the State stands in the place of the p3rent (as in the case of giving out-relief to widows with young children) nothing could be more short-sighted than to help inadequately. If a family be stinted at its grow- ing period, it will become stunted at the time for maturity. Even where adequate amounts are paid, there is a call for careful oversight in order that expenditure should be wisely made. The f,,cid question ought to have far more attention than it has yet received. It is at the basis of physical efficiency. Among many of the wage-earners there is abysimal ignorance as to food values and a sh, eking negled of cooking, while the teeth of the nation are in a wretched state, thus making still more difficult the question of bodily fitness. Everyone is aware that the cost of living has been advancing during tecent years and that wages have risen in sympathy. It would be interesting to know how far the wives of wage-earners— these clever home chancellors of the exchequers—have benefitted by the increase of wages. From the standpoint of national health the housewife should have the first claim on wages and on advances in wages. It is to be feared that not a few men adopt the principle of giving the wife a pound for housekeeping purposes and keeping the balance large or small for personal expenditure. This is done quite irrespective of the cost of living, although this is the basis of legitimate claims for advanced wages. Many wives know .nothing of the actual earnings of their husbands and remain substantially content with a sovereign, when its pur- chasing power is less. The same principle applies to amounts paid by wage-earning sons and daughters remaining in the home. Often the amounts paid are less than the value returned in food and shelter. The same remark is true also of young men in lodgings. Many a woman who takes in a lodger with the idea of adding to her income is really losing on the job, and would be better without the lodger, even supposing him to be a man who pays fully and regularly. As social facts begin to be sorted out consequent upon the working of the Insurance Act, one fact should</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Thousands are condemned to such a feverish search for food, that they have no leisure to look around and up,W.J.R. SOCIAL EVILS:</head>
        <p>Thousands are condemned to such a feverish search for food, that they have no leisure to look around and up,W.J.R. SOCIAL EVILS:</p>
        <p>MR. P. M. OLIVER ON INFANTILE MORTALITY.</p>
        <p>"MOTHER TO THE MILL: CHILD TO THE GRAVE."</p>
        <p>A striking address ou some of our social evils— notably infautiie mortality and the slum death-rate was delivered by Mr. P. M. Oliver, prospective Liberal candidate for the Knutsford Division, at a health week meeting held at Wycliffe Congiegationul Otiurch, under the auspices of the Free Church Council, on Sunday afternoon. A point on which Mr. Oliver laid special stress was the alarmingly high rate of mortality among infants in those districts where married women work in factories.</p>
        <p>The Bev 1"1.' JAMES presided over a large attendance which included a detachment of the B Life Brigade from Latchford. Among those present were : the Revs W A Beckett, W Cooper, H Ogden and J S Ched- burn, Messrs C E Parker, h Wynne Jones, B Farrar and %V Beswick.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said that when they thought that throughout toe country some eighty communities were engeged this week in a health crusade, they hoped it would result in something beyond speech-making and that an effective influence would go forth from the many meetings that would be held. The question was not only important but complex. An encouraging feature of a meeting like that, however, was that it helped to bring home to their minds the fact, that organized Christianity had to-day It place in the social programme and that they were realizing, to some exteut,the social implications of the teachings of .lesus Christ and of His coming into the world. 0113 of the really valuable things that could be done by Health Wees was the emphasising of personal responsibility. By giving attention to simple matters in connection with their own lives they could do very much to ensure good health as fares they were personally concerned. Of course, in dealing with the great mass of the popu- lation, much more was involved, and they were faced with many social problems, tue remething of which would ensure healthful environment. Addressing the members of the Boys' Life Brigade, Mr James told them that to serve Christ it was just as necessary to be on the top of their form as if they wished to win a race or a football match. They must have sound bodies if they wanted to follow Him worthily and beccme brave men. (Applause). Mr P M Oliver began his address with the words "God created man in His own image. In the image of God created He him." That image, he continued, had become dwarfed and stunted, crushed down with poverty and hunger, bruised and battered with disease. The object of the National Health Week was to bring to mankind something of the lost image of God and in its observance he hoped they would keep their eyes upon two things. In the first place there were the practical things—the little things that were so easy to do and consequently were so seldom done. It was with a view to emphasising the practical aide that they had enlisted the co-operation of such societies as the National Food Reform Association, the National League of Physical Education, and the Coal Smoke Abatement Society, and that in Warrington they were going to have health teaching in the schools, lectures on the care of teeth and such a homely but necessary thing as a cookery campaign. While they kept their eyes on practical things, however, they must not lose sight of the ideal. In that connection they had the interest of the Garden Cities and Town Planning Society and other organizations which were pointing the way to a better and healthier state. Discussing the practical aspect, Mr Oliver said they ought to begin their efforts at th, birth of the child. "I have called it the beginning," added Mr Oliver, "hut in only too many cases it is also the end, because immediately we are face to face with a problem— perhaps the most terrible which any social reformer has to face—the question of infantile mortality." In 1912, he continue t, 95,000 children in England and \Vales died before they had learnt to speak. That was bettor than a few years ago, for in 1905, when very close investigation was made into the subject, it was found thit 102,000 died. When one realised that, after all, only some 500,000 people died in England and Wales every year, the present rate of infantile mortality meant that out of every five people who died, one was an infant who had not learnt to speak or to walk. In many places the infantile mortality rate was not decreasing. In the city of Manchester it had practically not decreased for the last thirty years. In 188 L the average per 1,000 of the population was 161, while in 1911 it was 155. While the infantile mortality rate in so many cases persisted at that terribly high level, the birth rate was going down. It was no use talking about the problem if they could not solve it, and he made bold to say that if the mothers had the knowledge,opportunity and advantages that they ought to have, at least 50 per cent. of those children would be saved to the nation. The investiga- tion made in 1905 showed that 20,000 infants died in their first year through mismanagement and exposure to cold, 30,000 through unsatisfactory feeding and 40,000 through the defective plisique of their mothers. That meant that 50,000 children died in one year through the ignorance of their mothers. That was where they had a great field for voluntary effort such as Health Week suggested. Though they might smile at a cookery campaign and regard it as very small in com- parison with great matters of State, there was some- thing very important in it when remembered that 30,000 children died through the ignorance of the mothers as Ito the proper nature of food required. When they came to the 40,000 who died through the physical defects of their mothers they reached a sphere in which something more than voluntary effort was needed. It was wrong that young married women, and especially mothers, should</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>WORK IN THE fILL.</head>
        <p>WORK IN THE fILL.</p>
        <p>At Burnley 60 per cent. of the married women under thirty-five years of age worked at occupations away from their homes and 20 per cent. of their children died before they were one year old. At Bury 45 per cent, of the married women were employed away from home and 16 per cent. of the children died before attaining the age of one year. At Burton, where only 2 per cent, of the women were industrial workers, the number of infants dying fell to -12 per cent. Where they got women staying at home, the death rate among the children was little more than half what it was where the women went out to work. Much as some people might dislike it, the problem must be faced. " You send the woman to the mill " declared Mr Oliver " and you send the child to the grave." Infantile mortality, however, did not exhaust the problem, for the children who survived did not altogether escape the effect of the vicious causes which had sent 95,000 to the grave. Of the 6,000,000 children WiCtO were subject to medical impection 600,000 were stated two years ago to have defective sight, nearly 2.000,000 had defective teeth and 240,000 showed signs of consumption. The Act which imposed on local education authorities the.cluty of providing for medical inspection also gave them the power to make arrangements for attending to the health and physical education of the children. Many local authorities had exercised that power, by providing spectacles for children whose sight was defective,by employing school nurses, and arranging with the local hospitals for the t-eatment of certain oases. Some of them were, now-</p>
        <p>ever, backward in that respect and it was the duty of those who were interested in the Health Week move- ment to enforce upon all authorities the necessity of their making full use of their powers. Passing on to the ideal which health reformers must have in view, Mr Oliver said that while we had</p>
        <p>SLUMS AND POVERTY we must have a tremendous amount of ill-health, not- withstanding all the efforts that might be put forth to deal with the children. Fie quoted the case of Huime in Manchester. Some 63,000 people lived in that district and there was only one open space, and that was a disused grave yard. The death rate in Eltilme was 20 8, whereas in Withington, one of the healthier and wealthier suburbs of Manchester, it was only 10 1. In Salford the average death rate was 17.4, while in the Islington ward it was 33'8. To realize the true effect of deep poverty and disgusting surroundings upon health, they must select a small slum area. A few years ago the Corporation of Salford wiped out several blocks of insanitary property, and in the wont of those blocks the death-rate was 78 2. The medical officer of health for Manchester, in his report for 1906, gave the death-rate in one-roomed tenements in Hulnie us 124.5, which meant that 12 cut of every 100 people in those dwellings died every year, and every man, woman and child in them had only seven years to live. Those were terrible figures, and showed the immensity of the problem that confronted them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>BRIGHTER PICTURE.</head>
        <p>A BRIGHTER PICTURE.</p>
        <p>But there was another picture to give them n hope. They could go from the one-roomed tenement in Halm° to the garden city of Port Sunlight, where figures were avaimble to show the effect of good surroundings, especially upon the health of the children. In the higher grade schools of Liverpool the average weight of children of eleven years of age was 701bs. In the better class Council schools it was 61Ibs ; in the poor Council schools 591hs, and in the Council schools in slums 551bs At Port Sunlight the weight of children of the same age was 79Ibs. The average weight of the child in Port Sunlight was, therefore, 9lbs more than that of the child, even in the higher grade schools in Liverpool. •That picture must give social reformers and every citizen hope for the future, because what had been done at Port Sunlight might be achieved else- where, although the task might be harder and longer. It would be the great work of the twentieth century to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>HOUSE TII E SOCIAL CONSCIENCE</head>
        <p>HOUSE TII E SOCIAL CONSCIENCE</p>
        <p>to these matters. In the nineteenth century we had an age of great benevolence and philanthrophy. One of the greatest things that century did was to erect in praetically every great centre of population a fine hospital, often raised by the voluntary contributions of the rich. kilhen the poor man fell sick, the hospital door was opened to him and at his bedside he had the experience of science and the trained care of nurses. The rich man largely provided for that, because, know- ing from experience what sickness was, he feared it and could feel pity for the poor man. Wnen the poor man recovered and loft the hospital, however, the heart of the rich man closed with the hospital door. The poor man went out from the light and warmth of the hospital down to the slum from wniclu he had come. The rich- man did not pity him in the slum. Why ? Because he had not experienced alum life and therefore did not fear it, and where they had no fear they had no pity. There was, however, something deeper, wider and better than pity. There was a feeling of brotherhood and love which was foundel upon a social conscience. It was to rouse that social conscience to a knowledge of the evils which surrounded us that Health Week had beeen inaugurated, and they hoped by such means to help to re-create man in the image of God—the image which we had known and lost. i Applause). Mr C E Parker, in voicing the thanks of the meeting to Mr Oliver, said he trusted they would all do their part during Health Week, in concentrating their attention upon the one subject of health They were all in danger of getting one ideal into their liver and feeling that that was the only cure by which the world could be put right and God's image re-created in man. Some were apt to think that the whole question would be settled by a universal raising of wages, while others relied upon land nationalization. It must, however,be realized that, social difficulties would have to he approached and dealt with from many points of view and it was upon the health question that they wished at present to focuss attention. As combatting the idea that it was merely a question of the poverty of the parent s,they had the significant fact that in Ireland, a much poorer country, where the wages were in many cases about half what they were in England,the infantile death rate was very much lower. The same conditions applied to poor countries like N orvray and Sweden. A large part of the work of Jesus Christ upon earth con- sisting of healing the physical ills of man. We had been accustomed to think that that was done to con- firm His divinity, but it was because Jesus Christ saw that there was a want of health of body or mind that He worked His miracles of healing. It was only in so far as we followed in His steps and endeavoured to allay human suffering that we were fulfilling our pur- pose in life. (Applause). Mrs E A Sanders sang with much feeling the solos "Come unto Me "and "0 Divine Redeemer." Mr W II I'4yton, Mus. Bac. playing the organ accompaniments. RELIGION AND DISEASE.</p>
        <p>Writine on" Religion and Disease "in the Burton- wood "Parish Monthly," the Rev A M Mitchell characterises Health Week as a wise, humane institution. Religion, he says, is a transgressor against the body, because badly ventilated churches and chapels on the one hand and draughty buildings on the other are responsible for much disease and sickness. The same remark applies to tbose sacred edifices which, seldom or never, are thoroughly cleansed and purified, in which " the dust of ages is allowed to collect. How far our churches and chapels are responsible for the spread of The White Plague "it is impossible to say, but that there is grave responsibility and considerable culpability is beyond disputation. We would carry the thought of the reader from the Nave to the Altar. There is no surer meats of propagating consumption than for the healthy to drink out of the serve cup, or glass, as the tuberculous, without previous sterilisation 4 the vessel. Yet in the most solemn service of the Church and at, as we may say, the supreme moment in that service, the same Cup is passed from hand to hand, from mouth to mouth—in, perhaps, 99 cases out of one hundred without the least attempt to safeguard the healthy Communicant against the unhealthy. To be compelled to drink out of the same cup with others without the least show or pretence of rubbing dry the rim of the Chalice, and even whilst infectious breath is clinging to its surface, is a thought both alarming and disgusting. What we deem loathsome in our homes we freely practice and encourage in the Church of God. There is much to be said in favour of individual Com- munion Cups, if the administration of the Holy Sacrament in both kinds is to bs continued. We wrote on this subject as far back as 1908, ride articles in the Parish Magazine: "Death in the Cup" and The Infected Cnalice." It is evident something should be done and done quickly to safeguard the health and allay the reasonable fears of devout Communicants. The danger is two-fold—physical and spiritual—the propagation of bodily disease and the loss of spirit power through abstention from reception of the Holy Mysteries. No one should should be exposed to bodily danger through partaking of the Holy Sacrament, Every Communicant should be fully assured, "I incur no bodily risk by drinking of this Cup." "The Cup of the Lord is not the Cup of disease." THE REC UDR AND THE SLUM PROBLEM. The Rector in the course of a health sermon at the Parish Church, on Sunday evening, made a comparison between our rivers and the rivers on the Continent and elsewhere. He had seen the Rhine, the Blue Danube and other rivers—splendid streams of transparent water —and he contended that there was no reason why our rivers could not be equally pure and clear. Then look at the atmosphere. When the wind was in the North we got the whole smoke of Lancashire. How therefore could we keep our houses clean. Englishmen liked to see fires' and that was very natural, but abroad there was not to be seen that polluting smoke which, he thought, could be consumed. Then think ot the past, when there 4ere castles for the rich and hovels for the poor. Things, however, had vastly improved in that respect throughout the country, and in Warrington. All insanitary dwellings should be pulled down, and we ought to have proper dwellings for the very poor. There was a great deal of neglect in the matter of personal cleanliness. He had been reading some of the pamphlets which had been sent to him during the past few days, and he saw from them how deafness, blindness, and lameness, and all kinds of disease might be prevented and averted if we were only careful. This was Health Week, and it was the duty of the clergy to join with the laity and try to help the officials of the town, who were setting an excellent example to make the town one of the best in the country. There were difficulties in the way in such a manufacturing county as Lancashire, but if we were only in earnest and unselfish they might be overcome BOLD-STREET WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>In accordance with the request of the committee appointed to arrange for the celebration of " Health Week," the Rev Hugh Williamson, 8.A., preached a sermon based on the text" Glorify God in your body." In the course of his remarks Mr Williamson said that a request to emphasize the matter of health is one which a Christian cannot refuse. It is a minister's iirivilege to see mare of people's home-life than the average layman sees. During the pastoral visitations terrible things are seen. Tee commandment. "Thou shalt do no murder," is broken in such a manner as to prevent punishment by the laws of the land. Children are murdered by culpable ignorance, when the observ- ance of the commonest laws of health would keep them alive. This state of affairs happene frequently. People marry, and in time children are born. The unhealthy, wrong treatment to which these innocent ones are sub- jected is a species of torture which ends in death itself. Many times it would have been kinder to have placed a knife at the throat of the baby immediately after birth than to bring it up in the careless manner that pre- vails in many homes. It is the duty and also the privi- lege of a minister to visit the sick and the sad. It would be far better and more Christ-like if all Christians did something in this direction. A splendid education in sympathy is obtained in such work. Any- thing that can be done to prevent sorrow is infinitely better, however, than visits of sympathy when sickness or sorrow • arrives. ,Many cases of terrible sorrow would never have,occurred if people would only live as God would have us live. This means that we must put our homes and our town in a sanitary condition. Many deaths occur through preventable causes, and if we can</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <p>. . •</p>
        <p>for their prevent the terrible sights of mothers weeping children, and wives wailing for the breadwiennerer' it is a work well worth our support. Titre are scores of people in the world whe 100 incapable through laek of vitality, due to the soa either of their fathers or themselves. Again, 2' children under the age of fifteen are blind throld neglect. Instead of raising our voices in anerAratn asking how God can allow all this, it will be to ask, why we allow all this neglect ? It is possilfort cleanse the country from many a scourge and the e is one which must commend itself to every follower fn. Christ. The law of Christ is a law for the wholeel!—,,, and the whole range of Christian activities. Our bocliede are His, as well as our minds and our soul! to he ig to His honour and glory. The life of a soul!, strenuous, and enough to exhaust the healthiest. only way to glorify God is to live as Christ would ha% LB live; and this is best done when the physical frealo is fit and well. The solo "Lead kindly Ligbt"' %sell rendered by Mr McArthur.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>WHOLESOME FOODLOW WAGES,</head>
        <p>WHOLESOME FOOD- LOW WAGES,</p>
        <p>BY C. E. PARKER. The local efforts that are being made help the most needy of the communitY 10. regard to cooking, the selection of nourishi% food and the like,are praiseworthy and Ing,e,„ needed. But in very many cases the troni lies deeper and cannot be remedied by sile„a means. The are no doubt many with income coming into the house, where ,t", margin is ample to enable them to leaa,,e healthy life, and to give attention to t% selection and cooking of nourishing food. in a large proportion of cases the dii-non; arises from an insufficiency of income. Wril 5 ever may be said of the income of artisage and piece workers generally,there are a large number of families in the town existing °llk weekly iiicome of 18s to 23s a wee'7, and this supposes constant work and °I breaks through ill-health or out of work. tried to frame a family budget on the basis 20s. a week but,do what I would, I could get provide the barest necessities for the anlollelle so I had to raise the figure to 225. and t".s following is the result. This is on the b3si of a man, his wife and three children. Bent 3 ,6 12 loaves at 3d ... „'" 0 Coals Gas Soap, soda &amp;c. -!;lb Margarine ... S Matches, blacking, Bz. of tea wood and house Milk condensed ,lO utensils Clothing Boots State Insurance Death „ 9 11 0 11 221' Now consider what our budget has not Fe); vided for. It has left no margin for beer 0 baccy, occasional luxury, amusements, sicl ness, out of work, furniture, &amp;c. Should ej additional child come to the home,the nattlo,o instinct of parental love is deadened by t°4 thought that the additional mouth ill somehow be . fed by the sacrifice in food °' - clothing for the others. What item, 1:10 perous reader, will you elect to cut dowo provide for the new child? In regard amusements, let any middle-class individllit or well-paid workingman consider whab.,e, would mean to leave all these out of his 114, Would such be able to exercise the iron se4o control which circumstances demand of !ha family in question? Here we expect 17f, man to spend nothing on hirnsetho never to attend a football match, /a visit a pub, smoke a pipe, h 3,1 a tram ride, or to buy an" Examiner," alff, the woman must never spend a 111 of Penny on herself, never have a glass .ko supper beer, never a new bonnet with .1:0 ribbon of red, never get a bottle of medic'Tie when she feels.a "sinking in the pit of tat stomach," never have an occasional night.„„v the" Hip "or even go to a Salvation meeting because of the collection. --ThEll--0 regard to the children no toys, no go-cart' lit cinematograph show, no outirvis. exceP., charity treats. There must be no sicknes:; accidents, or this model family budgeLt tio upset. If there are mishaps the only bani'- draw upon is the stomach. le Mrs. Pember Reeves, in an admirabo pamphlet just published "Family Life on 4 pound a Week," speaks of the question superior food value. tl5 "Superior food value generally rileg,„ longer cooking—more utensils—more wh°'; some air and storage conveniences thin cte be commanded by these women. To tacl porridge as an instance. When well coolcr, for an hour and eaten with milk and suPd most children would find it delicious wholesome. But when the remainder 0f1T.,0 night's pennyworth of gas is all that can,sve - allowed for its cooking, when the pot is same as that in which fish or potatoes if meat are cooked, when it has to be eaten litiof raw without milk and with but a hint a sugar, the children loathe it. They eat brelio and dripping with relish. No cooking required there, for which the weary, haras74 mother is only too thankful—so they airily" live on bread and dripping." This suggests difficulties in the case of poorer members of the community in carrYP'e, out the admirable recipes issued in congefor tion with the "Health Week." We haveo example a simple recipe for Irish stew • has to be cooked for three hours. .)-Yrof probably means at least an extra halfPer/Ave in coals or gas, bringing the meal for the l'„,jo persons up to Bd. It is apparently a sirgYff dinner, but means over the week 4s. Bd. 4-je we suppose this diet does away with "04 meat, fish, potatoes, and say Bd. of the breeig bill in our budget, it increases the outgoyLie, one shilling. This Irish stew is admirami "A dainty dish to set before a king" but bviii is it to be done on the 225. income A laity friend before whom I put the difficw,,/ suggested reducing the cost of the Irish stiig by cutting down the 11/2 lbs. of meat to pound, and increasing the potatoes by a 0116 weight. This lady also suggested redllg,lld, the cost of the Scotch broth from 6.W. to 1 by using half a pound of mutton instead pound, and the like through the list oftthe recipes given. No doubt this is necessaryllitli recipes are to be any use for the family' svore the 225. income. But what would be 111,be to the purpose would be to increase ;tie income to fit the recipes, not cut dow° Toe meat to fit the income. This is the 5.,3 point well expressed by George ShannVe in regard to the Housing Problem said age; should not try to fit the house to a low Ncr it is time we fitted the wage to a good holloe But how is this to be accomplished' tier' t answer belongs to another chapter altoge a- to Were I to attempt it here I shoio raise more difficulties • than I c, settle. I am content for the preseg:e6 having shown that with these low waveise, workingman, be he carter or anything4l,l, with wife and three children, has a terloo hard task in keeping a roof over his „00° and providing food for those dependent a"tror him, and he is landed in want whe,° strial thing goes wrong. Some talk of ina° ocl unrest as though it were a bad thing ast danger to the community. The unrie an° good and healthy; what are bluc3o9e dangerous are the conditions which</p>
        <p>this unrest,</p>
        <p>AST RIKI NG—EXAM PLE °1 genuine value is afforded bY the fact that Steedman's Powder's have had a steadily increasing sal3 for nearly 100 years, and still stan unrivalled as a safe medicine for children. These powders contain no poison. Observe the EE in Steednian'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>ARIZOL..JOIIN srrom CAE S</head>
        <p>ARIZOL..JOIIN srrom CAE S</p>
        <p>11.9 - - H.P. 4 Cylinder 63x 120, with Four Seater Torpedo Body, Detachable wheels with spare wheel and Tyre, Four Speeds £285. 15 9 HP. 4 Cylinder 80 x 140, ditto 095. A Y 1511'1 1:3 (oi:ins</p>
        <p>4 Cylinder 60 x 120 Wits Two Seater Torpeda Bfly, £197. 10 H.P. 4 Cylinder 63 x 123 with Four Seater Torpedo 831 V4l 19 HP 4 C d- 75 x 133, with Four Seater Torpedo Duly, y . H.P. y ..r Four Speeh, Detachable W:I2CIS with slare wheel and Tyre H»d, Serail, 5 Lamps, Horn, Tools, etc. £350, PALL.A.pluivi CARS</p>
        <p>10/18 H.P. 4 Cylinder (65 x 130) with High Class Three Seater Torpedo Body, Lamps, Horn, Tools . £250. Ditto Four Seater £271. TALBOT C fl</p>
        <p>HOW THE ANT WASHES AND DRESSES.</p>
        <p>There are many interesting things about an ant and one of them is the facilities it possesses for making its toilet. It has a sponge and it has soap ; it also has a hair brush and a strong comb, and in addition it has an extra comb with fine teeth.</p>
        <p>If you ever have the opportunity to watch the ant when it grooms itself (I suppose that to be polite I ought to say "makes its toilet ") don't miss it on any account. If you can watch it through a magnifying glass, so much the better. At first you will get the idea that the little black creature is going through a gymnastic exercise, but careful watching will show that it, is going through an orderly routine. The washing of the legs requires con- siderable skill for they are drawn through the mandibles or upper jaws. Here we discover the first surprising thing about the ant's outfit. In the jaws it can prepare a fluid which is quite like soap-suds, a very good toilet lotion which cleans the skin and makes the hair brilliant and flexible. The next part of the body to be washed is the back and this is done with a good sponge. Yes, the tongue is the sponge, for the flat surface of it has a circular raised portion covered with short, strong bristles. After scrubbing the back with soap suds, the , bristly tongue is drawn over the legs and leaves the hairs all lying the right way and in smooth order. The next part to be dealt with is the under-part of the body,which is covered with hairs. The hair is dealt with by means of the coarse comb. This comb has forty-five strong teeth (you can count them under a microscope), and is a fine instrument for rough work. No, it is not a comb that the ant carries in its pocket ! It is part of its body, and may be seen at what we should call the ankle, or the place where the foot is joined to the leg. And now the fine-tooth comb. This is affixed to the tibia of the fore leg. The fine comb has 55 teeth, and nothing can escape it. It is the most wonderful thing of all, for it is affixed to the leg and has a short handle and a stiff back in which the teeth are set.</p>
        <p>If you ever have the good fortune to see the ant putting itself in order, you will enjoy it ever so much. Its seems to be tying itself in knots, and its limbs twirl and shoot in all directions.</p>
        <p>Brushes, combs, toilet lotions, soaps and sponge—all these to make neat and beautiful a little creature only half the length of your finger nail. Nature has been kind to the ant in toilet requisites, and it shows its apprecia- tion by keeping its body beautifully neat and clean.</p>
        <p>PUT-OFF TOWN. Did you ever go to Put-cif Town, Where the houses are old and tumble-down, And everything tarries and everything flags, With dirty streets and people in rags? On the street of Slow lives Old Man Wait And his two little boysolamed Linger and Late, With unclean hands and tousled hair, And a naughty sister named Don't Care. Grandmother Growl lives in this town With her two lit le daughters, Fret aid Frown; And wntlernan Lazy lives all alone Around the corner of street Postpone Did you ever go to Put- c IT Town, To play with the little girls Fret and Frown, Or visit the home of Old Man Wait, And call for his boys to come to the gate ? To play all day in Tarry-street, Laving your et rands for other feet, To stop, or shirk, or linger, or frown, Is the nearest way to this old town. THIS WEEK'S STORY.</p>
        <p>Mabel, aged three, was taken to the butcher's shop for the first time. The butcher seemed a very terrible man to her and when she found the floor covered with sawdust, another dreadful thought struck her. "Ma," she whispered," and does he kill dolls too ? "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>RAILWAY FATALITY.</head>
        <p>RAILWAY FATALITY.</p>
        <p>Orford Man's Last Visit to the Races.</p>
        <p>The Liverpool City Coroner held an inquiry on Mon- day into the circumstances attending the death of Samuel Houghton (30), a labourer, who was killed en the Lancashire and Yorkshire Railway, at Walton Junction on Saturday morning. Mrs Jane Houghton, of Victoria-crescent, Orford, Warrington, mother of the deceased, informed the court that for some years past he had been leading rather a roving life.He was addicted to drink and spr nt all his money in liquor. He was of a cheerful disposition. and witness did not think he would clmmit suicide, She thought he ‘izited Liverpool with a view to earn- ing some money at the races, and was of the opinion he must have got on to the railway while in a state of intoxication.</p>
        <p>TLe body was found early on Saturday morning. It was about thirty yards from the station, ea the line leading to Liverpool. The head NVIII in the four-foot, and completely severed from the trunk. There was nothing to t uggest foul play, and it was thought by the aailway employees that he had been killed by a Manchester express. The jury found that the deceased was killed on the railway, but that there was not sufficient evidence to show how he got on to the line.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>BOVRIL AWARDED DAMAGES,</head>
        <p>BOVRIL AWARDED DAMAGES,</p>
        <p>IN the High Court of Justice, Chancery Division/ bef, re Mr Justice Eve, n Friday, 4th April, 1913, Bovril, Ltd., moved for an injunction to restrain the North-Eastern Railway Company from passing off as Bovril, or in response to orders for 13ovril, any other meat preparation. The defendants stated by their Counsel that the plaiting off complained of was done without their isnoNtledge and contrary to their instruc- tior, and agreed to give a perpetual undertaking in the terms of the motion, and also to pay £lO damages and costs.</p>
      </div>
