      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 12, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 12, 1913.</p>
        <p>NATIONAL INSURANCE.</p>
        <p>THE REMARKABLE SUCCESS OF MR. LLOYD GEORGE'S SCHEME_ Lecture by Mr. J. B. Martindale.</p>
        <p>To lecturing on " State Insurance in Europe " before the members of the Warrington Junior Liberal As,ociation, on Monday evening, Mr J B Martindale, prospective Liberal candidate for the Widnes division, had much that was interesting to say about the remarkable success of Mr Lloyd George's great health insurance scheme. Incidentally he forecasted direct:ons in which the scope of the Act would probably be extended. Councillor P PEACOCK, chairman of the Executive of the Liberal Association, presided and extended a hearty welcome to Mr Martindale who, they hoped, would be the winner of V% idnes at the next election. (Applause.) He had undertaken a big fight which only a man with the vitality and enthusiasm of Mr Martindale could hope to wage with success. (Applause). Mr MARTINDALE said they had Low had an opportunity of seeing the Insurance Act in full operation, for at the end of this week the benefits had been payable for a quarter. One of the first results of the measure had been an amazing alteration in the mind of the public. People were now Lcussicg their attention to a much gieater extent than previously upon the vitally impartant subject of the public health and the granting of the maternity benefit, with its accompanying presentations of mementoes to the first recipients, bad suddenly led people to realise that infant life was extremely valuable, as the raw material out of which we made the nation. In Switzerland during the present year a new Icsurance Act was coming into operation and, benefiting by our experience in England, it would include a maternity benefit of Hs. Toe Act would, however, go a stage further by doubling the grant to mothers who would nurse their babies for inure than four weeks. At the present time, continued Mr Martindale, 270,000 people per wok were drawing the sickness benefit. Mr Handel Booth had further stated that in one week, to his knowledge, over 300,000 benefits were paid. The probability was that prior to the Act coming into fo:ce, the vast majority of those 300,000 people had made no provision against ill-health, because there was no doubt that the friendly societies in times gone by had attracted not only a good class of life but a class which was thrifty in other respects. 500,000 people per week vr( re taking advantage of the medical benefit. The funds banked to date amounted to over £13,000,000, and a large proportion of it was ear-marked for the payment of invalidity pensions which would come into operation in a little over eighteen months. The maternity benefit was paid in over 20,000 cases every week. Over 10,000 people were already taking advantage of the sanatorium benefit, and it would be safe to say that over 7,000 would have bad nothing done for them, if it had not been for the Act. Much was said in the Tory newspapers as to the allege] lack of accommoda- tion to administer that section of the Act, but between 7,000 and 8,000 of those consumptives were actually in sanatoria. It was a significant fact that a great Corporation like that of Leeds was availing itself of that clause which stated that if a local authority wou'd raise a certain amount, not exceeding a penny rate, for the treatment of consumptives, the State would aid it in oqual proportion. (Applause). Touching upon medical hem fit, Mr MARTINDALE said there had been far more malingering dur.ng the past three months than ever they anticipateA, end he was sorry to say a great deal of it bad teen encouraged by the lightness with ehieli certain doctors undertook their respoesibilities. The doctors were in a very curious temper when the Act came into operation. They thought they knew best and that they could frame the finsst ieguletticiis under which they could work. They evolved systems for the Insusaece Commissioners who allowed when they thought they were equiteble, as far as the insured persons were concerned. Referring to the failure of the poo:ing of the insurance funds among the doctors in certain centres, Mr Martindale mid he hoped the eoetors had taught themselves a drastic lessen, They were apt, like meat professional men, to think they were the soul of honour, but they had discovered that certain doctors were quite ready to take advantage of the fact that the more attendances they could register, the more they could draw out of the pool at the expense of some other doctor. lie hoped the doctors would recognize, in the light of present events, that the Chancellor of the Exchequer was really wanting to do them good when he framed the feet. One astounding proof of tbe benefit of the Act to the profession was that there was not a single locum to be had in the country at the present time. That meant that an opeortunity was now given to the doctor who had hitherto bt ea handicapped by lack of capital, and that his qualifications would be the supreme test of his succers. That was democratic and, alter all, they wanted to democratise the medical profession which was so intimately connected with the life and comfort of the pe pie. (Applause.) About 14,000,000 people were insured at the present time ahel of those nearly 20,000 were purely voluntar) tha lie atgerebeudt forwtihthe pluck Mr Wort iington Evans when contributors. he said of Mr Lloyd George, we should meter have had the insurance Act. (Applause) He did not want to put any fly into the ointment or to rake up bygones, but he thought that when Mr Worthington Evans made such an assertion he ought to add " And it is really small thanks to me that the Insurance Mt is on the Statute Book." (Hear, hear.) It was easy at this stage to acclaim the Insurance Act and its author, but the Tories ought to have realized the greatness ot the Act when it was passing throueh the House of Comm ms and have assisted to make it popular in the country instead of putting barriers in the way. (Applause). Referring to the success of the unemployment section of the Act, Mr Martindale pointed out that the majority of the men who were drawing unemployment pay were unskilled workers who wou'd have had nithing coming in, if it hal not been for the Insurance Act. Mr MARTINDALE next made some interesting coin- parisons with various Continental systems of insurance. He said that in Germany they had State contributory insurance against sickness, accident, invalidity and old-age, wherees in England old-age pensions were charged entirely on. the State, while accidents were made the responsibility of the employer. In Italy death insurance yvas taken over by the State and Mr Mesterman had hinted that possibly the State would have something to say about that in this country, sooner or later. lie (Mr Martindale) thou ht the sooner they had indus'rial insurance in the hands of the State th? better, He did not want to say anything against what had been done by the great industrial insurance companics w'aich had undoubtedly filed a huge want, but every time be picked up the balance sheets of those concerns, and thought their profits had to be earned out of the ceppers which were paid by the workers—coppers which would provide a larger sum in death benefits—he could not help thinking that the workers ought to have, as under the insurance Act, the fuPest possible value for their contributions. On every occasion that the Gern an scheme had been amended, it had been in the direc iota ci eolerging its scope. Practically every worker in that country was insured, although their standard of wanes was considerably lower than ours. Having shown that the British insurance scheme gave better value to the workers than those of other countries, Mr Martindale expressed the view that some national provision ought to be wade by which employers c.iiid insure against the risk of accident. It was not satie- factory that private enterp.ise should be entirely responsible for that branch of insurance. England, continued Mr Martindale, was the last not only of the great powers but of the secondary powers to adopt a scheme of National Insurance against unemployment. National Insurance was not thet unique measure that people would have us believe. Some Tories talktd as if it were a had dream of Mr Lloyd George's, whereas it was a vision. It was, however, quite wrong to suppose that Mr Lloyd George suddenly revolved the scheme,for a staff had been investigating the conditions in Continental nations'eince 1007. We had done with insurance, what we did with airships. In the latter respect we were told that we were behind our Con- tinental rivals, but they were continually reading in the Continental Press of Zeppelin airships being wrecked, lie would much prefer that Germany should spend her money on experimenting then we should spend wars on huilding airships to be smashed.We could afford to wait, as we did in the case of the submarine boat. In drawing up our insurance scheme we bad profi'ed by the experience of Germany and consequentiy our Act contained the narrowest percentage of flaws. The Germans got Protection land had their fingers blistered. They got national insurance and found in it a perfect salve. We hid got the salve without the blisters. (Applause.) In conclusion Mr Martindale declared that the old age pensions and national insur- ance had done more for the vast mass of the people than any other measures of social reform since the abolition of the Corn Laws. (Applause ) Mr E LLOYD JONES, in proposing a vote of thanks to the chairenan and Mr Martindale, said be frequently had occasion to visit the Widnes division and he found that Mr Martindale was creating an atmosphere of Liberal- ism had never existed there before. If Mr Lloyd George or any other Chancellor for the next twenty years ventured to touch the question of death insurance, he would be forfeiting the confidence of those who had done most for the successful working of the Insurance Act, Over 6,000,000 of those insured under the Act had been brought into the scheme by the, active co-operation of the industrial insurance companys and the collecting friendly societies. When Mr Lloyd George asked for their co-operation be made a definite promise that he would not, under an consideration, deal with</p>
        <p>the vest ion of death benefits. Moreover, life insur- ance in thislcountry was not in mot) a condition 53 would pay the State to take it up. The average dna- tion of policies on the lives of the workers was only Eq ytars, because the industrial cmpanies and collecting societies took them, without rnedicAl examination,up to £25, the result being that the death ra'e was so high that there was no greAt profit in the industrial depart- ment. The irsuracce of Ow aristocratic, p!orissional and middle classes would be a far more prpfkable under g to the State. Mr TOUL3LIN s,conded and said that no ore but the finest rabid Tory would now wish to see the Insurawe</p>
        <p>Act repealed. In acknowledging the vote, Mr Martindale said he was only offering tentative p oposals as to the direc- tion ir which the insurance scheme might ultimately be extended. Nothing was further from his mind than the industrial computes should be penalisad. Finally Mr Martindale said the !Isn't of the last election was a disgrace to Warrington. The result at Widnes could hardly be regarded in the same light, because that con- stituency was chronically Tory whir; Warrington had previot.sly had a Liberal member. They gut rid of him aad look whom they replaced him with! (Laughter.) He felt ccrifident that CJuncillor Peacock, as a self- respecting citzen of Warrington, felt he had something to do for the borough in removing that reproach. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>GUARDIANS' ELECTION.</head>
        <p>GUARDIANS' ELECTION.</p>
        <p>PUBLICAN DEFEATED IN ORFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>Few e!ections of recent years have aroused such a slight degree of interest as the contests for seats on the Warrington Board of Guardians which t ,ok place in two of the wards of Warrington on Monday. Any excitement which might, under other circumstances, have been eng-ndered at the last moment was quite lacking in vies of the strong caunter-attraction in the shape of the lively street scenes attendant up 'n the carters' strike. In Orford Ward there was a three- cornered fight, Mr J A Rush so: fa, licensed victualler, the retiring representative, being opposed by Mr. Francis McKinley, insurance superintendent, and Mr Edward HA-rocks, joker, the last-named having previously unsuccrssfully s.ught municipal honours in tha Lab.mr interest. In Orford Ward Mrs Mary Roberts challenged the return of Mr George Allen (the retiring member). At the close of the poll, the ballot boxes were con- veyed to the Board Room at the Workhouse, where the counting proceeded under the supervision of the re:urning officer (Mr A Bottotnley). The rezults were announced as follows :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>HOITLE Y.</head>
        <p>HOITLE Y.</p>
        <p>*Mr Allen 251 Mrs Roberts</p>
        <p>Maj. r:ty 54 ORFORD.</p>
        <p>Mr McKinley 333 *Ar Rushworth Mr liorrocks</p>
        <p>Majority for Mr Kinley over Mr Rusttwor,h *ll notes retiring member. _</p>
        <p>Mr ALLEN prop sed a vote of thanks to the Return- ing Officer and the officials for the excellent way in which they bad conducted the poll. He commended the way in which Mrs Roberts had f ught tho election and described her as" a good sportswoman." Mrs ROBERTS in seconding said she was well satis- fied, and had been beaten fairly this time, Mr MCKINLEY expressed his admiration for the con- duct of his two opponents. Nothing of a personal nature had been indulged in. Mr RE:AMORY!! said that all the art angements fur the poll had been conducted in a most impartial spirit. Mr IlonitocKs said be had never met a more impar- tial body of men than the officials comected with that election.</p>
        <p>The motion was heartily carried, and Mr B OTTOMLEY returned thanks.</p>
        <p>VIADUCT WARD, NEWTON.</p>
        <p>In this Ward Mrs Mike put up a keen fight against Mr M W Set in, the retiring member. The result was ai follows : Mr M W Seten Mrs Jennie Clarke</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Majority lB6 IN THE RURAL DISTRICT.</head>
        <p>Majority lB6 IN THE RURAL DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>There were two contests f.r the ( face cf, Runt District Councillor which also carries a seat on the Board of Guardians. BURTON WOOD. Considerable interest centred in the fight in this village where the redoubtable Vicar (Rev A M Machell) was opposed by Mr William Yairclough, farmer. Hearty congratulations have been (stended to the Vicar on the result which was as follows : Rev A M Mitcht-11 Mr William Fairclotigh</p>
        <p>M irity PENKETEL</p>
        <p>Dr Murray having retil ed, the contest was between Mr Philip C Garnett and Mr George Foulkner, chair- man of the local bracc4 of the Lab, ur pars. The result was as followt : Mr Philip Carlisle Garnett Mr George Faulkner llO</p>
        <p>Majority</p>
