      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>FROM POVERTY TO PROSPERITY.</head>
        <p>FROM POVERTY TO PROSPERITY.</p>
        <p>" Regarding a man's prospects ber,, it is onlY ne-essary to say that the majority of the farmers eronnd he-e are men who, a few years ago, came from the Old Cauntry with nothing but determination to work and make for themselves what they could not make in England—'a home.'" _ _</p>
        <p>This extract from the letter of a British settler in Ontario is interesting as illustrating the more p1.,- pitious conditions in that province as compared with the Old Land. Prosperity, independence, even affluence wait on industry in Ontario. A writer recently set out to inquire as to the progress of an Englishman who settled in Ontario. Ho arrived practically penniless and without experience. The first year he saved more money than he bad ever done in his life. The second year he rented a small piece of lend. A year later saw him the owner of a thriving farm—not all paid for,it is true. With the fourth he extended his holding, and in less than five years he was a prosperous farmer with a balance in the bank. That was not accomplished with- out hard work •, but it is what can be expccted by any man who is willing to use end develop his natural ability.</p>
        <p>Offertory boxes in All S .ints' Church, Friern Barnet, have been tampered with and charred by the insertion of lighted matches, and it has been decided to keep the church locked on Sunday between the services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS RE-ELECTED CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>Councillor Pemberton and a Missing Notice.</p>
        <p>Councillor Henry Roberts was re-elected chairman of the W.rrington 11:ducatien Committee at the ant ILI meeting of thal authority, held at the Town Hall on Monday evening. There were present :—Aldermen A Bennett and J Screthurot, Councillors H Roberts, J T Locl,er, J Pemberton, It Henshall, J Dickenson and J Brorldhurst,ttelßevs U C Briggs and Father N Messrs C E Parker, R T Fairclough, 11 Kingdon, A J J Jarru tn,E Plinsto' n J Upson, J Lengland, C El Unswortn, C S Burgess, J Moore Murn) (director), and R B Roberts (assis..ant director). Mr E PLINSToN proposed the re-election of Councillor Roberts as chairrna:: of the committee. liverybAy who had been a member of Oa., authority for the last two or three yeas knew bow faithfully and well Cnuncilhir Roberts had discharged the duties. Mr R T FAIIICL Calf Sr conced. lie said Councillor Roberts had filled the p,,sition very worthily and they could not got a man who was batter fitted for the position. The proposition was carried with acclamation. Councillor R trEirrs, in returning thanks, raid he would Lot he speaking the truth if he did not arty Li at he cordially appr ciated that to e ‘al of their cocfl• deuce. He considered the position no extremely honourable as well as responsible one, but with their cordial co-operation, he had no douit tha,,, the work of the committee would proceed rs satisfactorily as in the past. Mr A J JOLLEY was re-elected deputy-chairman, on the prof° ition of Mr l'rs .s, seconded dy CounJillor DICKENSON.</p>
        <p>Ihe CHAIRMAN extended a cordill welcome to a new member, Mr J Longland, and expressed the hope that they would have the plea ore and advantage of his company and assistarce for a considerable time. When the return of attendances was submitted, Coun- cillor PEMBERToN complained that he did not receive notice of the last meeting of the Education Committee. I here were several cccasions on ahich he had not received notice of rub-committee meetings—not often of those connected with the Educ.scion authority—but he thought that was the first time he had not had the summons t.) the Education Committae. Before; running such a risk again he would not object to paying a registiaion fee for his notices. Lie bad a strong feeling on the matter, for the reason that be had intended to bring up a certain question in connection a ith the Sites and Buildings Committee. tie was actudly in the Town Hall attending another rneeting,and did not kncw the Education Committee was sitting, The DIRECToR : I cal only ray that the summons would be posted, and it has not been returned by the Pest Oflioe. We have never had experience of letters being absolutely lost in that way. Councillor PEMBERTON : My answer to that is that I say deliberately 1 Lever lual the notice. I am not siying a hose fault it is, but I feel very strongly about this one. The CHAIRMAN : I don't think anybody doubts</p>
        <p>_ your statement that you have not received the- notice. The cnly explaratkat that can be given is the one just given, that the notice has not been recur' el. Councillor PEMBERTON : If it was posted. Alderman SMETHURST : He says " If it was p)sted!" The DIRECTOR : I can only say this : It is hardly likely that one wott7cl be left out. It is almost certain to have been posted. The clerk is here, and he will tell you whether it was checked. (A moment later) : The clerk is quite satisfied it was posted and checked. Jr.uncillor PEMBERTON: It may be, but I didn't get it. The DIRECTOR : Anyway we can lardly be respon- sible for what happens in your home. It is not the first time you have accused us of not Eending notices, or stat,:,.d that you have not rc c3ived the notices. Coulcillor PEMBERTON : That is true. The DIRECTOR : But on every occasion e have been able to prove by the post b.)ok tbat they had been ported. Councillor PEMBERTON : Nothing of the kind 1111.1 Murray, I have spokm about the matter before, and you may have checked the post-book, but that doesn't ray it wrs posted.An accident may have happeted, but if it is registered there caa he no mistake about it. The DIRECTOR : We have never had this charge made spinet, us by any other member of the ccmmittee. Councillor PEMBERTON : '1 bat makes no difference. I don't want to have any more letters m's,ing. Mr II KING DON : May I suggest that the suffe.- cettes might have had something to do with it. (Laughter). A'derman SM F,T,IURST : How many days is it posted hot re the time of meeting ? The DIRECTOR: The notices are posted on tie Thursday. Aldermn SIM ETU UR ST : Three clear days. The notice rhould have been returned in th..t length of</p>
        <p>time. The CHAIRMAN : lam Or iid we canrot carry the n a ter any further at this end. It is quite evident the noi ice has teen duly posted, and at your end it h s not t eon received. You are between two stools. Conncillor PEM BERTos : I cm have them registered cannot I ?</p>
        <p>_ . The DIRECTOR : If yOll like to pay for them. Councillor PEMBERTON : Cerainly. I offered to do that.</p>
        <p>- ihe }TA IR MAN : Well, th ngreed to. Cotmcillor PEM BERTON : hank you very much.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>REV. DR. MELLOR ON " MATERIALISM."</head>
        <p>REV. DR. MELLOR ON " MATERIALISM."</p>
        <p>The Rev Dr Mellor gave an address on " Materalism•' before the members of tha Warrington Socillitt Society on Monday evening. He said Materialism mu t he discussed as a philosophical theory, not as a scientific hyphothesis. It must be treated as a reasoned answ r to the question ; What is Reality ? or what is the basis of the explanation of experience? These were philoso- phical questions, ultimate questions', and MateriaFsm professed to be one of many answers to them. The diffefence between scientifla hypothesis ard philo- s)phical theory must be insisted upon. It was one thing to assume, for purposes of scientifi: observation and experiment, that the phenomena dealt with were, in the essence, material, subj,mt to the inviolable law of cause and effect, capable of being dealt with from a m athe- matical and qunntative point of view. It wits quite another thing to declare that such a working hypothesis was ultimately true,and the last word that could be said about reality. Yet this was what materialism profeswd to declare. Dr. Meller pointed cut that, even as a scientific hypothesis, materialism is by no means unetni- mously ad ,pted by scientists, and the sciences oi biology and psychology at any rate seemed to demand another and wider hypothesis, lie referred to the theories of vitalism and activism which bulk largely in scientific thought to-d ay. Passing to materialism as a philosophical theory, Dr. Mellor said that he did not believe there was to be found a genuine materialist anywhere : certainly no philosophical thinker of repute accepted n:at-rialism as final truth. If there was a genuine materialist, what such a man would have to say would be that reality is in essence material and every- where subject to the law of cause and effect, mechanic- ally determined. The materialist is the man who says that, as between matter and mind, matter is the original, fundamental, prior principle, and mind is secondary, contingent, accidental. The materialist holds also, that the only principle of explanation in experience is a mechanical one, fill we need and all we can look for is the mechanical working of the so-called laws of matter, chief of which is the law of Cause and effect ; and the only causes are mechanical causes, simply the management of identical particles, or the varied combination of indestructible elements. Differ- ences between things are merely differences in arrange- ment or combination, or, generally, determination. ' Mind is the product of a certain peculiar arrange- ment of matter, determined by mechanical forces. Materialism denied all freedom, all spontaneity, and all genuine creati7e activity. With these various points Dr Mellor dealt at length, and then proceeded to point out the difficulties of Materialism. He instanced three especially :—Firstly, the difficulty of the Concept of Matter itself. What did the materialist mean by Matter ? Huxley declared that the notion cf matter in any philosophical sense was unthinkable. Secondly, there was the difficulty of showing how matter, even when riven, could produce mind. The materialist simply assumed that mind came from matter without proving how, or why, or when. In the third place, and this was the greatest difficulty in Materialism, how did the materialist know his theory to be true ? What Et indard had he for value or truth ? Mind, on the materialist view, is simply the accidental result of a certain combination of material atoms mechanically determined. Such a combination, in one case, may give a mind that believes in Materialism ; another combina- tion, in the same way, may give a mind that believes in idealism. Both themes owed their origin to exactly the same mechanical source, and, on the strict materialistic view, there could be nothing to choose between them No materialist could ever prove his theory true as against anyone who denied it. Materialism gave no test for truth, and no standard of value. This final paradox of materialism was made perfectly clear to the audience, the majority of whom, in the end, seemed ready to agree that no tensible man could possibly hold materialism as a philosphical creed. It might serve es a tcirntiflo hypothesis, but not a 3 an ultimate of explanation of life and experience There was a large attendance and a good discussicn</p>
        <p>A horse belonging to Messrs W H Smith and Son, wag seriously injured on Monday outside the Law Courts in a collision with a London General omnibus. The van was overturued and the driver's arm was broker].</p>
