      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 12, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 12, 1913.</p>
        <p>'ALL RIGHTS RIMIRVED</p>
        <p>PEGGY THE REBEL.</p>
        <p>BY ETHEL M LORIE</p>
        <p>Author of "The Spirit of a Ma n, " "The Bearing of It," 'The Idealist," " A Perfect Gentleman," ire.</p>
        <p>Peggy was twenty-three, and a hot revolu- tionist; and as her sweet-faced but somewhat insignificant mother sat opposite her, and realised that fact, her thoughts travelled back eighteen years to what she believed to be the first sign of Peggy's rebellion. She was mistaken, however. Revolutionists are born, not made, and Peggy's future atti- tude was declared at a much earlier age than five, though her tactics were not then rightly deciphered. At five there was open fight. Peggy had been censured by her nurse--a staid woman, who ruled the elder sisters with a rod of iron—for her callousness as to the requirements of the child's numerous doll family. She had apparently shown remorse, and the worthy nurse was therefore thunder- struck when, on entering the nursery an hour later, she found her youngest charge, lying full length on the sofa, beneath each of the four castors of which lay the naked body of a doll. She didn't look up as the woman came in, but hummed loudly to herself, and appeared to be engrossed in a picture-book. "Miss Peggy," Jenny said, angrily, "take them poor dolls out at once, you naughty, cruel little girl! " "No," Peggy said, slowly, not attempting to move, "I won't. I tried to get their best clothes on, and Agncs washed them too tight —the dolls is too fat, so I'm squashin"em down. P'raps if you was to get on the sofa too," she suggested, with a kindly critical glance at her guardian's ample proportions, "they mightn't have to stly down so long; but p raps you'd better not—you might burst 'em.</p>
        <p>"Well!" retorted Nurse, with a snort, as she dragged the child down from her position and rescued the unfortunate and now deeply- dented dolls. .</p>
        <p>Peggy spent the next five minutes in the corner, and on coming out sat down in a low chair and crossed her legs. " Nurse ! " she said, surveying the woman's carefully-saddened face. No answer. _</p>
        <p>" Nurse ! " she said again. And as there was still no reply, she went on: "What's a ' bloke '? "</p>
        <p>The woman's face was a study. "Where've you been, Miss Peggy?" she demanded, sternly. "Out in the Gardens, yesterday," Peggy went on, calmly. " Ainger's nurse told Agnes she'd copped a bloke.' What's a bloke?' With an inward vow of vengeance upon Agnes, Nurse turned a severe face to Peggy, whose wide grey eyes were full of curiosity. " Uncross your legs this instant, Miss Peggy!" she commanded. "That's very un- ladylike ! " And Peggy, in her eagerness for an answer, did so. "Now—what's a bloke?" she asked, in.- sistently. "A bloke," Nurse said, shooting her words like stones from a catapult—" bloke is a word no lady uses, and "Don't want to be a lady ! " Peggy re- torted, and being, as a matter of course, con- fined to the corner for another ten minutes she repeated that determination many times. Now, at twenty-three, she was propounding theories to her mother and two sisters. They were stitching dainties for Peggy's trousseau, and that young lady was sitting on the table surveying them. She was charming—from the top of her bright, proud little head to the tips of her well-shod feet, and her voice, in the music of its tones, was as bewitching. as her face. _</p>
        <p>"Mothers-in-law, as an -institution, are a mistake," she was saying, when her mother interrupted. "You forget, Peggy," she pleaded, "I shall be Basil's mother-in-law." "You're the one exception that just proves the rule," Peggy avowed, and, having descended and kissed her mother, she sat on the table once more. , "Basil is a man—a real live man," she said, emphatically, "and his worst fault isn't as big as my littlest one, even if he is quiet and absent-minded, and—" "Ugly," Elizabeth put in, without raising her eyes. "Ugly, then," Peggy said, as she might have granted an extra virtue; "but that doesn't excuse the fact that his mother's fussy old molly-coddle." " Peggy! " the mother remonstrated, feebly. "Yesterday," Peggy said, savagely, "she took me out for a ilrive specially to tell me that I must always be careful to see that his flannel shirts were well aired—indecent old thing!. I told her very .politely that I didn't even know he wore flannel shirts, and that if at his age he hadn't enough sense to look after his own shirts it was hopeless for me to try and teach him," Oh! Peggy, you shouldn't have done that," her mother said, gently. "Then, last night at the theatre," Peggy -vent on, "my new evening shoes pinched so horribly that I asked Basil to take them off for me, and when he did his mother glared at pie as if I'd committed an unpardonable sin. She's simply too ridiculous for words." "But she's very„ kind and generous," Mrs. Meredith urged ; and I really do think she's very fond of you, Peggy.'? • "She is kind," Peggy said, reluctantly; "And when I remember all her generosities I</p>
        <p>feel as if I ought to nail myself cin a tree and get someone to pelt stones at me for even thinking badly of her. But there, nothing will alter the fact that she's a molly-coddle, and however I'm going to manage her always I don't know.. She actually asked me the other day if I were a good cook." What did you say " the mother asked, amusedly, with vivid memories pf Peggy'q gallant attempts at cookery, for the future edification of Basil and the present martyr, dom of her own family. "I said," Peggy replied, "that he asked me to be his wife and not his cook, and I also said that I thought I knew the marriage pervice pretty well; but I'd read it up, and if there was any hint of cookery among my vows i'd change my mind and be married in a registry-office; and she was so shocked that she couldn't even laugh. She told me that men with wonderful brains like Basil's had to have carefully-prepared food, and when I asked her about my wonderful brain she didn't answer! " Peggy's brother had sauntered into the room, and, resting his back against the mantelpiece' he spoke, "You're jolly clever at firing off your smart little elnibs at people, Peg," he said, "but you've a tongue like a needle, and you'll give someone a pretty severe wound one of these days." And Peggy, who had a remarkable respect for her stalwart brother's opinions, changed her tone. "But it's only Mrs. Hastinls, Jim," she said. " I don't care if other people are ridi- culous—it doesn't concern me, you see." " I get on all right with her," Jim said. "But she isn't going to be your mother-in- law, and that makes all the difference in the world! " Peggy argued. " Granted, Jim continued, coolly, "that she is a molly-coddle, you have to recognise the fact that you are madly in love with her only son—" " He fell in love with me first," Peggy began, but Jim went on mercilessly. "There was not more than six hours be- tween your mutual knock-ovcr,' " he said; " and for two people who pride themselves on their common sense that's pretty quick going. I should say. You can't deny the fact—or, you did, you know, I wouldn't believe you— that there isn't a single thing in the whok wide world Basil could ask you that you'd refuse." " In reason," Peggy began again. "Or out of reason," Jim asserted. " If h( were to say to you, Peggy, we're going tc live at the North Pole after we're married,' you'd say—" "I'd say," Peggy said, firmly, My good man, we're not married, and if that's where you're going to live we never shall be! And Peggy swung her feet with studied in- difference. " Now, that's just what you wouldn't do," her brother said, knocking the ashes out of his pipe. "You'd probably look at him for about three minut.s. and then you'd co to the door and say, 'Very well, Basil, 1 can be ready in—three hours. Does one go by boat or by toiq? Xou @tea I I‘l3OW the two OS</p>
        <p>ion, and yam' you spout all that sharlov nonsense about your 'theories' there's one person in the world who counts it so much smoke. Anything to say? " he asked. "Only," Peggy declared, with a disdainful toss of her curly head, "that for a grown man you talk the most arrant rubbish!" And then there came an unmistakable ring at the bell, and, after a hurried survey of herself in a mirror, she ran towards the drawing-room. "Don't you think," Mrs. Meredith asked, pleadingly, "that you're a little hard—a little severe—towards Peggy?" "Not for a moment,' Jim said, cheerfully. "She lets fly at people so frankly herself that she deserves the same treatment now and again; and, for another thing, she's too sensible not to know that all I said was true."</p>
        <p>He relit his pipe, and buried himself in a newspaper. After reading for half an hour he looked at his watch. "Peg's been away a long time," he observed. "It's strange she didn't bring Basil in here as usual." Then, almost as he spoke, the front door banged and steps came slowly towards the morning-room. Peggy's every movement was so sprightly and full of demon that the eyes of all there turned towards her as she entered the room. Her face was white and dull-looking, its expression startled, as that of one who is re- covering from a shock. She stood for a moment at the door without speaking; then her words came with difficulty. "The most dreadful thing has happened," she faltered, taking no notice of the chair Jim proffered. " Basil's mother received a cable this morn- ing to say that there has been an explosion in the mine in which all her money is invested, i and of which her only brother s manager. Her brother has been very :seriously injured, and the list of the wounded is enormous. Mrs. Hastings wished Basil to go out at, once to the mine and—he—is—going—to-morrow," _ .</p>
        <p>she concluded, wretchedly. -"it-will take him three weeks to reach the place. lam going round to hi q mother, and I've promised not to leave her till he corccs back." And; with no further explanation, she went slowly to her own room.</p>
        <p>"By Jove! poor little Peg," Jim said, and he followed her. "I say, Peggums " he called at the door of her room, "may I come in?" And being answered in the affirmative, he entered.</p>
        <p>Peggy was standing at the open French window, her face and attitude the very em- bodiment of despair. Her brother walked quietly over to her. "Nothing's ever so bad as it looks at first, old girlie," he said, in the tone he only used when they were alone, and she, turning, buried her miserable face against the arm he put round her... " But this looks so horribly black," she shuddered.</p>
        <p>"All the same," Jim said, cheerily, "it might have been much worse, you know. For instance—Basil—"</p>
        <p>"Oh, don't! " She shuddered again, then she raised terrified eyes to him. If it had been Basil I should have gone mad," she whispered. "Well, then," went on the strong voice, "you see how much worse things might have been. You don't even know that Mrs.</p>
        <p>Hastings' brother is clone for yet, and, any- how, I don't see that you'll help anyone by looking like a funeral. Why don't you pre- tend things are going to be all right? Thought. that was one of your ` theories,' " he said, brightly. She threw back her shoulders with a characteristic little jerk. Her smile was a pathetic attempt. "So it is, and you're an old dear," she said; then she mopped her eyes impatiently. " D'you think anyone would, know I'd been crying?" she asked somewhat shamefacedly. "No !" Jim asserted. "You'd better give your cheeks a bit of a rub up, though, and do something to your hair. What do you want me to do—come round as far as Burke-street with you?" "Oh," said Peggy, gratefully, "if you only would ! "</p>
        <p>" Right 0! I'll wait for you downstairs, then," Jim answered, leaving the room. Peggy walked to the dressing-table, and as she brushed her unruly locks into something like neatness she talked aloud to herself, as she often did when excited. "Now, that's going to be the last of it," she decided. Fancy sitting down and howling, as if that'd do any good." She ran the hairpins into her hair as if they had been daggers, and she her own enemy, put on her most becom- ing hat and daintiest gloves, and ran down- stairs to her brother. I expect I look as if my smile is screwed on," she said to herself, I "but I'm determined to see this through I without any whining on my part. It's no use thinking about to-morrow,' and then, because her lower lip would tremble, she bit it bravely. "I'm ready, Jim," she said, quietly; and the two set out without speaking. At the Hastings' house a scene of the utmost confusion reigned—the luxuriously- furnished hall looked like a packing-room, so full of travelling trunks and passenger paraphernalia was it—chairs, lounges, and every available piece of furniture was loaded with shirts, socks, books, rifles, coats, and pipes hugging each other in helpless muddle. The Irish housemaid let the girl in—Jim promising to call later—and for the moment ' Peggy stood on the great Persian carpet sur- veying her surroundings. The sight gave her an inclination to laugh and cry at the 'same moment, so significant was its pathos. She laughed helplessly. hatever is all this for, Norah? " she asked. Tho girl shrugged her great shoulders. f` Sure, miss, 'Os only the mishtress knows, I soospect ; fer meself, Oim surprised she isn't lendin' half 'er own wardrobe to Mishter Basil, so mixed up is she, crying all day and thinkin' 'er bhoy's goin' to lie drownded at least." "Oh, that's rubbish! " Peggy said, with flinch vigour. "Where is Mrs. Hastings?" Just then that lady entered, her eyes swollen with prying, her dainty little cap awry, and her arms filled with clothes that certainly had, ....ever fitted Basil since he left school. Peggy took the collection from her, kissed her unhappy face, then, unceremoniously pushing a dress coat, a couple of pairs of pyjamas-, and a pair of gum boots on to the floor, put her future mother-in-law in an easy- chair. "Now, my dear," she said, with gentle decision, "just you sit here and superin- tend, and I'll work. First of all, let's have some tea or some wine, Norah." And the housemaid departed. "Ohs, my dear," whined the little woman, pathetically, "it's too dreadful—it really is!" Then she broke off. "I'm putting in all Basil's socks—" "So I should have thought!" reflerted Peggy, sotto voce. "And borrowed froni all the neighbours as well to get this accumula-</p>
        <p>tion !" . "One never knows what discomfort men have to endure out in those terrible places," Mrs. Hastings went on. " And °these pyja- mas "—she picked up some gaudy remini- scences of boarding-school time. "Aren't they rather small?" Peggy asked, cautiously. "Yes, dear, yes," the distressed voice went on. "But one never knows. I think he should have all his warm things with him." She rose with a sudden remembrance. "Why, dear me, there were some flannel shirts I made him two years ago, and put away in some drawer. I must go and get them." She rose, and sud- denly flung her arms round the girl's neck. " Sweet little Peggy," she said, sobbingly, "it is so splendid to have you And the suddenness of the unwonted burst of affection so astounded Peggy that tears rained down her face, and she clung to Basil's mother in an agony of grief for a moment. Then she recollected herself. " This will nerer do," she said, with a miserable attempt at brightness. " See, dear, you go and get what you want, and North and I will start packing." And, still sobbing, Mrs. Hastings went. No sooner had she reached the door than Peggy rang the bell violently, and on Norah's appearance, in answer to the summons, sze pointed breathlessly to the largest trunk. "Quick, Norah ! ' she commanded, taking one handle. " Let's get half of these absurd thinas nut. of the way Mr. Basil would only have them in me sea it we did pack them." " Roight you air, miss," Norah acquiesced, cheerfully. " A quarter of this noomber's enough fer wo bhoys bike Masther Basil, but ttAiq't ug tclla temisthrgzs )91."</p>
        <p>The appearance of a pile of flannel shirt; surmounted by a hat-box in the doorway, and followed by Mrs. Hastings, was the signal for Peggy and the Irish woman to put down the third truhk they had prepared to et,rry off, and Peggy immediately fell to packing wit'.l much seeming energy. In reality, her young heart was aching bitterly, and she hated every moment that kept her from Basil, who had gone to try and get the latest news of the disaster. The list of things Mrs. Hastings considered absolutely necessary increased every moment, and it took the girl as much time unpacking perfectly useless traps as it (lid to put in ordi- nary clothing. She was hot and weary, and utterly de- pressed, when the door opened and her lover entered the hall. He did not see her at first bending over a great cabin trunk, but he sud- denly heard a most untomboyish and wholly pathetic attempt at a whistle, and in two strides he was across the hall and had gathered the little vacker into his arms. " Oh, boy—boy!' was all she could find to say, but her arms were round his neck, her curly head was pressed passionately against his face.</p>
        <p>She. held him off from .Tier, and her sad eyes tried to smile into his bravely as she took in, as if to photograph it on her memory for ever, every fe:Ature of that dearly-loved face. "Oh, my clear "—she shivered—" how do women let their lovers go to war? I can't—l can't let you go from me." Basil was smoothing her shiny hair with his big gentle hand, and when he spoke tt last it was very slo•xly, as if dreading the answer. H e could not keep a certain joyous tone of expectancy out of his voice, however, and Peggy looked into his eyes with a sudden hope at her heart. It fell at his first words.</p>
        <p>"I have to go, little love," he said. "But another wire has come through to say that my uncle's accident is not really very serious, except that he has to lay up indefinitely." " Indefinitely!" Peggy said, breathlessly. Then ,Basil went on "quickly, 60 quickly that perhaps it accounted for the catch in the voice that Peggy loved so "Look, dearest, I don't know what you're going to say to me, but old Jim and I laid our heads together, and-" He stopped, unable to .go .on. • _ _</p>
        <p>Peggy slowly took his arm from round her shoulder and stood off. Her eyes were wide with ,astonishment. _ _</p>
        <p>"What have you done, boy?" she de. manded, hardly. above a whisper.</p>
        <p>Basil's face, half doubting half triumphant, made her heart leap madly in her breast. What was he about to tell her? Slowly he unbuttoned his coat and took out his pocket-book, feeling her burning eyes On him all the time. Then he handed her two long printed sheets of paper and stood watch- ing her intently as she took them in. His face had gone suddenly white, and his hands, deep in his pockets; were twitching nervously. Bewildered, Peggy looked over the paper, covered with dates and figures. "What, is it?" she asked, piteously; and Basil laid a finger on the top lines where names were written. "This is what I wart you to look at, little girl," he said, softly. And Peggy read on one "Mr. Basil Hast- ings, passenger for Johannesburg"; on the other "Mrs. Basil Hastings, passenger for Johannesburg." Her head swam, a mist was before her eyes, but in her excitement one hand closed over Basil's arm. " When? " she whispered. " When?" "Next week—Friday week," Basil faltered. "Oh! Peggy, my little, little queen—say you'll do it! I know it seems sudden; but it isn't, really. You see, it would only have been a couple of months off, anyhow. And think of the trip, think of the dais we'll have to- gether, and think of how it would be with us if we had to be parted!" Once more his strong arm was round her shoulder, and her face was pressed against his cheek. "Speak, dear one. I don't know what you are going to say. Does it seem dreadful to you?" " No—no—no—but----" Peggy began, then, lest he should see the rosy pink that flushed her cheeks, her ears, even her neck, she buried her face once more. ,</p>
        <p>"1 will go, boy—of course, of course I will," she stammered, and could say no more. And then, to his eternal shame be it said, Jim parted the hall curtains and said, he&amp;rt.- lessly, to Peggy: "Well, if it's the North Pole, does one go by train or by boat?" [THE END.]</p>
        <p>ACID STOMACHS ARE DANGEROUS.</p>
        <p>NINE-TENTHS OF ALL sromAca TROUBLE SAID TO BE DUE TO ACIDITY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>PHYSICIAN'S ADVICE ON CAUSE</head>
        <p>A PHYSICIAN'S ADVICE ON CAUSE</p>
        <p>AND CURE.</p>
        <p>A famous physician whose successful researches into the cause and cure of stomach and intestinal diseases have earned for him an international reputation, said in the course of a recent lecture that nearly all ii.testinal troubles, as well as many diseases of the vital organs, we e directly traceable to a deranged condition cf the stomach, which in turn was dni nine' times out of ten to excessive acidity, which not only iriitated and inflamed the delicate lining of the stomach, but also set up gastritis and stomaeh ulcers It is interesting to note that he condemns the use ( f patent medicines as well as of medical treatment for the stomach, stating that he and his colleagues ha' e secured rem-rkable results by the use of ordinaiy biAirated magnesia, which, by neutralisiug the acidity of the fo d, removes the source of the trouble. He ct)ttflds that it is as fool.sh to treat the stomach itself as it would be for a man who stepped on a tack to rub liniment on the foot without first removing the tack. Remove the tack and the foot will heal itself—neutralise the acid and stomach troubles will disappear. Iriitating medicines and medical treat- ments are useless, so long as the contents of the stomach remain acid ; remove the acidity, and there ' will be no need for medicine the Awned lining of the stomach will then heal itself Sufferers from acidity should get a small bottle of bisurated magnesia from their chemist, and take half a teaspoonful in a quarter of a glass of hot or cold water after meals, repeating in fifteen minutes, if necessary, this being the dose which the doctor has found most efficacious in all cases.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>WORKING CLASSES AND CONSCRIPTION.</head>
        <p>WORKING CLASSES AND CONSCRIPTION.</p>
        <p>Lab9ur M.P.'s Warning.</p>
        <p>Mr. Stephen Walsh, M.P., speaking at Abram, in the Ince Division, on Sgurday night, said that if a Tory Government came into power at the next election, the women of this land might depend upon it that con- scription would follow, acd that their children would be compelled to enlist in the Army for a greater or lesser number of years. Their sons, the very flawr of the nation, would be taken away from them at a time when they were most useful and entwined themselves most deeply in the t ffectis n of mothers, sisters, and wives, and they would be taken away not to fight the battle of the workers, but to fight the battle of me possessing classes. (Applause.) The working people of this country had no quarrel with the working people of any other country, and the wcrking people of other nations, be they German or French, bad no quarrel with the working people of England. (Hear. hear.) loternatisnal quarrels were not fomented by the working classes of any nation, but by those who, whether in this country r on the Continent, lived upon the blood and the labour of the workers... (Applause.) The workers ought to resist by every possible means in their power any attempt, or any tendency even, to force conscription upon the British people. (Hear, hear.) Although Tariff "Reform," cr at least taxes en food, had been for the time being set aside, they were being constantly told that thosa bad not been abandoned, and as soon as a Tory Government got into power they might depend upon it that food taxes would again become a stern reality. It would seem that in the very near future the pol'cy of the Tory party would be conscription for the children of the poor and taxes upon the f, od of the working classes. (Hear, hear.) What a splendid prospect and what a magLificent invitation to the people of the country to return a Tory Government to power</p>
      </div>
