      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The German R )yal Wedding.—The wedding of Prins° 1 Ernest of Cumberland and Princess Victoria of Potosi!' which is to take place at Potsdam on May 21,.1), will be , somewhat less of a grand public function than niir.: f be expected from the fact that the bride-elect ii..; Kaiser's only deuehter. Among the guests wily; many of Quern Victoria's relatives incluJing Kl" George and Qieen Mary, whose coming his be-11 eeloonsei by the German Press- as being particularly, gratifsing to the Pieople and the Imperial family lb° visit of the King and Queen to &amp;Alin will, of courlo9 t e of a strictly private and family character, but nevsr- theless it could not have bsen arranged had there bsee asything like a strained situation between the twee co sat ries over politicsl and naval rivalries. Tao for' ()ming happy event which effects a reconciliatle°, bitween the Heuse of Hohenzollern and the House II .never, alss increases the ties of kinship between 01: Royal Family and that of Germany, as Pi ince Eraesti: a nephew of Qteen Alexandra and first cousin of George. The New Vi -countess Wolseley.—Nresoun Ns! Wolteley inherits much of the organizing genius of be:, father, the late Lord Wolseley, and for the past esTe.,; or eight years she has carried on a school for !flee' gardeners at Raggedlands wheie she lives at Glyn&amp; T. Su sex. There is accommodation for about a doze' lady gardeners, a staff of inseruotors, and about Weil acres of land, for carrying on the work of the scbo.4 Visceunteas Wolseley, who is heiress of her fill- title, was born in 1872 and is unmarried. She loves t'l country and outdoor occupations, and has always "5 objects in life other than the mere frivolous diversioes of society. With ideals of her own regsrding work the r n'y true source of happiness, she has been le,r't successful in the training of pupils with a teste 1° gardening. of ' Tea Shop Weitresses.—Of the many varied:4 a work.ng girls, the waitresses edieloyel in the tea 011°.Y1 are perhaps best known to the general public re although long hours and low wages are their l'ot' Welt are for the mrst part happy and contented in theln busin-ss surroundings. They can just Bowe, &amp;lon% ?, the*r earnings which at the neat do not exceed ge,„ week, and en the ave-age are not much more then !'"d te les but Vier° is always the posalCillty of -0136 et wieh some Prit ce Charming, among the onstoisere, ala Inatri. that compensates for all. They regard their buggies? lite as merely temporary and it is as a sort of msnial agercy that the tea shop is such en ettractiss (Idling to the workingolass girl, and even to note girls of gentle birth. Various attempts have hee, fest made to organise tea shop waitresses with a trili: '- to %Ilion, but that road to economic titivation appeal° have no attraction for them. For some time WO tbahee Women's Industrial Council has been inquiring ieW lir pay and conditions of their employment and the Oh% remedy they can suggest is that it should be b:0011 within the scope of the Trades Boards Act. Pulicewomen.—The case recently in which ,: constable in the exercise of his duty, successfulyi disguised himself as a girl is an evidence in favour ...ivi the appointment of policewomen. At first this prell°P was received with ridicule, but the experiment has teend tied in Germany, Denmark, the United States te Canada, and it has been considered by the Loire, County Council. It has keen suggested that prq'ciii women, in addition to strict duty would be especiarts, useful in looking alter the railway stations, ri'aed picture palaces, dancing establishments, etc., ;bey keeping an eye on 3 oung and wayward girls. S" .c• ceutd not wear hobble skirts, but might have ap attr_%o tive uniform of a long navy Wee coat with 81,11:18 buttons, blue felt hat., and black gloves. r ''Shtitachens and Male Critica.—Women rule touch importance to maselly re Feminine nin not e a criticism of-their dress. Medical men especially 004 much given to regarding women's dress as a faslureeB„o. it is Dot surprising to find Sir John Cockburn involsee, ing against wearing a veil as being ugly and unhealtl7,l It is only one of women's many unhygienic habitsre which he regards the wearing of night attire 45 telt but be will not convince tbe feminice world that e.'-istf are an evil. When the first freshness of youth is a eell chosen veil adds attractiveness to a prette St it by the touch of mystery it imparts, and the idea tbsoct is insanitary is not to to taken seriously. It is la: es due to the tendency to follow the masculine easht, more closely at health. than ever this Spring than to any (Ve3tl°°,l le The Watch of Yesterday and To-day.—Fashione na jewellery are almost as inconstant as in dres3,ll4.f changes are very marked in the matter of latter-071; watches and the manner of wea•ing them. Our 1116,35; mothers valued their watches primarily for their util,l,,tie their ornamentation was of secondary importaces •' of reverse is the rule to-day. Attached to a long chalooo gold, more or less weighty and ornate, in accorte a ith the mesn of the male belongings whose pri/l tbe it was to present fiances, wives or daughters with ad, coveted possessions, the recipients of these often 'Oro, some presents when wearing the watches hid there o.Pory fully away from prying eyes in pockets aesclote designed to keep them from thieves and pickPo° jet) Now the watch is primarily an ornamsnt, est' lee bracelet and exposed to publics vie v on the arm of,o_ree out of every tea women who desire to ba consWerio fashionable. That it should be a reliable timekeeroyo a matter of secondary importance. Nor is it " of worn on the arm. It has recently taken the elltv'test buckles on shoes but that is an American notice popular in England. a 0° Other Devices.—Then there is the muff watch be 13,;'.401 a cord with the muff. This is usually in a leather A-b et which serves also as a receptacle for stamps. ADyes 0 variety is the watch that is so small it can be wortliod pendant or brooch. This ornament is often esti i 0 between two aoverigns or two half sovereigns—noo, on that account called the sovereign watch—Dhoti-001 also undergone radical changes and the convelweliel circle is now retained almost exclesively for Mast ef wear. The tiny trifles for the fair sex shoelreie fashionable jewellers have any shape but thei°ol some are Oval, others square, elliptical an,`"; eolllt, octagcnal. All are thin and slender, to match 1' eee seem the silhouette which fashion still decree! toil prevail where the human figure is concerned. P not gold is the favoured metal from which the vrt`tbil tb°o to-day is fashioned, and this beautiful triufilph °is o ' ra jeweller's art adorned with the rarest gems its seit ieo, narrow flexible band of platinum. A pretty Snu arrq,l, less expensive idea is to carry the watch on a band, or wristlet, of ribbon to match the VwnvoLleri which it. is worn. This is affected chiefly Ve'ellerd gitls, who are still supposed to wear lese Je" • than the elders.</p>
