        <p>/4;</p>
        <p>.„cel fire extinguishers be purchased from Messrs Jul n Ken and Co., at a cost of Z)Os each, less 10 per cent discount. The question of electric lighting was deferred until the next meeting, The Superintendent's report snowed that there had been nine cases of sickness amongst the children. Six, however, had recovered. lie asked the Board to consider the question of allowing him further assistance in the management of the Horne. Votes of thanks ware accorded to Mr Bellamy and Mrs Whitley for their presents to the home ; also to the Runcorn and Widnes Co-operative Society for their loan of chairs at the opening of the Home. On the proposition of the Chairman, seconded by Mr Wood, the minutes were confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>VOTES OF THANKS.</head>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>Mr Morris proposed a vote of t-12;as to Mr Davies, a retiring mernoer. He knew Mr Davies was a most useful member of the Board, and on many occasions he had acted with due dignity as chairman of the House Committee. He had served the Board faithfully and we:l in many directions during the three years he Lai been a member.</p>
        <p>Mr Antwis secc,,nded and the Chairman supported. Mr Davies in reply thanked the members for their e7:pressions of appreciation of his services which he had rendered willingly. It had been a pleasure to him and the kindness which had been extended to him had been a pleasant incentive to work. (Applause.) Mr Woodward said it was incumbent upon them to move a vote of thanks to the Chairman who had pre- sided over nearly all their meetings in the last three years. He had occupied the chair with dignity and efficiency. Mr Lioaker in seconding said Mr Spruce could look back with pleasure on the fact that &amp;frill', his term ot office the much-vexed question of the children had been settled. It was his (tne chairman's) great desire that the children should be settled in a new home, and that desire had been realised. Mr Hallows supported and Mr Spruce having replied proposed a vote of thanks to Mr Linaker, jun., their able vice-chairman. His forthcoming term of ofnce as chairman would be msrkea by effic,iencyand ability. (Hear, hear.) Mr Davies seconded and said that on the House and Relief Sup9rvisiou Committees Mr Linaker had rendered valuable help. Mr Linaker briefly replied and remarked that it was only on four oc3ssions that he had to occupy the chairman in the absence of Mr Spruce.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Council WftS held at Dutton on Tuesday, Mr C E Linaker, presiding.</p>
        <p>M-ANLEY WATERWORKS!</p>
        <p>.• • The Loctl Government wrote sanctioning the pur- chase of the Manley vv,iterworks. The Board observed that although the works were described as being for the township of Manley, the township of Kingavvood was also supplied and they were directed to state the cost should apparently be apportioned between the two contributory places. Tao Cleric was instructed to write the Local Govern- ment Board a fuller explauttion of the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>EIGHT ACHE FOOTPATH.</head>
        <p>EIGHT ACHE FOOTPATH.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that at a meeting of the rate- payers of Daresbury, it was resolved that the dangerous condition of the fo,,tpath in the Eight Acre, leading from Daresbury to Moore, ba brought before the notice of the Council. It was decicied to leave the matter with the Parish Council.</p>
        <p>HEALTH OF' THE DISTRICT. MEDICAL OFFICER'S ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr Adams presented his annual report as medics officer of health for the district. In it he states : The grass total of 3C6 deaths in your district includes eight deaths occurring in persons helcnging to the Runcorn Urban District, and which occurred in the Union Workhouse Hospital, at Dutton ; these eight deaths must therefore he deducted from your total, as not belonging to your district. When births and deaths for 1912 are comparal with the averages of the preceding five years, it will be cb e ,ed th t tie- re is a decrease or about 59 birthr,witti a decrease of 33 deaths Tire total number of cases of infectious diseases notified during 1912 WAS 90, which, with the exception of 1900, is the smallest number that has occurred in any year since 1590, a span of 22 years. Coincident with this very great decrease of infectious diseases has heed the completion and opening of the new large ward at the Isolation Hospital, Dutton, during 1912 increasing the accommodation from 12 to 24 beds, thus bringing your Hospital accommodatioa up to the full rrquirements of the Local Government Board, of one beu per LOCO of population. It is allowed us to hope that it may never be called upon to have all its beds occupied at one time, and this icerease of beds is the best means of keeping down t'le number of cases In regard to the pollution of rivers and streams in the district the dcctor says he may assert that the to till amount of pollution of streams in the district is quite trivial when compared with the aggregate pollution of rivers and streams from communities situated on, or contributing pollution to, these waters before they reach the district.</p>
        <p>'file Clerk said that with regard to scarlet fever they were in a better position than ever. Dr Adirns said thit was so, as there had been no deaths from the disease for two years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>VOTES OF THANKS.</head>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>Mr Woolward proposed a hearty vote of thanks to their genial chairman. He had don' all they had expa3ted him to do, to their pleasure and gratification. In the position of chairman they required a dignified man who fully roalised the responsibilities of the office he held. Ihey had such a man ia Mr Linaker for he had fully maintained the dignity of the office. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Mr Wright seconded and Messrs Pilling, Antwiss, and Davies supported. Mr Linaker, in reply, spoke of the pleasure it bad given him to preside over such an important body. Public work had always had a fascination for him and he was proudof the fact that he had been chairman of such an important body. The medical officer's report was strong proof that they were doing all they could in an 'economical way to carry out the orders they received. The state of health in the district could not be any better and that was a fact to be proud of. On the chairman's proposition a vote of thanks was accorded the vice-chairman, Mr Antwiss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Employment for the Blind. ,</head>
        <p>Employment for the Blind. ,</p>
        <p>A Bill to provide for the technical education, the employment and maintenance of the blind was the subject for discussion at a special meeting of the Warrington Branch of the National League of the Blind, held at lirick-street. A good number of blind people and friends interested in the League were present. The Secretary (Mr T II Shaw) said that owing to the recent formation of the branch very few of its members were well versed in the different clauses which the Bill contains. The Bill was read over and explained and a lengthy discussion followed on Clause No 2 which reads as follows :—" The minimum wage to a female adult worker shall be 1:Is per week and the minimum wage to a male adult worker shill be determined by reference to the rate of pay paid by the County Councils and Borough Councils in their respective areas to unskilled workers capable of seeing." Mr Mellor said that he was greatly opposed to this particular clause. In his opinion it was better to ask for a little at first and when they had achieved that, the minimum wsge question could be pushed forward. He should propose a monthly grant of an amount to be determined by the local authority but not less than a sum equivalent to a weekly payment of 55. in respect of each capable blind person. Every local authority, he proposed, should also make to every blind person who through infirmity or incapacity, is unable to learn or to support himself, a monthly grant for his main- tenance of an amount to be determined by the local autbority,but being not less than equivalent to a weekly payment of 10s., nor more than a sum to be fixed by a Secretary of State in respect of each such blind person. A number of other members also expressed their opinion on the subject, a large majority being in favour of the amendment. The next meeting will be held on Tuesday. April 29th, when new members and friends will be cordially welcomed.</p>
        <p>, 7- . _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>M!n</head>
        <p>M!n</p>
        <p>, : ' • -.... ..... '.... : - .0 ',..,. - • - ....., , • 4..0' Le..i iki, I k ' , ;-,,..... , 1 ' - -1, , ',711, -._ ' . i :-...,....„, 1.... ; , ICT 4(' ; :...wir-:':- , V</p>
        <p>LYIII3I.</p>
        <p>TIIE GUARDIANS' ELECTION.</p>
        <p>The election of two representatives from the Lvmm Parish to the Bucklow B,ard of Guardians passed off on Saturday -.vithout any untoward incidents. Though there was some little evidence, other than the usual conveyal cos and committee rooms on which were carcs bearing such election advice as " Vote for--", or, " Plump for--", that a contest was in progress, yet th interest in the event was shown by tee jact toot there was a heavier poll than even was the case of the poll on the much debated Lyratu Parliamentary Bill. With the exception of a short period about noon voting was very slow uatil attcr six o'clock when Messrs Lewis (Booth's Hill Ward), Jennitg (Heatley , If Qui° (Lymru), and W Mullard (Statham) as presiding officers, together with their clerks had .quite a taisy tlme. The rotiring members were Messrs S Davies and S W Gould, who in seeking re-election were opposed by Mr T F W Dixon Soon after the Close of the poll at eight o'clock the ballot boxes were brought to toe Council Chamber for the counting of the votes. In due time Mr Mulford, as Mr George Leigh's deputy returning officer, an:lot:DC:ea the result as :—Mr T F W Dixon, 504 ;Mr S W Gould, 493 Mr S Davies, 420 ; and declared Mr T P W Dixon and Mr S W Gould as duly elected Guardians fur the Parish of Lymm. Mr S W Gould in proposing a vote of thanks to Mr _Milliard as returning 4.-15.cer, said that they always felt safe in his hands as he was most fair and um/dr-sad in all his dealings.—This ws seconded by Mr T F W Dixon, and supported by Mr S Davies.—fn respondirg Mr Muilard</p>
        <p>said that while he appreciated their vote he thought that their thanks were more due to Mr Leigh and his officers. Mr Milliard then went downstairs to the front of the Council Chamber where he declared the result, amidst much cheering, to the large crowd that had assembled.—Mr T F W Dixon, in a few words expressed his thanks for being placed at the head of the poll, and promised to do his hest for the electors of not only the Booth's Hill and Statham Wards, hut for the Lymin and Heatley Wards as well. He also thanked his workers.—Mr S W Gould and Mr Davies also responded. MISSION SERVICE3.—The series of Mission Services at the Lymm Primitive Methodist Church conducted by Mrs Johnson-Wright last week have been continued with success during the present week. Open-air meetings have on several occasions preceded the usual service, whilst on Friday of last we,-,k the meeting was held in Brookfield Rcom, Booth's Hill. Last night Mrs Wright spoke of the causes that led to her becoming an evangelist and told of her experiences in that capacity</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>THE AMALGAMATION QUESTION. VIEWS OF DISTRICT COUNCIL CHAIRMAN. Responding to a vote of thanks at the meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council, on Tuesday, the Chairman (Mr C E Linaker), referred to the proposed amalgamation of St )ckton Health with Warrington. He said he was glad to see there were some who were sensible enough to debate the question and consider all the phases fairly and squarely. If Sto.ck ton Heath did go over to Warregton they would soon regret the step. That Cauncil had always endeavoured to carry out the wishes of Stockton Heath in the way of improv- ing the highways, &amp;c, and he did not think they could say the Rural District Council had not done its duty. The two representatives from Stockton Heath would find a vast difference. At Dutton the meetings were condnoted in quite an orderly manner but the scenes at the Warringtnn Town Council meetings were quite the reverse. (Hear, hear). STOCI:TON HEATH BAPTIST CHURCH.—A tuc,-.essful tea and social organized by the men of the congregation on behalf of the new organ fund toots place in the evening. The concert was presided over by the Rev Ge.)rge Curtis (the pastor). Messrs E Leigh and Isaac Wl:kinson were the soloists and received hearty encores. Messrs C Furness and John Derbyshire delighted the audience with their humorous recitations. Mr W +,viikinson presided at the piano. Light refreshments %lore served during the evening. Much amusement was caused by a button sewing competition for gentleman. ST. THOMAS MEN'S CLASS (B.)-0a Sunday after- noon, a send-off service was given to Messrs Richard isherwood, Oliver and Percy Williams, who are leavine for Canada. There was a good attendance. The Rev IV Bracecatnp, on behalf of the teacher and chtss, presented each of them with a Bible, and in doing so earnestly I asked those who were going away and those who were staying at home to carry out three instructions—to remearoer their Clod, their prayers, and their Bible. 'ose going abroad might be tempted by new scenes and new interests. They might he handicapped or dis- heartened by being far distant from a place of worship. , but they must persevere in spite of difficulties, and, at any cost, put God first. With regard to the Bible, the se-called Higher Criticism had made more materialists than Christians. He preferred to take the Bible as it was because in it there was all the advice and assist- ance neccessare for this life. In conclusion he asked teem never to forget their home letters, but system- atically to keep in coramunication with relatives and friends in the old country.—The recipients replied,after wh ch Mr Rose said that separations were, of necessity, somewhat depressing, but they must remember that wherever they were, they were still the same community, working with the same objects in view. It was the tradition of Britishers to lead clean lives and to he true to their country and their God; and he could offer them no tbetter advice. After a few parting words from the teacher (Mr Hallows) Mr l'etley, Mr Baguley and Mr Morris thanked Mr Bracecamp for his attendance, and wished the three brothers every success in the new sphere of life. The singing of" God be with you" brought is very impressive service to a close. ELECTION OF PARISH COUNCILLORS.</p>
        <p>The disclosures with regard to the tire bell comedy and the attitude of Stockton Heath in regard to the amalgamation genstion with Warrington focussed an unusual degree of interest on the triennial election of members of the Parish Council which took place on Monday. The chief issues cf the contest have been the amalgamation question, the lighting of the district and street improvements. There have been no election meetings the candidates having con- tented themselves with canvassing the electors Considerable comment has been occasioned by the issue of the following circular which has been rather bitterly resented by candidates not included in the list :—" If the electors desire good government and true progress they will do well to vote for the follow- ing candidates :—Brocklehurst, Henry ; Clarke, J H ; Hallows, John ; McDermid,John; Mclntyre, W; Monks, Joseph ; Pearson, F ; Rose, F ; Warburton, Frank ; Westbrook, H 3 Westbrook, T R ; Whitley, J." These were termed the select 12.</p>
        <p>The poll was opened at 12 o'clock in the Church Room, Victoria-square,and closed at eight. Mr George F Ashton (clerk to the Runcorn Rural District Council) was the returning officer, and Mr E Bradshaw (clerk to t'Ao Stockton Heath Parish Council), deputy returning officer. _</p>
        <p>The complete •list of the candidates is appendel : James Baguley, 6, Grice-street, blacksmith ; Adam Bennett, S, Cawdor-street, niculder ; Arthur Bottomley, Whitecroft, Whitetteld-road, superin- tendent registrar ; Hy Brooklehurst, Rose Bank, Grappenhall-road, commercial traveller ; Joseph Houghton Clarke, 27, Grappenhal I-road, company director ; John William Crossley, 39, London-road watchmaker and jeweller; Thomas Deakin, 59, Cawdor-street, bank manager; Charles Dukes, 26, Dundonald-avenue, trade union secretary ; Charles Hallows, 32, London-road, sub-postmaster and stationer ; John Hallows, Lilac Bank, Grappenhall road, gentleman ; Joseph Jones, 134, London- roal, commercial traveller ; John McDermid, 38, Fair- field-r( ad, foundry stockbrcker, William Mclntyre, Red- Lion Inn, Lotioon-road, licensed victualler ; Thomas Millar, 29, Chapel-lane, blacksmith ; Joseph Monks, 18, Whitetield-road, director of steamship company; Frederick Pearson, Grappenhall-road, cashier; Frederic Rose Brookleigh, Grappenhall-road, schoolmaster; Frank Warburton,Walton-road, builder and contractor; Herbert James Westbrook, -45,Whitetleld-road,solicitor ; Thomas Boyle Westbrook, Victoria-square, cashier ; James Whitley, 2, Chapel-lane, grocer ; John Richard Wood, -17. London-road, music dealer. From 830 a large crowd of people assembled outside the Institute but it was not until 11 o'clock that Mr Bradshaw declared the result of the poll, The following gentle- men were elected. * denotes retiring member. *I? Pearson John Hallows 350 *H J Westbrook *James Whitley *William Mclntyre H Clarke *F Warburton A Bottomley C Dukes *J McDermid *F' Bose *Charles Hallows 250</p>
        <p>The unsuccessful candidates were : J Baguley, 78; H Brocklehurst, 213; A Bennett. 215 ; .1 W Crossley, 178; T Deakin. 156; J Jones, 110; T Millar, 140; J Nionks, 188; T R Westbrook, 209; and J R Wood*, 204</p>
        <p>PETTY SESSIONS. TUESDAY.—Before Messrs A H Talbot (in the chair). Roger C Parr, J Hepherd, W Fletcher and H G</p>
        <p>Roberts.</p>
        <p>SHOP HOURS ACT PROSECUTTON.—The first local case under the Shop Hours Act was heard when Alice Keuwright, Roman-road, was summoned for a breach of the measure. Mr Roger Bind, an inspector under the Act, explained that the County Council had fixed Thursday as the day for closing in the afternoon. On the date in question he visited the defendant's shop and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>IF YOU HAVE ANY</head>
        <p>IF YOU HAVE ANY</p>
        <p>DIFFICULTY IN</p>
        <p>Reading or Sewing</p>
        <p>CONSULT</p>
