        <p>GA itNIFIKV:4 A t' 1.40 Ni!:tt PRICES than it is</p>
        <p>possible for them to he procured at from</p>
        <p>saw a number of customers. His assistant purchased some tea, which was not one of the exempted goods she was allowed to sell. ,efendant said she was not aware that she was doing wrong, as she closed on Monday afternoons, but Mr Hind said Thursday afternoon was fixed, owing to the majority of the tradesmen sending in a written request for that day. The Chairman said the Bench wished it to be known that shopkeeper, must close on the day selected.—Defendant was ordered to pay the costs. CRUELTY TO A DOG.—Joseph Spann, farm labourer, Norton, was fined lOs and costs for illtreating a dog in Walton-read, Stokton Heath, on March 14th.—Phree lady witnesses spoke to seeing the defendant riding a bicycle and dragging the animal along the road. When a policeman saw the animal it was exhausted.—The Bench thanked the witnesses for bringing the case before the notice af the Inspector of the R S.P.C.A. WESLEYAN CHOIR CONCERT.—A grand concert was given by the above choir on Thursday evening in the Wesleyan School in aid of the New Organ Fund. The chair was taken by Mr .1 Taylor, L.D.S. and the Choir acquitted themselves with credit under the conductor- ship of Mr F E Penketh. Miss May Isherwood slng very sweetly in the solos that fell to her lot. Madam Lily Fairhurst sang the " Beautiful land of Nod," for whicn she was given an encore. She also sang the old song "The flight of ages " with much taste and expression, her voice being heard to advantage in a song of this description. Mr Richard Tranter, baritone, Sc Helens (the winner of the Blackpool Festival 1909 and 1910) sang " The Bandolero," and the manner he worked up to the climax of declaring " I am the bandolero ' was a treat. For the encore he sang the "The old side car "and would have received another encore if time had allowed. Dr F H Gandhi, violinist, played" Thais' Meditation" (Massenet), " Romance " (Gabriel Fituse), and" Le Cygne," with taste and skill. trlr H M Ashton was entrusted with the humorous element and right well he filled the bill. The choir opened with the glee "Strike the lyre," which was capitally rendered and shoe ed the careful training that they had received. "0 happy eyes" (Elgar), was sung very creditably and the ct. oir showed a thorough appreciation of the words. The rendering of "By Babylon's Wave," was full of colour and the beautiful pianissimos in the opening passages were very neatly done. The male glee "0 peaceful night" was very well sung, end chorus, "Happy and light" from Bohemian Girl was very daintily given and provided a nice change from the glee. " The long day closes " was one of the best achievements of the choir for some considerable time. Mrs F Pendlebury and Mr J White were the accompanists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>NEW CONGREGATIONAL MINISTER.</head>
        <p>NEW CONGREGATIONAL MINISTER.</p>
        <p>As announced in our issue of March 29th, the Lymm Congregationalists have obtAined the services of the Rev Arthur Penaliegon as their pew pastor. He should have commenced his pastorate at Lymm last Sunday, but was unfortunately cmtined to his houce with an attack of intluenz,4, from which, we are glad to say, he iq recovering. Mr Penaliggon comes to the Lymm Church at a very opportune stage in its history, the Jubilee anniversary services being celebrated this v‘ eek- end.</p>
        <p>The Rev Arthur Penaliggon is a native of Nesvquay, Cornwall. After receiving a sound education at Newquay and St Austell College, he entered upon a commercial career where he eventually rose to the holding of important positions as accountant and cushier in one of the leading Banks. He was early influenced by the aggressive and evangelical Noncon- formity of his native County and believing that his true vocation was to be found in the ministry, he entered upon a course of theological training, after which, in 1909, he accepted the charge of St Columb, Cornwall. Alter a successful though short pastorate he accepted the unanimous call to Bncklow Hill, where his enthusiastic efforts have resulted in raising the church membership nearly fifty per cent. The Bucalow Hill Sunday school has also been re-organised, and a Men's Institute built at a cost of over £3OO, which at present has a membership of over nicety, and is serving a very useful purpose. Nit. Penaliggon is keenly interested in social reform and all movements for the advancement of true and worthy ideals of manhood and brotherhood.</p>
        <p>HORTICULTURE.—On Friday the 4th mat, Mr R G 1 James of the Holmes Chapel Horticultural and Agricul- tural College brought a series of interesting and instructive lectures to a close by dealing exhaustively on the subject of Chrysauthemums. Not the least interesting part of the meetings has been the innumer- able questions and answers bearing on the experiences of leLturer and audience. The only disappointing feature has been the very meagre attendance. RECITAL AT BROOM EDGE.—Owing to the generosity of Mr and Mrs H Champ the inhabitants of Broom Edge had an opportunity of spending a very pleasant evening last Wednesday in the Primitive Methodist Church when Mr James Bernard presented a miscellaneous recital covering stories, scenes, end sketches of life and character. There ,:as a splendid attendance, and the funds of the church must have benefited in a very substantial manner. Whilst humour predominated, " The Going of the White Swan," a story of Labrador, end " Our Pardner " gave the reciter fell scone to display his ability to move his audience by pathos. In his "Sanderson and on talk matters over "Mr Bernard was at his best, and the moral of the scene was portrayed in au unmistakable manner. " The Enchanted Shirt," and " A Nippy Tongue" caused much merriment. Other ite- a were " The Patriot " (Robert Browning), and a scene of comedy from Shakespeare, " Dogherry and Verges instruct the ‘k atchmen." Miss Hind contributed two whistling solos, and Mr J H Brazendak gave two songs. The duties of aceompinist were carried out by Mr F Champ.</p>
