      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>RISLEY.</head>
        <p>RISLEY.</p>
        <p>INDEPENDENT METHODIST CHURCH, FE ARNHEA O.— Open Sunday was observed in the school in connection with the above place of worship on Sunday last, a good number of people being present, Mr Thomas Harrison, jun., occupied the chair. Mr H Bradburn contributed a helpful address, on the subject of " Character." Various songs, duets, were given by the juveniles who did their part splendidly, the after- noon being thoroughly enjoyed by everybody.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>FIRE.—An alarming fire took place on Saturday morning shortly before nine o'clock at the works of Messrs Tomlinson and Hayward, who carry on business as chemical manufacturers, close to Fiddler's Ferry Station. The alarm was given by one of the workmen who perceived smoke issuing from the sulphur shed. Speedy efforts were made to summon the Warrington Fire Brigade and had it not been for the extremely smart work of the firemen much more serious damage would have been done and much credit is due to the Warrington Brigade under the direction of the Deputy-Superintendent, for the remarkably short time that elapsed from the time the fire bell rang to getting playing on the flames. The works being situated close to the canal side, the supply of water was adequate. Fortunately the fire had not extended beyond the sulphur sheds when the brigade arrived, and the firemen succeeded in confining the flames to that particular part of the works where a quantity of sulphur and weed killers was stored. The firm were able to continue their business on Monday as usual. The fumes from the sulphur sheds were a great difficulty to the firemen at times. The brigade were able to return home about noon on Saturday. The damage, which is estimated at between £5OO and LOGO, is covered by insurance. PARISH COUNCIL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>The village of Penketh was the scene of a great deal of excitement on Monday, espezially towards the evening, when voting for Parish Councillors took place at. the Council schools (where also the voting for District Councillor was proceeding.) There were fourteen candidates for the seven seats. Ail the old councillors retained their positions on the Council. The following was the result : Mr T Routledge, 193 ; Dr Murray, 176 ; Messrs .1 G Withinshaw, 167 ; W Davies, 145 ; J Woodward, 145 ; J Ditchfield, 143; Miss Tinsley, 141 ; Messrs W Woods, 126 ; G Faulkner, 119 : C Wozeacroft, 102 ; H Hunter, 68 ; J Midwood, 61 : H Pownall, 48 ; W W Shaw, 26. The first seven were declared elected by the returning officer (Mr Shearer). Mr Faulkner proposed a vote of thanks to the return- ing officer (Mr Shearer) for the satisfactory way he had carried out. the duties.—Mr Garnett seconaed, and said he was pleased the election bad been carried on Rich such a good spirit on both sides.</p>
        <p>"What the suburbs of London and other cities will have to show on their outskirts in 50 or 60 years to come is too dreadful to contemplate," aid Dr F G Vesey, archdeacon of Huntingdon, in his annual charge denouncing the practice of jerry-building.</p>
        <p>GRAPPENHA LL.</p>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL ELECI lONS.</p>
        <p>Much interest was taken in the Parish Councl elections at Grappenhall on Monday. There were 19 candidates for the 10 seats, and a keen canvass had been made of the ratepayers of the parish, who turned up in good numbers to the poll. The polling booth was in the old parish room, and Mr ‘Varburton (registrar of births) was the returning officer. Shortly before ten o'clock the result was declared as follows :—Messrs A Lister-Kaye, 143 ; F A Frost, 138 ; J Bradford,l32; F M Appleton, 130 ; A Earwaker, 121 ; It W IVilde, 116 ; W Winstaoley, 114 ; W A Wooding, 97; E Allen, 93 ; W Lomas, 86 ; C F Read, 36; G A Leach, 64 ; F L Garside, 63 ; F Fox, 62 ; N Cook, sen., 59 ; J Carter, 52 ; W H Hartley, 50; F Ellison, 49 ; F Burrows, 33. The first ten were elected, lots being drawn between Messrs W Lomas and C F Read, and Mr Lomas was successful.</p>
        <p>The only members of the old Council re-elected are Messrs Lister-Kaye, J Bradford, and \V Lomas. A vote of thanks was accorded to the Returning Officer, on the proposition of Mr A Lister-Kaye, seconded by Mr G A Leach.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS GUARDIANS' ELECTION. In connection with the triennial election of the Prescot Board of Guardians, four contests took place on Monday, in St. Helens. In Central Ward, a lady, Mrs E Jukes, was one of the candidates, this being the first time that any lady has contested a guaroians election in the town. The poll resulted in the return of _all the old members Details :</p>
        <p>Central Ward—*T Baxter, --290.; Mrs Jukes, 190 ; J Hewitt, 44. North Eccieston—*T Eyres, 299 ; J II Dickson, 287; W Johnson, 123. Hardshaw—*F Crawther, 306 ; W Bromilow, 293 ; M E Darby, 41. East Sutton—*C Walsh, 513 ; W Taylor, 405 ; J B Langford, 52.</p>
        <p>THE ROYAL VISIT TO ST. HELENS. SPECIAL TREAT FOR SCHOOL CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>In connection with the visit of their Majesties, the King and Queen, to St. Helens on Tuesday, July Bth, there is to be a slight deviation in the route so as to enable the school children to see their Majesties with- out any risk of being crushed by the crowd. After leaving Messrs Pilkinijon's Cowley Hill works their Majesties will turn to the left into Cowley Hill- lane, hut instead of going straight along Cowley Hill- lane, they will enter Victoria Park by the gateway nearly opposite City Gardens, and drive along the lower road nearly parallel to Cowley Hill-lane, and go ont of the park by the gateway at the lodge in Cowley Hill- lane and thence alung Bishop-road as already arranged. The gateway, which is at present only wide enough for foot passengers, will be widened so as to admit of the passage of the cars.</p>
        <p>The children will be arranged on the grass on both sides of the lower road, and the park will be closed tn the general public for the necessary time.</p>
      </div>
