      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <p>REV. A. J. BRATT AND CHRISTIAN SCIENCE. Sir,—ln your i-sue of April sth, theta is a report of the farewell sermon delivered by the Rev A. J Bratt, in which he is cited as speaking of Christian Science as "unchristian." It seems a pity that the reverend gentleman should have used this auspicious occasion for decrying other people's religion. The Christian Scientist has no time for abusing anyone else's religion. His own mission in life is constructive and not destructive, and he considers this to be a more, Christian attitude than .that of the critics who term his religion "unchristian." It would be interesting to know what Mr Bratt's idea of a Christian is? how- ever, we have the standard of a Christian set by cur Saviour (and He undoubtedly knew what a Christian was) in the words: "Go ye into all the world, preach the gospel, heal the sick, cleanse the lepers, raise the dead.' Christian Scientists are always will- ing to be judged by this standard, and although they are not accomplishing all that they would wish to along these lines, they generally find that their critics get no further than the first commandment. "Preach the gospel," if indeed abusing someone else's religion can be termed preaching the gospel. After the first injunction (ur critics generally aescend to the level of inanimate drugs to do the rest for them, thus confessing their inability to demonstrate Christianiry as our Saviour understood it. Mr Brett is quite [correct in stating that men and women are turning to Christian science for great spiritual ttuths, and they arc undoubtedly finding them there. The growth of the Christian Science movement is the wonder of the age, and to-day its healing and -saving gospel is being preached all ovsr the world with "signs following." In conclusion,while wishing the reverend gentleman gS4VcAS-1. in hit new field of lalxur, may I give him a -vrc,rd of lotrnirhg. "Refrain from thasa Ecen and let them alone, for if this counsel be of men, it will be overthrown, but if it is of God, ye will not be able to overthrow them lest haply ye be fcund even to be fighting againet God."—Yours obediently, JOHN W. DOORLEY, Manchester, April Sth.</p>
        <p>THE CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY AND POVERTY. Sir,—ln your issue of March 29th you published a letter signed "Labor Omnia Vincit," which the writer complained had been forwarded to the local editor of the Co-operative Wheatsheal and tbat it was not even acknowledged and also remarked that courtesy and good manners are.ever so cheap. It should be hardly necessary to point out that no editor of a newspaper would undertake to acknow- ledge the receipt of letters other than through the columns of the paper if they were not published. If he did so a staff of clerks would be necessary to do the work. Your correspondent was in a trifle too much of a hurry to lush into print with his complaint as the letter was received too late for one monthly issue and was therefore also too late to be acknowledged as having been received and has been printed in the issue which should have been to band on April Ist., but owing to the holidays has only arrived this morning, and the letter was actually in type nearly a fortnight before the complaint appeared in your paper. Regarding the other matters raised as to the con- duct of the business meeting of the society, may I submit that the public press is not the place to dis- cuse the chairman's ruling. As a general principle the Co-operative movement has held itself aloof from party politics of any and every kind, and to our mind at least the chairman was perfectly justified in ruling out of order an attempt to discuss a matter which according to your own correspondent is certainly, to /say the least, identified distinctly with Trades Unions and Socialist organisations, especially so as there had been ample opportunity for the forwarding of a resolution that could have been put on the agenda of the meeting if it had been considered part of the duties of a distinctly lintinnas meeting to discuss it. Whether y( ur correspondent agrees with this or not, we trust that at least he will be able now to see that his complaint as to want of manners has been absolutely unfair and unjustified.—Yours truly, EDITOR OF THE LOCAL WHEATSHEAF.</p>
        <p>April 4th 1913. Sir, lam sorry such a letter as that from Labor Omnia Vincit, should be necessary, seeing that inside the Co-operative movement we have a periodical, in which we ought to be able to air our views, discuss, or criticise, any, or every phase of the movement fi eely or to suggest advance or change. This freedom being denied both in cur organ,and the members' meeting, I am glad L.O.V. has gone outside the movement, for the purpose of letting the members) know, what is going on, and how our so called demo- cratic organisation is conducted. L O.V. has sent his letter for insertion in the " heatAeaf," and it has not appeared though our local Editor has repeatedly asked the members, to write on any subject of interest. L. 0. V. refers to the last quarterly meeting, and also to myself as the mover of the resolution, which read as follows That we as a society take an active part along with other co-operative societies in the present aOation, for legislation next session, re the abolition of poverty. I had, prior to the meeting carefully examined the rules, to see if I should be in order, and could find nothing to put me out of order if fairly administered. True, in the Standing Orders it says : "The decision of the Chairman on any pcint of order shall be final." What I wanted to know tuen and still want to know is— the point of order on which he gave his decision. I had previously written the committee asking that that this question be taken up. and we come into line with other progressive societies. The committee would not move on their own, so I wanted the members to move in the matter, hence the resolution. —Thank- ing you in anticipation,</p>
        <p>W. LOWE.</p>
        <p>29, Orford-avenue.</p>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church. The Rev W Reid addressed the children in the morn- ing on "habits and their influence on the health of the body," and advised boys and girls to make confidants of their parents, especially concerning those things of which they were in doubt. In the evening he spoke from the words originally used in connection with a scene of treachery and blood- shed "Art thou in health " ? The preacher stated that no one had a greater right to ask the question than the disciple of Jesus Christ. True christianity is not con- fined to soul or spirit but stands for the betterment of man. To say that religion makes all of the next and nothing of this life is to say what is not correct or even possible. He pointed out the responsibility laid upon all of us to fight against sins of corruption and of omission, against prejudice, Interference, irregularity, all of which and not God, in thousi.nds of ',saes lard upon humanity and its offspring, unnecessary pain and suffering and death. He argued against the forms of amusement that create the inordinate desire for excite- ment, and injure the body through the mind, and pleaded for, especially among members of Christ's Church, the clean mind and tongue. For all poisoning of the body through evil temper, and impoverishment of health self-created, God will hold us responsible and juige us not by what we are but by what we might have bets'.</p>
        <p>THE LAWN TENNIS SEASON. A fortnight hence the lawn-tennis season opens, and in preparation therefor innumerable enthusiasts all over the country are busy overhauling their rackets and club paraphernalia. This is the time when last season's racket is usually found to have "sprung," or to he suffering from a broken string or two. Repair may be possible, but in most cases the only real cure will be a new racket. We have had experience of rackets by many makers, but have found no goods quite the equal of those produced by Messrs F la Ayres Limited the noted sports manufacturer of 111, Aldersgate- street, _London E.C. In addition to heing makers of the famous " Ayres " ball they are specialists in racket-building and can always be relied upon to introduce the Wee. improvements. This season all Ayres rackets of " Champion " quality are treated with a special preparation which renders the gut practically impervious to the effects of changing climatic conditions. Tests in various quarters of the globe have given most satisfactory results and the new/ treatment should greatly prolong the life of a racket. Two of the firm's leading specialities are The Wilding and the S.N.D. rackets. We have tried both and can confidently recommend them. Our last stood two seasons of hard usage, and is still dead true and "tight." Our new one is a delight to both eye and band. These are both high-class' high-priced rackets, but they are worth the money lb the long run. Messis Ayres make innumerable varieties at prices to suit everyone, and the best thing local players can do is to send for an Ayres' catalogue. They are sure to find what they require, either for personal or club use, and all goods sold by the firm baTe the practical guarantee of the name, F H Ayres.</p>
        <p>, . I 11 Lctters ea, 111.fil f to the , „EDITOR._ 141</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>MEMORATION.</head>
        <p>MEMORATION.</p>
        <p>Photos Parkinson. MR. C. D. LAW MISS GLADYS ADAMS (" Shylock.") (" Portia.") Warrington's annual Shakespeare Commemoration will take place at the Royal Court Theatre on the four evenings commencing on Wednesday next. The plays selected for presentation are " The Merchant of Venice" on Wednesday and Thursday and " Macbeth " on Friday and Saturday. We understand that the bookings of reserved seats are exceptionally good and the commemoration promises to maintain in every respect the high standard set by the two previous enterprises for which our local amateurs have been responsible.</p>
        <p>not be vouched for by the health officials. More- over, their finely-mixed condition and the added condiments prevented the public judging for them- selves as to the soundness of the food, either from its appearance, taste, or smell. When there was any outbreak of food-poisoning, it could generally be traced to such articles as these; and be would urge, therefore, that until greater controlling powers were given to the health authority, both as regarded the premises on which articles of food were prepared and the precautions to be taken in their preparation and storage, it was far safer for the public to keep to dishes prepared and cooked at home from materials which could be more easily proved by one's ordinary senses to be sound and wholesome.</p>
