      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>MEDICAL OFFICER'S SUGGESTIONS. HEALTH POINTS FOR WARRINGTON. Smoke Not so iia-c-1( as Painted.</head>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S SUGGESTIONS. HEALTH POINTS FOR WARRINGTON. Smoke Not so iia-c-1( as Painted.</p>
        <p>"Some essential points in the health work of the borough and the need for the general public co-operating therein" was the subject of a lecture given IV the medical officer of health (Dr. J. Coote Hibbert) at the Town Hall on Thursday • evening, in connection with the local observance of Health Week. There was a fair attendance, including Aldermen Beni-Al 411 d Burton, Conn- cillOrs Hawthorn and Skit*, the Rev. W. Brace- camp, and Mr. W. G. Rowland '(secretary of the Guild of Help). The Mayor, who presided, said it was almost incredible that-they should have to force upon the attention of the public the necessity for taking measures to promotesor maintain their own health which, next to life itself, Was undoubtedly the greatest gift of Providence. Referring to the special lessons on hygiene, which had been given in the schools, as part of the Health Week pro- gramme, the Mayor said that morning he spent nearly an hour in Heathside school. He went through every class and, after hearing the char- acter of the lessons given, he could not speak too highly of it. From the lowest .to the highest class, the lessons were adapted for the special con- ditions, and were such as could be understood by every scholar, all of whom seemed to take a lively interest in what they were being taught. Allud- ing to the official visit to alum areas, the Mayor said there was great cause for encouragement in the conditions that obtained in those districts as compared with last year. On the former visit it was quite a rare occurrence to see a clean, tidy, well-kept house. Now the houses that were well kept were almost equal in number to those that were not. It might be said that a great many people were expecting the visit and had. therefore made preparations, but efen if that were so, it was encouraging, because it showed that they were capable of being roused, whereas formerly they were apparently in such a state of apathy that nothing on earth would incite them to keep their houses clean. THE MAYOR AND CHEAP BATHS. His worship alluded to the recent discussion in the Town Council with regard to the provision of cheap baths for workingmen. He maintained that it was open to question as to whether they were taking the right course in that matter, or whether the desired results could not be obtained in smile other way by helping people to keep themselves clean in their own homes. He knew that it was quite possible, even without baths, to keep oneself clean. With a rough towel and plenty of clean water a person could very easily sponge himself over after a day's work, and it would refresh him and make. him enjoy his evening's recreation so much better. His worship also suggested the .provision of movable or collapsible baths in those homes where the usual baths had not been pro- vided. He questioned whether the Water Com- mittee would like it, but it would be money well spent, even if the town authorities expended some- thing on providing extra water and movable baths, rather than in having the public baths used indiscriminately by people who might not be adapted for that. privilege. Dr. Hibbert said the main hygienic necessities of a town like Warrington were as follows : It must have a pure and sufficient water and food supply; it must be efficiently cleansed and scav- enged, and all refuse and waste products must be expeditiously removed and properly disposed of; it must be well paved and drained; the hous- ing accommodation must be such that there was no overcrowding, and each house must. be pro- vided with adequate ventilation, lighting,, and protection from dampness; the air must be kept free from harmful contamination; infectious disease must be efficiently dealt with. The doctor made special reference to the importance of milk as a food, andi said they should .welcome all legislation which in any way tended to the PURITY OE THE MILK CLUPPLY. That they had need to wait so long for legislation bearing on the question of tuberculous milk was one of those puzzles which it was difficult for the ordinary sanitarian to unravel, but perchance the better-instructed party politician could give the solution. Retail milk shops were very numer- ous in Warrington, and frequently the conditions were such that it was impossible for the milk to be efficiently protected from all contamination. That danger would not be eliminated until the retailing of milk was confined to certain licensed premises, which were specially adapted for the purpose. Whe doctor emphasised the necessity for proper storage of milk and cleanliness in the consumers' homes. The Health Committee of Warrington, he continued, had gone further than the majority of other towns in getting special legislation on the subject of the milk supply, but there was still much to be done. If the general public could only be shown how important a food milk is, they would demand that all necessary Kecautions should be taken to ensure its purity, and the Governmeut of the day would be com- pelled to find time to give the protection that adequate legislation could alone offer. Meantime ho would repeat the advice, ."Boil all your milk." WARNING AGAINST PREPARED FOODS. Food, generally, as far as this borough was con- cerned, might be said, on the whole, to be sound and of good quality. So long as the public bought definite articles of food which they prepared and cooked for themselves, they might feel reasonably confident of obtaining it in a sound and wholesonie condition. Unfortunately, however, there was a considerable demand for prepared dishes, such as sausage, potted meats, brawn, etc., and the pre- paration of those foods wash not under the control of the Health Department. 'They were not always prepared under satisfactory conditions,. and the soundness and quality of the ingredients could</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>SAVING THE CHILDREN</head>
        <p>SAVING THE CHILDREN</p>
        <p>Dealing with the sanitary conditions of the town, Dr. Hibbert called attention to the fall in 'the infantile mortality rate from 144 in 1879 to 92 in 1912. He thought they were justified in ascribing the improvement to the gradual advance in the general sanitation of the borough, and also to the progressive enlightenmeht of the people in health matters. At the same time, they were still open to serious attack under certain climatic con- ditions, as witness the infantile death-rate of 147 during the hot summer of 1911. In this con- nection Dr. Hibbert emphasised the necessity for strict cleanliness in the 'homes and the sweeping away of undesirable matter from the vicinity of</p>
        <p>THE SMOKE QUESTION.</p>
        <p>There had been a great change in recent years in the housing .conditions of the poorer parts of. Warrington, and the Health Committee could show a long list of achievements in that respect. With respect to contamination of the air one was on less sure grounds. Smoke and other products of combustion were, of course, the chief contamina- tion in a manufacturing borough like Warrington, but although the indirect ill-effects of a smoke- laden atmosphere were undoubted, it was still very difficult to prove that it had a direct harmful action on the human system. The indirect harm arising from a smoke-laden atmosphere—the clos- ing of windows to keep out the smoky air, its tendency to make the housewife feel -that home cleanliness was impossible, its cutting off or dim- inishing the advantages of sunlight—were evident, but it was more 'difficult to say that the inhaled smoke did any detite harm to the human body. The Public Health Act gave power to deal with black smoke when emitted in such quantities as to be a nuisance, provided it did not arise from ceitain trade procedures, such as smelting of ores and minerals, or the calcining, puddling and roll- ing of iron and other metals, or the conversion of pig iron into wrought iron, so as to obstruct cr interfere with any of such processes. Those ex- empted works casued the greater part of the smoke hi Warrington, and next in order came the domes- tic chimneys, also exempted from legislation. Hence the strong criticisms of the Health Com- mittee on their not dealing more rigorously with the smoke question were evidently unjustified— they could not deal with, it under present condi- tions. The thorough dealing with the smoke question, he was afraid, must in a manufacturing town such as Warrington be left to the progress of science and to the application of new scientific discoveries to manufacturing processes and domestic heating. When it was definitely proved that the melting of ores and manufacture of iron could be satisfactorily carried out without, the production of smoke, then he presumed legislation would definitely impose a penalty on manufac- turers if they continued to pollute the atmosphere unnecessarily, and manufacturers themselves, in their own interest, would be only too glad to adopt an equally efficient process which did not cause the waste of fuel in the shape of smoke. The public would also be willing to welcome gas and electricity in their homes when those agents would stirve the purposes of the more 'dirty coal with equally good results and at no greater cost.</p>
        <p>A WORD TO SUNDAY SCHOOL AUTHORITIES. Dealing, with infectious diseases, Dr. Hibbert said that on account of measles in the elementary' schools, children were at times excluded for cer- tain periods, the object being to try and stop the spreading of infection by preventing children suf- fering from the disease in the very early and in-. definite stage having an opportunity of infecting a large number in the same room. That precau- tion might, however, be to a large extent nullified by the excluded' children flocking into picture- dromes and other places of entertainment. There was, moreover, the Sunday .school, and although some Sunday school authorities assisted the Health Committee . in -attempting to check a measles epidemic by also excluding the children from Sun- day school, there were, unfortunately, many Sun- day schools which either refused in any way to help or only helped in a very half-hearted fashion. He was sorry to have to sa'y that in public, but it was a fact, and one which did not bring credit on the persons .concerned. In connection with tuberculosis, there was especially the greatest need for the general public acting in union' with the health authority. - .All the sanatoria in the world would not stamp out tuberculosis so long as a few persons were forgetful of their obligations to their fellows and allowed their sputum to be scattered about, and thus perpetuated the disease. Dr. Hibbert concluded by again commending the sim- ple but potent remedies, of cleanliness, fresh air and 'sunlight. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>REDUCING THE SMOKE OUTPUT.</head>
        <p>REDUCING THE SMOKE OUTPUT.</p>
        <p>Alderman Burton, in proposing a vote of thanks to Dr. Hibbert, said he was glad to hear his re- marks with regard to smoke. 'Perhaps it was not so injurious to health as some people would have them believe, but he was pleased to say that the works with which he was connected had recently installed some electrical machinery, at a large expense, and had consequently been able to do away with five firing boilers. That proved that the manufacturers of Warrington were making headway in that direction. If they took a walk along Liverpool-road, they would find that a large amount of smoke had been abolished.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett seconded. He said many workingmen's houses were robbed of sunlight by</p>
        <p>the ugly and absurd way in which they were built. He held that the front iind back of a house ought to be equally attractive. With regard to the smoke nuisance, the doctor had practically blessed it with faint blame. Unfortunately, however, the outside public might take it that he favoured smoke and, instead of strengthening the hands of these who were fighting against the scourge, he was unintentionally weakening them. If sunlight was so essential as the doctor said, that in itself was sufficient reason for wishing to drive smoke from ,the atmosphere. The Rev. W. Bracecamp supported, and said it would be much better for the town if they would mitigate the smoke evil. Dr. Hihbert, in responding to the vote, said he agreed that sitiolze was a harmful thing. As the law stood, however, the Health Committee could not do any more than it was at present doing. The Mayor was thanked for presiding, on the proposition of Mr. -Rowland, seconded by Council- lor Hawthorn. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>ROYAL VISIT.</head>
        <p>THE ROYAL VISIT.</p>
        <p>---- GUARDIANS NOT TO BE PRESENTED</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians on Wednesday, the clerk (Mr A Bottomley) reported that he had inter- viewed the Town Clerk with reference to the Board's request that representatives of the Board should be presented to the King and Queen on the occasion of the Royal Visit to Warrington. The Town Clerk told himthat he already had more than the specified number of names and also gave particulars of Lord Derby's suggestion as to the way in which the list should be made up. He (the clerk) wrote Lord Derby and had received the following reply :—Nobody recognises more than I do the work done by the various Guardians throughout the county and I wish it were in my power to arrange for their presentation to their Majesties, but I fear it is quite impossible, time absolutely preventing any such arrangement. I have equally been reluctantly compelled to give up any idea of arranging that chairmen of Ruud District Councils should be presented.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>BLACKBURN PHILANTHROPIC FRIENDLY</head>
        <p>BLACKBURN PHILANTHROPIC FRIENDLY</p>
        <p>COLLECTING SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>We have just received the annual report and balance sheet of the above society, Established in 1839, it has thus, 73 yrnrs of industrial life business to its credit. his old rnd well-managed society is noted for its liberal table of benefits. Especially does this apply to the infantile section, inasmuch as the society pays twice as much in one-half the time as compared with most other offices. When speaking of Industrial life assurance it can he truthfully said of the Bleickburn l'hilautl;ropic that it assuredly gives the highest benefits for the lowest premiums. These liberal benefits, however, are not given to the detriment of the society's funds, inasmuch as the surplus funds in excess of tiahiti ties as disclosed by the last valuation renched no less a sum than £130,837. The above figures are ample proof of tho wonderful stability of the s.,ciety. These figures in themselves are sufficient to warrant a strong recommendation of the society's pros- pectus to the iusuricg public.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>PROGRESS 111 1912.</head>
        <p>PROGRESS 111 1912.</p>
        <p>Thn premium income for 1912 amounts to £125,040 Os I d. This is an increase on the previous year's inco“,e of £5,251 8s GAd ; a very satisfactory increase having regard to the wuroing of the losuranc Aotand to the facts that during the early part of the year there wds a cotton lock-out, and later a coal strike, both of which impoverished the people, and in consequence they were unable to regularly pay their contribution!, and many have had a difficulty in redeeming their former positions. In all caves the Board have been as o.,nsiderate and as liberal as possible. The income rom all sources amounts to £147,912 is 7,1 d, as against £141,214 Cs 10,1c1 ; shev‘log an increase of £6.697 148 9d. '1 he Mortality experience was not quite so heavy a during 1911 ; the number of claims fell:1g 5,817, at a cr-st of £53,645 Os. Gd., as against 6,275 at a cost of £53,318 14s. 8101., shewing a decrease in the number of 453, but an increase in amount of £326 58. 9:1. This decrease iu number, but increase in arnouat of claims, means that the average amount per claim }as been incseased from £8 10s. 0 9d. to £9 48. 531. All claims have been paid in full without any deductions whatever. and the rate evidences the careful selection of lives. The Bwird are able to report a greater increase in funds than on any previous occasions the said increase being £48,978.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>ECONOMICAL MANAGEMENT.</head>
        <p>ECONOMICAL MANAGEMENT.</p>
        <p>Economy, consistent with efficient management, seems to be the watchword of the society. The invest- ments of the society are all of a satisfactory character, and their interest earning capacity is still increasing ; the rate they are now earning being equal to a percent- age of 318 of the total funds, as against 3 67, which it is submitted, ought to give great satisfaction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>NATIONAL INSURANCE ACT.</head>
        <p>THE NATIONAL INSURANCE ACT.</p>
        <p>The year 1912 will ever be remembered by all who were engaged in the business of industrial life assur- ance as one of the most, if not the most strenuous of years. The formation and establishment of a separate section under the provisions of, and in accordance with sect ion 23 (1 of the National Insurance Act, 1911, for the purpose of transacting business under Part I (Health Insurance) of this said Act, put a severe strain on the management and stsffs of the society ; but the Board of Mbnagement are able ro report hearty an:i willing service on tue p.rt of all, and, as a sequence, the estab- lishment of a sepnrue section, consisting of upwards of 50,000 members ; and it is the earnest hope of all that by careful st-lootion, strict supervision, business enterprise, aiid economy in management expenses, it will be found possible to build up a sound and success- ful approved society. Any further information may be obtained from the local superim endent, Mr Joseph Fairhurst, Carlton House Chambers, 82, Saukey-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>IN SLUMDOM.</head>
