      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>MAYORS VISIT TO THE COURTS</head>
        <p>THE MAYORS VISIT TO THE COURTS</p>
        <p>OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>It was under the most depressing condi- tions that the Mayor and a group of leading townsmen made an expedition into slumdom on Thursday afternoon. Rain fell pitilessly, making neighbourhoods which at best appear sombre and dreary, even more cheer- less than is their wont. Notwithstanding the formidable dicouragement, the visitors stuck manfully to their task for an hour and a half, but by that time most were so thoroughly drenched that the pilgrimage was abandoned before the complete itinerary had been followed. The party, however, saw quite sufficient to give them furiously to think. They peered into dismal apologies for homes, where the sun's rays could hardly by any chance penetrate, and saw little children being brought up amid surroundings which breed hopeless indifference—and, one would expect, fell , disease. But the marvel was that most of the people who were questioned declared that they and their families were quite healthy, happy and contented—in only one case was there any indication of rebellion against the cruelty of circumstance. Through squalid courts and back passages the party made their way. They saw blocks of dwellings which had been condemned and, derelict and boarded up, await demolition. Others, just " habitable " in an official sense, have escaped that fate for the time being, and one could not help wondering how long they would serve to house human beings who have to seek shelter at a rent of 2s. to 2s. 6d. a week. Still there were some signs of improvement, both in the environment and habits of the inhabitants of some of these dark quarters. Several courts and passages had been paved, thus remedying one fruitful source of discom- fort and disease, and in some cases modern sanitary conveniences had been introduced. In the great majority of houseslinspected, there was a genuine attempt to secure cleanliness and some semblance of tidiness— in fact, one frequently saw evidences of house-pride where it was least to be expected. At the close of the visit the Mayor declared that he had been impressed i with the improvement n the social habits of the people since the last official visit. The tour began in that labyrinth of courts which lies between Bridge-street and Butter- market-street. Next, a number of the yards and weints off Buttermaiket-street were inspected and the visit concluded in the neighbourhood of James-street and Cockhedge The Mayor was the pioneer of the party and entered many houses, talking with the people and putting in many a word in season —if the inhabitants would only act upon the sound advice proffered. There were some illuminating conversations. One woman told his Worship that her husband earned 5s per day of ten hours as a labourer, and that she had also a son working. The.family numbered five and the home bore every evidence of a diligent housewife's care. Still, it was in a dismal yard and could not by anyistretch of the imagination,be regarded as a comfortable abode. The rent is 2s 8d per week.</p>
        <p>"Can't you afford to pay more than 2s 8d ?" asked his Worship. "No " was the reply ," Taking them the year through, my husband's earnings are not more than lOs. a week." The clean curtains at the windows of many houses occasioned favourable comment and,after contrasting the wretched conditions in some houses with the much brighter appearance of others in the same row, the Mayor remarked : "It shows what can be done. It is the habits of the people rather than the places themselves that count."</p>
        <p>In one house,two sisters—old age pensioners —had been living for four weeks. They had been forced to quit their former abode because it had been condemned, and had removed to a dwelling which the Mayor observed was "very nearly condemned." "It's a grand house, too," was the ironical comment of on of the occupants who seemed to have some sense of the fitness of things. " I shall not stay in it "she added. "It's not fit for a pig to live in !" "How much rent do you pay?" queried the Mayor. "Half-a-crown, and it's not woith a shilling !" was the emphatic response. In the Little Weint, off Buttermarket- street, were houses which, undoubtedly did their tenants credit. So appreciative were the comments passed by the deputation that one alderman expressed fear lest the effect might take the form of an advance in the rents Here were to be seen window boxes, and the people spoke with hopeful anticipation of the days when flowers would brighten the exterior of their dwellings. In the vicinity of Rose Yard, a block of houses has been demolished and the hope was expressed that ultimately it would be found posOble to supply the demand for accom- modation created in this way, by the provision of cheap municipal dwellings. A glance at some ancient and tumble- down cottages drew from the Rev. W. Cooper the terse comment: "One is not always a lover of the antique." "No " said the Mayor, 'Things that are old al e not always beautiful."</p>
        <p>In one very drab and unsavoury street, the party encountered a woman who , laughingly exclaimed It's the healthiest street in England. I've lived here over fifty years and I shan't leave here until they take me up Church-street" She added that she had had seventeen children, that she had always enjoyed good health, and that those of her children who survived were robus t too.</p>
        <p>In two houses ventilation shafts had been introduced but the occupants had gone to great pains to stuff them up—" to keep the cold out." They were waiting until the fine weather came, before putting the ventilators to their legitimate use. The Mayor ventured a mild remonstrance , against this refusal to inhale fresh air. "I would not be as healthy as I am if I had bad air" retorted one woman. "I don't need any doctor or any medicine" she added confidently. At the close of the visitation a vote of thanks was passed to the Mayor for heading the party, which consisted of Aldermen Smethurst and Burton, Councillor Osborne, the Revs. W. Bracecamp, H. H. Corsbie, T. T. James and W Cooper, Drs. J. Coote Hibbert (medical officer of health), and G. W. N. Joseph (assistant medical officer), Mr. W. T. Flood (inspector of nuisances), Mr. W. J. Rowland and Mr. J. Himsworth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
