      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-04-12_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>NATURE HEALING.</head>
        <p>NATURE HEALING.</p>
        <p>It is not true that electric trains and pniting motors and hurrying cycles wreck the repose of life. Th,el rather enrich us, in this respect, that they make tver country accessible to us. You can get from the city to the green lanes of Hertfordshire or the unfrequented field paths of Middlesex in thirty minutes to-day- An Nature welcomes yo a. She is always there; alsvaYe° ready for you ; always at rest ; waiting to cistil her eternal repose upon wearied brain and nerve. And yet there is an outre-ge Nature suffers which seems to be aggravated in mauy places and frorn which it is high time that we delivered her. It 16 often complained of, yet it is still with us—the out- rage of the great display advertisement. These hideous things in cur fields and lanes are an cutrage" too, not oely up nr Nature, but upon everyone rib° travels from the city with confident expectation to seek Nature's healing. Let sr&amp;-A a victim of or modern high pressure pass out of the city bounds to find refreshment in the country, and !he is pursued' by this übiquitous foe. He may ride, or motor, or cycle, or walk; he c innot get away from this plague of advertisements. Be may 101 l get a late Saturday afternoon, or a longer Sundsi afternoon, or he may be fortunate enotifill to get a day off in the middle of the week; 130 may be limited to short journeys never far from home.' or he may travel through a coupe of coutities in weekly worship of Nature; but whenever and where" ever he goes this foe pursues him. He-e, where' is Nature's divine function to restore and heal, along every wayside in sem° gaunt timber structure. may even bear in huge letters the name of our friend's business competitor. The very goods he deals in nol be here presented under another name in rivalry of bi°' own. The very litigant, maybe, against whom he icsl unsuccessful lately. is here staring him in the face offerinr, his warts in the country lames. Alas! og`' friend who came seeking Nature healing is back in 105 office in a moment, back in his store rooms, the Ls° Courts, the St, ck Exchange. A bill on a board is 9 quiet lane has done it. It is all an outrage! A plague on your boards slici posters and enamel!' d ir n ! For pity's sake till': them down. Let us forget them in our country la"ef and quitt village scenes. it is time we formed ; society to boycott every article of commerce erbosa supposed merits are flung in our faces from field 311 tree pn I ancient wall. There is a great commercial company that bss indeed already set a magnificent example in respect. In many ways the Michelin Tyre Co. expressed its natriotism, and not least in the distribil- tion of about 22,000 in aviation prizes. This i 9 " noble service to the nation—but the service this colne. paily is now ret dering to the countryside, and to 03 wanderer in village and fiqld and lane, is greatee far' This company has set itself to prelerve the amenities of Nature. Already during the p:esent year it hsei removed from country scenes nearly a thousand or its; cwn signs. These have doubtless been erected 8` great expense, but the further offer is now made 11 this patriotic Company that on receipt of a postcar. 18 or information from any individual throughout Kingdom, it will remove any particular sign of t% Company's to which objection may be taken. Tbisii.o. a very fine response to that undercurrent of pub'la protest against the degradation of field, lane 013 hedeerovv. to We want a few more of our princes of commerce do likewise, and then the countryside shall have lobi own again, and shall undisturbed pursue the art Nature-healing. What is to take the place of these signs in an adveer; tising sense to the Michelin Tyre Co. The anso_ has been the publication of a Motorist's Guide, ernbrs,7, ing the whole of the United Kingdom and Irelaou which is the only one carried by motorists tredsf. . The third edition of this Guide, i.e., 1913, is Pd. out and can be had in exchange for a remittanos Is. 3d.</p>
