        <p>covered with sand and are cleaned out once a week until the chicks are four weeks old, and twice a week afterwards. FIFTH TO SEVENTH WEEK. When the chicks are four weeks old (i .e., at begin- ing of fifth week after hatching), they are fed as follows until they are seven weeks old : The hours of feeding remain the same, but (1) boiled wheat is given instead of rice; (2) instead of dry chick feed the following is given: 75 per cent. whole wheat, 25 per cent, equal parts cracked maize, millet ard hemp. W4ter and grit as before. When five weeks old, one third of each hatch is transferred to another brooder, as., the 120 chickens are divided into three lots instead of two. Each lot should have a wire run to itself as before. EIGHTH WEEK TO SIXTEENTH WEEK (READY FOR MARKET). When the chicks are seven weeks old they are moved to larger runs (100 yards by 10 yards), and larger houses of the ark type. Food.—The feeding is as follows: lst feed, 7 a.m. (or as early as possible), meat 11 the, biscuit dust, lbs, pollard 11b, total, slbs. This is soaked in I', gallons of boiling water and dried cif with: barley meal, 3lbs. best fine sharps. 61135. Second feed: 1 p.m., the same again. Third feed, 6 p.m, or half- hour before sunset (whichever is earlier) : Whole wheat 85 per cent, cracked maize 15 per cent. QUERIES. A correspondent writes to ask about Kavaris and chicken rearing. He wants to know how soon Kavaris may be given to chickens. I should recommend that it be used at one. week (3,1, especially when the stock biris !have 13,-•en fed en Kavaris that chicks are being reared from. The quantity to tie given is as follows : A dessert spoonful in sufficient soft food for a &amp;zen chicks and pro raf a Increase the quantity as the chicks it,crease in age.</p>
